const DATA = {"modules":[{"id":11,"title":"Managing Support Procedures","lessons":["11A Documentation","11B Professional Communication","11C Operating Systems"],"icon":"fa-clipboard-list","color":"from-blue-500 to-indigo-600"},{"id":12,"title":"Configuring Windows","lessons":["12A Windows User Settings","12B Windows System Settings","12C Applications","12D Cloud Apps"],"icon":"fa-sliders","color":"from-sky-500 to-blue-600"},{"id":13,"title":"Managing Windows","lessons":["13A Management Consoles","13B Command-Line Tools","13C Windows Networking"],"icon":"fa-terminal","color":"from-emerald-500 to-teal-700"},{"id":14,"title":"Supporting Windows","lessons":["14A Network Troubleshooting","14B Remote Access","14C Performance Tools","14D OS Troubleshooting"],"icon":"fa-screwdriver-wrench","color":"from-amber-500 to-orange-600"},{"id":15,"title":"Securing Windows","lessons":["15A Logical Security","15B Windows Security Settings","15C Windows Shares"],"icon":"fa-shield-halved","color":"from-purple-500 to-indigo-700"},{"id":16,"title":"Installing Operating Systems","lessons":["16A Windows Editions","16B OS Installations & Upgrades"],"icon":"fa-hard-drive","color":"from-cyan-500 to-blue-600"},{"id":17,"title":"Supporting Other OS","lessons":["17A Linux Features","17B Package & Network Mgmt","17C macOS Features"],"icon":"fa-brands fa-linux","color":"from-amber-600 to-yellow-500"},{"id":18,"title":"Configuring SOHO Network Security","lessons":["18A Attacks & Threats","18B Wireless Security","18C SOHO Router Security"],"icon":"fa-wifi","color":"from-rose-500 to-red-600"},{"id":19,"title":"Managing Security Settings","lessons":["19A Account Security","19B Workstation Security","19C Browser Security"],"icon":"fa-key","color":"from-purple-600 to-pink-600"},{"id":20,"title":"Supporting Mobile Software","lessons":["20A Mobile OS Security","20B Mobile App Troubleshooting"],"icon":"fa-mobile-screen","color":"from-teal-500 to-emerald-600"},{"id":21,"title":"Using Data Security","lessons":["21A Data Backup & Recovery","21B Data Handling Best Practices","21C Artificial Intelligence"],"icon":"fa-database","color":"from-indigo-600 to-blue-700"},{"id":22,"title":"Implementing Operational Procedures","lessons":["22A Change Management","22B Safety & Environment","22C Scripting Basics"],"icon":"fa-code","color":"from-slate-600 to-slate-800"}],"questions":[{"id":"c2-0001","module":11,"lesson":"11A Documentation","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nEmployees must understand how to use computers and networked services securely and safely and be aware of their responsibilities. To support this, the organization needs to create written policies and procedures to help staff understand and fulfill their tasks and follow best practices:","context":"Employees must understand how to use computers and networked services securely and safely and be aware of their responsibilities. To support this, the organization needs to create written policies and procedures to help staff understand and fulfill their tasks and follow best practices:","options":["Deal Appropriately with Confidential and Private Materials","Standard Operating Procedure","Escalation Levels","Professional Documentation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Standard Operating Procedure: Employees must understand how to use computers and networked services securely and safely and be aware of their responsibilities. To support this, the organization needs to create written policies and procedures to help staff understand and fulfill their tasks and follow best practices:"},{"id":"c2-0002","module":11,"lesson":"11B Professional Communication","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\n• Procedures for custom installation of software packages, such as verifying system requirements, validating download/installation source, confirming license validity, adding the software to change control/monitoring processes, and developing support/training documentation","context":"• Procedures for custom installation of software packages, such as verifying system requirements, validating download/installation source, confirming license validity, adding the software to change control/monitoring processes, and developing support/training documentation","options":["Professional Appearance","Typical examples of SOPs are as follows:","Set Expectations and Timeline","Support Documentation and Knowledge Base Articles"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Typical examples of SOPs are as follows:: • Procedures for custom installation of software packages, such as verifying system requirements, validating download/installation source, confirming license validity, adding the software to change control/monitoring processes, and developing support/training documentation"},{"id":"c2-0003","module":11,"lesson":"11C Operating Systems","question":"What is being described here?\n\nService level agreements (SLAs) define the level of service requirements from an internal department or external, third-party vendor. Examples of services that most likely have an SLA in place include: • Internal departments of the company that are providing resources to one another such as","context":"Service level agreements (SLAs) define the level of service requirements from an internal department or external, third-party vendor. Examples of services that most likely have an SLA in place include: • Internal departments of the company that are providing resources to one another such as","options":["Clear Written Communication","Using Proper Language","Meet Expectations and Timeline","Service Level Agreements"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Service Level Agreements: Service level agreements (SLAs) define the level of service requirements from an internal department or external, third-party vendor. Examples of services that most likely have an SLA in place include: • Internal departments of the company that are providing resources to one another such as"},{"id":"c2-0004","module":11,"lesson":"11A Documentation","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nA ticketing system manages requests, incidents, and problems. Ticketing systems can be used to support both internal end-users and external customers.","context":"A ticketing system manages requests, incidents, and problems. Ticketing systems can be used to support both internal end-users and external customers.","options":["Cultural Sensitivity","Repair and Replace Options","Knowledge Base","Incident Reporting and Ticketing Systems"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Incident Reporting and Ticketing Systems: A ticketing system manages requests, incidents, and problems. Ticketing systems can be used to support both internal end-users and external customers."},{"id":"c2-0005","module":11,"lesson":"11B Professional Communication","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\n1. A user contacts the help desk, perhaps by phone or email, or directly via the ticketing system.","context":"1. A user contacts the help desk, perhaps by phone or email, or directly via the ticketing system.","options":["The general process of ticket management is as follows:","Professional Communications","Follow Up","Policy Documentation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The general process of ticket management is as follows:: 1. A user contacts the help desk, perhaps by phone or email, or directly via the ticketing system."},{"id":"c2-0006","module":11,"lesson":"11C Operating Systems","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nCategories Categories and subcategories group related tickets together. This is useful for assigning tickets to the relevant support section or technician and for reporting and analysis.","context":"Categories Categories and subcategories group related tickets together. This is useful for assigning tickets to the relevant support section or technician and for reporting and analysis.","options":["Active Listening","Professional Support Processes","Categories and Severity","Professional Support Delivery"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Categories and Severity: Categories Categories and subcategories group related tickets together. This is useful for assigning tickets to the relevant support section or technician and for reporting and analysis."},{"id":"c2-0007","module":11,"lesson":"11A Documentation","question":"What is being described here?\n\nCategories and subcategories group related tickets together. This is useful for assigning tickets to the relevant support section or technician and for reporting and analysis.","context":"Categories and subcategories group related tickets together. This is useful for assigning tickets to the relevant support section or technician and for reporting and analysis.","options":["Be On Time","Categories","Clarifying and Questioning Techniques","Professional Documentation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Categories: Categories and subcategories group related tickets together. This is useful for assigning tickets to the relevant support section or technician and for reporting and analysis."},{"id":"c2-0008","module":11,"lesson":"11B Professional Communication","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nAfter opening an incident or problem ticket, the troubleshooting process is applied until the issue is resolved. At each stage, the system must track the ownership of the ticket (who is dealing with it) and its status (what has been done).","context":"After opening an incident or problem ticket, the troubleshooting process is applied until the issue is resolved. At each stage, the system must track the ownership of the ticket (who is dealing with it) and its status (what has been done).","options":["Set Expectations and Timeline","Difficult Situations","Ticket Management","Avoid Distractions"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Ticket Management: After opening an incident or problem ticket, the troubleshooting process is applied until the issue is resolved. At each stage, the system must track the ownership of the ticket (who is dealing with it) and its status (what has been done)."},{"id":"c2-0009","module":11,"lesson":"11C Operating Systems","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nEscalation occurs when an agent cannot resolve the ticket. Some of the many reasons for escalation include: • The incident is related to a problem and requires analysis by senior technicians or by a third-party/warranty support service.","context":"Escalation occurs when an agent cannot resolve the ticket. Some of the many reasons for escalation include: • The incident is related to a problem and requires analysis by senior technicians or by a third-party/warranty support service.","options":["Escalation Levels","Meet Expectations and Timeline","Maintain a Positive Attitude","Deal Appropriately with Confidential and Private Materials"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Escalation Levels: Escalation occurs when an agent cannot resolve the ticket. Some of the many reasons for escalation include: • The incident is related to a problem and requires analysis by senior technicians or by a third-party/warranty support service."},{"id":"c2-0010","module":11,"lesson":"11A Documentation","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nIt is also useful to link an inventory record to appropriate troubleshooting and support sources.","context":"It is also useful to link an inventory record to appropriate troubleshooting and support sources.","options":["Professional Appearance","Dealing with Difficult Customers","Repair and Replace Options","Support Documentation and Knowledge Base Articles"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Support Documentation and Knowledge Base Articles: It is also useful to link an inventory record to appropriate troubleshooting and support sources."},{"id":"c2-0011","module":11,"lesson":"11B Professional Communication","question":"What is being described here?\n\nFree-form text fields allow ticket requesters and agents to add descriptive information. There are normally three fields to reflect the ticket life cycle: • Issue description records the initial request with any detail that could easily be collected at the time.","context":"Free-form text fields allow ticket requesters and agents to add descriptive information. There are normally three fields to reflect the ticket life cycle: • Issue description records the initial request with any detail that could easily be collected at the time.","options":["Identify early signs that a customer is becoming angry","Clear Written Communication","Using Proper Language","Follow Up"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Clear Written Communication: Free-form text fields allow ticket requesters and agents to add descriptive information. There are normally three fields to reflect the ticket life cycle: • Issue description records the initial request with any detail that could easily be collected at the time."},{"id":"c2-0012","module":11,"lesson":"11C Operating Systems","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nA knowledge base is a self-serve central repository for information. IT service organizations will normally house troubleshooting articles that end users can refer to when attempting to self-correct or diagnose an issue. Frequently asked questions (FAQs) may also be provided to","context":"A knowledge base is a self-serve central repository for information. IT service organizations will normally house troubleshooting articles that end users can refer to when attempting to self-correct or diagnose an issue. Frequently asked questions (FAQs) may also be provided to","options":["Knowledge Base","Do Not Post Experiences on Social Media","Cultural Sensitivity","Professional Support Delivery"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Knowledge Base: A knowledge base is a self-serve central repository for information. IT service organizations will normally house troubleshooting articles that end users can refer to when attempting to self-correct or diagnose an issue. Frequently asked questions (FAQs) may also be provided to"},{"id":"c2-0013","module":11,"lesson":"11A Documentation","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nAn acceptable use policy (AUP) sets out what someone is allowed to use a particular service or resource for. Such a policy might be used in different contexts. For example, an AUP could be enforced by a business to govern how employees use equipment and services such as","context":"An acceptable use policy (AUP) sets out what someone is allowed to use a particular service or resource for. Such a policy might be used in different contexts. For example, an AUP could be enforced by a business to govern how employees use equipment and services such as","options":["1.1 Explain common operating system (OS) types and their purposes","Policy Documentation","Professional Communications","Be On Time"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Policy Documentation: An acceptable use policy (AUP) sets out what someone is allowed to use a particular service or resource for. Such a policy might be used in different contexts. For example, an AUP could be enforced by a business to govern how employees use equipment and services such as"},{"id":"c2-0014","module":11,"lesson":"11B Professional Communication","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nFrom the point of first contact, the support process must reassure customers that their inquiry will be handled efficiently. If the customer has already encountered a problem with a product, finding that the support process is also faulty will double their poor impression of your","context":"From the point of first contact, the support process must reassure customers that their inquiry will be handled efficiently. If the customer has already encountered a problem with a product, finding that the support process is also faulty will double their poor impression of your","options":["Professional Support Processes","Standard Operating Procedure","Avoid Distractions","Active Listening"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Professional Support Processes: From the point of first contact, the support process must reassure customers that their inquiry will be handled efficiently. If the customer has already encountered a problem with a product, finding that the support process is also faulty will double their poor impression of your"},{"id":"c2-0015","module":11,"lesson":"11C Operating Systems","question":"What is being described here?\n\nSupport contact information and hours of operation should be well-advertised so that the customer knows exactly how to open a ticket. The service should have proper documentation so the customer knows what to expect regarding supported items, how long incidents may take","context":"Support contact information and hours of operation should be well-advertised so that the customer knows exactly how to open a ticket. The service should have proper documentation so the customer knows what to expect regarding supported items, how long incidents may take","options":["Typical examples of SOPs are as follows:","Deal Appropriately with Confidential and Private Materials","Professional Documentation","Clarifying and Questioning Techniques"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Professional Documentation: Support contact information and hours of operation should be well-advertised so that the customer knows exactly how to open a ticket. The service should have proper documentation so the customer knows what to expect regarding supported items, how long incidents may take"},{"id":"c2-0016","module":11,"lesson":"11A Documentation","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nOn receiving the request (whether it is a call, email, or face-to-face contact), acknowledge the request and set expectations. For example, repeat the request back to the customer, then state the next steps and establish a timeline. For example, \"I have assigned this problem to","context":"On receiving the request (whether it is a call, email, or face-to-face contact), acknowledge the request and set expectations. For example, repeat the request back to the customer, then state the next steps and establish a timeline. For example, \"I have assigned this problem to","options":["Professional Appearance","Service Level Agreements","Set Expectations and Timeline","Difficult Situations"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Set Expectations and Timeline: On receiving the request (whether it is a call, email, or face-to-face contact), acknowledge the request and set expectations. For example, repeat the request back to the customer, then state the next steps and establish a timeline. For example, \"I have assigned this problem to"},{"id":"c2-0017","module":11,"lesson":"11B Professional Communication","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nIf possible, the request should be resolved in one call. If this is not possible, the call should be dealt with as quickly as possible and escalated to a senior support team if a solution cannot be found promptly. What is important is that you drive problem acceptance and resolution, either","context":"If possible, the request should be resolved in one call. If this is not possible, the call should be dealt with as quickly as possible and escalated to a senior support team if a solution cannot be found promptly. What is important is that you drive problem acceptance and resolution, either","options":["Meet Expectations and Timeline","Using Proper Language","Maintain a Positive Attitude","Incident Reporting and Ticketing Systems"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Meet Expectations and Timeline: If possible, the request should be resolved in one call. If this is not possible, the call should be dealt with as quickly as possible and escalated to a senior support team if a solution cannot be found promptly. What is important is that you drive problem acceptance and resolution, either"},{"id":"c2-0018","module":11,"lesson":"11C Operating Systems","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nIf there is a product issue that cannot be solved remotely, you might offer to repair or replace the product: • Repair-—The customer will need clear instructions about how to pack and return the item to a repair center, along with a ticket-tracking number and returned-merchandise authorization","context":"If there is a product issue that cannot be solved remotely, you might offer to repair or replace the product: • Repair-—The customer will need clear instructions about how to pack and return the item to a repair center, along with a ticket-tracking number and returned-merchandise authorization","options":["The general process of ticket management is as follows:","Cultural Sensitivity","Repair and Replace Options","Dealing with Difficult Customers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Repair and Replace Options: If there is a product issue that cannot be solved remotely, you might offer to repair or replace the product: • Repair-—The customer will need clear instructions about how to pack and return the item to a repair center, along with a ticket-tracking number and returned-merchandise authorization"},{"id":"c2-0019","module":11,"lesson":"11A Documentation","question":"What is being described here?\n\nIf you have resolved the ticket and tested that the system is operating normally again, you should give the customer a general indication of what caused the issue and what you did to fix it, along with assurance that the problem is now fixed and unlikely to reoccur. Upon leaving","context":"If you have resolved the ticket and tested that the system is operating normally again, you should give the customer a general indication of what caused the issue and what you did to fix it, along with assurance that the problem is now fixed and unlikely to reoccur. Upon leaving","options":["Follow Up","Professional Communications","Identify early signs that a customer is becoming angry","Categories and Severity"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Follow Up: If you have resolved the ticket and tested that the system is operating normally again, you should give the customer a general indication of what caused the issue and what you did to fix it, along with assurance that the problem is now fixed and unlikely to reoccur. Upon leaving"},{"id":"c2-0020","module":11,"lesson":"11B Professional Communication","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nRespect means that you treat others (and their property) as you would like to be treated.","context":"Respect means that you treat others (and their property) as you would like to be treated.","options":["Active Listening","Do Not Post Experiences on Social Media","Professional Support Delivery","Categories"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Professional Support Delivery: Respect means that you treat others (and their property) as you would like to be treated."},{"id":"c2-0021","module":11,"lesson":"11C Operating Systems","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nEnsure that you are on time for each in-person appointment or contact call. If it becomes obvious that you are not going to be on time, inform the customer as soon as possible. Be accountable for your actions, both before you arrive on site and while on site. This means being","context":"Ensure that you are on time for each in-person appointment or contact call. If it becomes obvious that you are not going to be on time, inform the customer as soon as possible. Be accountable for your actions, both before you arrive on site and while on site. This means being","options":["1.1 Explain common operating system (OS) types and their purposes","Be On Time","Ticket Management","Clarifying and Questioning Techniques"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Be On Time: Ensure that you are on time for each in-person appointment or contact call. If it becomes obvious that you are not going to be on time, inform the customer as soon as possible. Be accountable for your actions, both before you arrive on site and while on site. This means being"},{"id":"c2-0022","module":11,"lesson":"11A Documentation","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nA distraction is anything that interrupts you from the task of resolving the ticket. Other than a genuinely critical incident taking priority, do not allow interruptions when you are working at a customer's site. Do not take calls from colleagues unless they are work-related and urgent.","context":"A distraction is anything that interrupts you from the task of resolving the ticket. Other than a genuinely critical incident taking priority, do not allow interruptions when you are working at a customer's site. Do not take calls from colleagues unless they are work-related and urgent.","options":["Standard Operating Procedure","Difficult Situations","Avoid Distractions","Escalation Levels"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Avoid Distractions: A distraction is anything that interrupts you from the task of resolving the ticket. Other than a genuinely critical incident taking priority, do not allow interruptions when you are working at a customer's site. Do not take calls from colleagues unless they are work-related and urgent."},{"id":"c2-0023","module":11,"lesson":"11B Professional Communication","question":"What is being described here?\n\nYou must also demonstrate respect for the customer's property, including any confidential or private data they might have stored on a PC or smartphone, or printed as a document: • Do not open data files, email applications, contact managers, web pages that are signed into","context":"You must also demonstrate respect for the customer's property, including any confidential or private data they might have stored on a PC or smartphone, or printed as a document: • Do not open data files, email applications, contact managers, web pages that are signed into","options":["Typical examples of SOPs are as follows:","Deal Appropriately with Confidential and Private Materials","Support Documentation and Knowledge Base Articles","Maintain a Positive Attitude"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Deal Appropriately with Confidential and Private Materials: You must also demonstrate respect for the customer's property, including any confidential or private data they might have stored on a PC or smartphone, or printed as a document: • Do not open data files, email applications, contact managers, web pages that are signed into"},{"id":"c2-0024","module":11,"lesson":"11C Operating Systems","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nThere are many things that contribute to the art of presentation. Your appearance and attire, the words you use, and respecting cultural sensitivities are particularly important.","context":"There are many things that contribute to the art of presentation. Your appearance and attire, the words you use, and respecting cultural sensitivities are particularly important.","options":["Dealing with Difficult Customers","Clear Written Communication","Professional Appearance","Service Level Agreements"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Professional Appearance: There are many things that contribute to the art of presentation. Your appearance and attire, the words you use, and respecting cultural sensitivities are particularly important."},{"id":"c2-0025","module":11,"lesson":"11A Documentation","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nWhen you greet someone, you should be conscious of making a good first impression. When you arrive on site, make eye contact, greet the customer, and introduce yourself and your company. When you answer the phone, introduce yourself and your department and offer","context":"When you greet someone, you should be conscious of making a good first impression. When you arrive on site, make eye contact, greet the customer, and introduce yourself and your company. When you answer the phone, introduce yourself and your department and offer","options":["Knowledge Base","Using Proper Language","Identify early signs that a customer is becoming angry","Incident Reporting and Ticketing Systems"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Using Proper Language: When you greet someone, you should be conscious of making a good first impression. When you arrive on site, make eye contact, greet the customer, and introduce yourself and your company. When you answer the phone, introduce yourself and your department and offer"},{"id":"c2-0026","module":11,"lesson":"11B Professional Communication","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nCultural sensitivity means being aware of customs and habits used by other people. It is easy to associate culture simply with national elements, such as the difference between the way Americans and Japanese people greet one another. However, within each nation there are many","context":"Cultural sensitivity means being aware of customs and habits used by other people. It is easy to associate culture simply with national elements, such as the difference between the way Americans and Japanese people greet one another. However, within each nation there are many","options":["The general process of ticket management is as follows:","Cultural Sensitivity","Policy Documentation","Do Not Post Experiences on Social Media"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cultural Sensitivity: Cultural sensitivity means being aware of customs and habits used by other people. It is easy to associate culture simply with national elements, such as the difference between the way Americans and Japanese people greet one another. However, within each nation there are many"},{"id":"c2-0027","module":11,"lesson":"11C Operating Systems","question":"What is being described here?\n\nYou must listen carefully to what is being said to you; it will give you clues to the customer's technical level, enabling you to pace and adapt your replies accordingly.","context":"You must listen carefully to what is being said to you; it will give you clues to the customer's technical level, enabling you to pace and adapt your replies accordingly.","options":["Categories and Severity","Professional Communications","Professional Support Processes","1.1 Explain common operating system (OS) types and their purposes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Professional Communications: You must listen carefully to what is being said to you; it will give you clues to the customer's technical level, enabling you to pace and adapt your replies accordingly."},{"id":"c2-0028","module":11,"lesson":"11A Documentation","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nActive listening is the skill of listening to an individual with your full attention without arguing with, commenting on, or misinterpreting what they have said. With active listening, you make a conscious effort to keep your attention focused on what the other person is saying, as","context":"Active listening is the skill of listening to an individual with your full attention without arguing with, commenting on, or misinterpreting what they have said. With active listening, you make a conscious effort to keep your attention focused on what the other person is saying, as","options":["Professional Documentation","Categories","Standard Operating Procedure","Active Listening"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Active Listening: Active listening is the skill of listening to an individual with your full attention without arguing with, commenting on, or misinterpreting what they have said. With active listening, you make a conscious effort to keep your attention focused on what the other person is saying, as"},{"id":"c2-0029","module":11,"lesson":"11B Professional Communication","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nThere will inevitably be a need to establish some technical facts with the customer. This means directing the customer to answer your questions. There are two broad types of questioning: • Open-ended—A question that invites the other person to compose a response. For example,","context":"There will inevitably be a need to establish some technical facts with the customer. This means directing the customer to answer your questions. There are two broad types of questioning: • Open-ended—A question that invites the other person to compose a response. For example,","options":["Set Expectations and Timeline","Clarifying and Questioning Techniques","Ticket Management","Typical examples of SOPs are as follows:"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Clarifying and Questioning Techniques: There will inevitably be a need to establish some technical facts with the customer. This means directing the customer to answer your questions. There are two broad types of questioning: • Open-ended—A question that invites the other person to compose a response. For example,"},{"id":"c2-0030","module":11,"lesson":"11C Operating Systems","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nA difficult situation occurs when either you or the customer becomes, or risks becoming, angry or upset. There are several techniques that you can use to defuse this type of tension.","context":"A difficult situation occurs when either you or the customer becomes, or risks becoming, angry or upset. There are several techniques that you can use to defuse this type of tension.","options":["Difficult Situations","Meet Expectations and Timeline","Service Level Agreements","Escalation Levels"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Difficult Situations: A difficult situation occurs when either you or the customer becomes, or risks becoming, angry or upset. There are several techniques that you can use to defuse this type of tension."},{"id":"c2-0031","module":11,"lesson":"11A Documentation","question":"What is being described here?\n\nUnderstand that an angry customer is usually frustrated that things are not working properly, or feels let down. Perhaps a technician arrived late and the customer is already irritated. Or perhaps the customer has spent a large amount of money and is now anxious that it has been","context":"Understand that an angry customer is usually frustrated that things are not working properly, or feels let down. Perhaps a technician arrived late and the customer is already irritated. Or perhaps the customer has spent a large amount of money and is now anxious that it has been","options":["Incident Reporting and Ticketing Systems","Repair and Replace Options","Support Documentation and Knowledge Base Articles","Maintain a Positive Attitude"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Maintain a Positive Attitude: Understand that an angry customer is usually frustrated that things are not working properly, or feels let down. Perhaps a technician arrived late and the customer is already irritated. Or perhaps the customer has spent a large amount of money and is now anxious that it has been"},{"id":"c2-0032","module":11,"lesson":"11B Professional Communication","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nIt is never easy to talk to someone who is unreasonable, abusive, or shouting, but it is important to be able to deal with these situations professionally.","context":"It is never easy to talk to someone who is unreasonable, abusive, or shouting, but it is important to be able to deal with these situations professionally.","options":["Clear Written Communication","Follow Up","Dealing with Difficult Customers","The general process of ticket management is as follows:"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Dealing with Difficult Customers: It is never easy to talk to someone who is unreasonable, abusive, or shouting, but it is important to be able to deal with these situations professionally."},{"id":"c2-0033","module":11,"lesson":"11C Operating Systems","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nImage by Wang Tom © 123RF.com. Do Not Post Experiences on Social Media Everyone has bad days when they feel the need to get some difficult situation off their chest.","context":"Image by Wang Tom © 123RF.com. Do Not Post Experiences on Social Media Everyone has bad days when they feel the need to get some difficult situation off their chest.","options":["Identify early signs that a customer is becoming angry","Categories and Severity","Knowledge Base","Professional Support Delivery"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Identify early signs that a customer is becoming angry: Image by Wang Tom © 123RF.com. Do Not Post Experiences on Social Media Everyone has bad days when they feel the need to get some difficult situation off their chest."},{"id":"c2-0034","module":11,"lesson":"11A Documentation","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nEveryone has bad days when they feel the need to get some difficult situation off their chest.","context":"Everyone has bad days when they feel the need to get some difficult situation off their chest.","options":["Policy Documentation","Categories","Do Not Post Experiences on Social Media","Be On Time"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Do Not Post Experiences on Social Media: Everyone has bad days when they feel the need to get some difficult situation off their chest."},{"id":"c2-0035","module":11,"lesson":"11B Professional Communication","question":"What is being described here?\n\nLearning Outcomes As you study this lesson, please answer the following questions: • What are the four main operating systems found on computer systems?","context":"Learning Outcomes As you study this lesson, please answer the following questions: • What are the four main operating systems found on computer systems?","options":["Professional Support Processes","Avoid Distractions","1.1 Explain common operating system (OS) types and their purposes","Ticket Management"],"answer":2,"explanation":"1.1 Explain common operating system (OS) types and their purposes: Learning Outcomes As you study this lesson, please answer the following questions: • What are the four main operating systems found on computer systems?"},{"id":"c2-0036","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows User Settings","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nAn OS is made up of kernel files and device drivers to interface with the hardware plus programs to provide a user interface and configuration tools. The earliest operating systems for PCs, such as Microsoft's Disk Operating System (DOS), used a command-line user interface","context":"An OS is made up of kernel files and device drivers to interface with the hardware plus programs to provide a user interface and configuration tools. The earliest operating systems for PCs, such as Microsoft's Disk Operating System (DOS), used a command-line user interface","options":["Windows Account Settings","Personalization Settings","Windows Interfaces","Windows Security"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Windows Interfaces: An OS is made up of kernel files and device drivers to interface with the hardware plus programs to provide a user interface and configuration tools. The earliest operating systems for PCs, such as Microsoft's Disk Operating System (DOS), used a command-line user interface"},{"id":"c2-0037","module":12,"lesson":"12B Windows System Settings","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nOne of the main functions of an OS is to provide an interface (or shell) for the user to configure and operate the computer hardware and software. Windows has several interface components designed both for general use and for more technical configuration and troubleshooting.","context":"One of the main functions of an OS is to provide an interface (or shell) for the user to configure and operate the computer hardware and software. Windows has several interface components designed both for general use and for more technical configuration and troubleshooting.","options":["Activation","Windows 10 Desktop","File Explorer","User Accounts Control Panel Applet"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Windows 10 Desktop: One of the main functions of an OS is to provide an interface (or shell) for the user to configure and operate the computer hardware and software. Windows has several interface components designed both for general use and for more technical configuration and troubleshooting."},{"id":"c2-0038","module":12,"lesson":"12C Applications","question":"What is being described here?\n\nWindows 11 refreshes the desktop style by introducing a center-aligned taskbar, better spacing for touch control, and rounded corners. It also makes the multiple desktops feature more accessible. Multiple desktops allow the user to set up different workspaces, such as one","context":"Windows 11 refreshes the desktop style by introducing a center-aligned taskbar, better spacing for touch control, and rounded corners. It also makes the multiple desktops feature more accessible. Multiple desktops allow the user to set up different workspaces, such as one","options":["System Objects","Windows 11 Desktop","Privacy and Security Settings","Device Settings"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Windows 11 Desktop: Windows 11 refreshes the desktop style by introducing a center-aligned taskbar, better spacing for touch control, and rounded corners. It also makes the multiple desktops feature more accessible. Multiple desktops allow the user to set up different workspaces, such as one"},{"id":"c2-0039","module":12,"lesson":"12D Cloud Apps","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nThe Windows Settings app and Control Panel are the two main interfaces for administering Windows. Administering an OS means configuring options, setting up user accounts, and adding and removing devices and software. All Windows configuration data is ultimately held in","context":"The Windows Settings app and Control Panel are the two main interfaces for administering Windows. Administering an OS means configuring options, setting up user accounts, and adding and removing devices and software. All Windows configuration data is ultimately held in","options":["Drives and Folders","Display and Sound Settings","Windows Settings and Control Panel","Desktop Settings"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Windows Settings and Control Panel: The Windows Settings app and Control Panel are the two main interfaces for administering Windows. Administering an OS means configuring options, setting up user accounts, and adding and removing devices and software. All Windows configuration data is ultimately held in"},{"id":"c2-0040","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows User Settings","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nWindows Settings is a touch-enabled interface for managing Windows. The Settings app is the preferred administrative interface. Configuration option \"pages\" are divided between a few main headings.","context":"Windows Settings is a touch-enabled interface for managing Windows. The Settings app is the preferred administrative interface. Configuration option \"pages\" are divided between a few main headings.","options":["The principal Display configuration settings are:","Windows Settings","Time and Language Settings","File Explorer Options"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Windows Settings: Windows Settings is a touch-enabled interface for managing Windows. The Settings app is the preferred administrative interface. Configuration option \"pages\" are divided between a few main headings."},{"id":"c2-0041","module":12,"lesson":"12B Windows System Settings","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nMost of the standard Windows 10 and Windows 11 configuration settings can be located within Windows Settings, but not all of them. Some options are still configured via the legacy Control Panel interface.","context":"Most of the standard Windows 10 and Windows 11 configuration settings can be located within Windows Settings, but not all of them. Some options are still configured via the legacy Control Panel interface.","options":["Power Options","Control Panel","Indexing Options","The Time and Language settings pages are used for two main purposes:"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Control Panel: Most of the standard Windows 10 and Windows 11 configuration settings can be located within Windows Settings, but not all of them. Some options are still configured via the legacy Control Panel interface."},{"id":"c2-0042","module":12,"lesson":"12C Applications","question":"What is being described here?\n\nAccounts Settings A user account controls access to the computer. Each account can be assigned rights or privileges to make OS configuration changes. Accounts can also be assigned permissions on files, folders, and printers.","context":"Accounts Settings A user account controls access to the computer. Each account can be assigned rights or privileges to make OS configuration changes. Accounts can also be assigned permissions on files, folders, and printers.","options":["Apps, Programs, and Features","Tools Work Folders","Personalization Settings","System Settings"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Tools Work Folders: Accounts Settings A user account controls access to the computer. Each account can be assigned rights or privileges to make OS configuration changes. Accounts can also be assigned permissions on files, folders, and printers."},{"id":"c2-0043","module":12,"lesson":"12D Cloud Apps","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nA user account controls access to the computer. Each account can be assigned rights or privileges to make OS configuration changes. Accounts can also be assigned permissions on files, folders, and printers.","context":"A user account controls access to the computer. Each account can be assigned rights or privileges to make OS configuration changes. Accounts can also be assigned permissions on files, folders, and printers.","options":["Windows supports several types of installable software:","Accounts Settings","File Explorer","Windows Update"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Accounts Settings: A user account controls access to the computer. Each account can be assigned rights or privileges to make OS configuration changes. Accounts can also be assigned permissions on files, folders, and printers."},{"id":"c2-0044","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows User Settings","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nA Windows account can either be configured as a local-only account or linked to a Microsoft Account. A local account can be used to sign in on a single computer only. A Microsoft account gives access to Microsoft's cloud services and allows sign-in and syncs desktop settings and","context":"A Windows account can either be configured as a local-only account or linked to a Microsoft Account. A local account can be used to sign in on a single computer only. A Microsoft account gives access to Microsoft's cloud services and allows sign-in and syncs desktop settings and","options":["Windows Security","Windows Account Settings","System Objects","Apps Settings"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Windows Account Settings: A Windows account can either be configured as a local-only account or linked to a Microsoft Account. A local account can be used to sign in on a single computer only. A Microsoft account gives access to Microsoft's cloud services and allows sign-in and syncs desktop settings and"},{"id":"c2-0045","module":12,"lesson":"12B Windows System Settings","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nThe User Accounts Applet in Control Panel is the legacy interface. Many of its functions are obfuscated or hidden and may not readily be used to add new accounts. It does provide options for adjusting the account name and changing the account privilege level between the","context":"The User Accounts Applet in Control Panel is the legacy interface. Many of its functions are obfuscated or hidden and may not readily be used to add new accounts. It does provide options for adjusting the account name and changing the account privilege level between the","options":["Activation","Drives and Folders","Programs and Features","User Accounts Control Panel Applet"],"answer":3,"explanation":"User Accounts Control Panel Applet: The User Accounts Applet in Control Panel is the legacy interface. Many of its functions are obfuscated or hidden and may not readily be used to add new accounts. It does provide options for adjusting the account name and changing the account privilege level between the"},{"id":"c2-0046","module":12,"lesson":"12C Applications","question":"What is being described here?\n\nPrivacy and Security Settings govern what usage data Windows is permitted to collect and what device functions are enabled and for which apps. The security settings allow a user to change antivirus, browser, and firewall settings. There are multiple settings toggles to determine what","context":"Privacy and Security Settings govern what usage data Windows is permitted to collect and what device functions are enabled and for which apps. The security settings allow a user to change antivirus, browser, and firewall settings. There are multiple settings toggles to determine what","options":["File Explorer Options","Mail","Device Settings","Privacy and Security Settings"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Privacy and Security Settings: Privacy and Security Settings govern what usage data Windows is permitted to collect and what device functions are enabled and for which apps. The security settings allow a user to change antivirus, browser, and firewall settings. There are multiple settings toggles to determine what"},{"id":"c2-0047","module":12,"lesson":"12D Cloud Apps","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nThe desktop can be configured to use local settings and personalized to adjust its appearance.","context":"The desktop can be configured to use local settings and personalized to adjust its appearance.","options":["Desktop Settings","Indexing Options","Display and Sound Settings","Gaming"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Desktop Settings: The desktop can be configured to use local settings and personalized to adjust its appearance."},{"id":"c2-0048","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows User Settings","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nThe Time and Language settings pages are used for two main purposes: • Set the correct date/time and time zone. Keeping the PC synchronized to an accurate time source is important for processes such as authentication and backup.","context":"The Time and Language settings pages are used for two main purposes: • Set the correct date/time and time zone. Keeping the PC synchronized to an accurate time source is important for processes such as authentication and backup.","options":["Network Settings","Time and Language Settings","System Settings","The principal Display configuration settings are:"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Time and Language Settings: The Time and Language settings pages are used for two main purposes: • Set the correct date/time and time zone. Keeping the PC synchronized to an accurate time source is important for processes such as authentication and backup."},{"id":"c2-0049","module":12,"lesson":"12B Windows System Settings","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\n• Set the correct date/time and time zone. Keeping the PC synchronized to an accurate time source is important for processes such as authentication and backup.","context":"• Set the correct date/time and time zone. Keeping the PC synchronized to an accurate time source is important for processes such as authentication and backup.","options":["Windows Update","Windows Interfaces","The Time and Language settings pages are used for two main purposes:","Power Options"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The Time and Language settings pages are used for two main purposes:: • Set the correct date/time and time zone. Keeping the PC synchronized to an accurate time source is important for processes such as authentication and backup."},{"id":"c2-0050","module":12,"lesson":"12C Applications","question":"What is being described here?\n\nThe Personalization Settings allow you to select and customize themes, which set the appearance of the desktop environment. Personalization and theme settings include the desktop wallpaper, screen saver, color scheme, font, and properties for the Start menu and","context":"The Personalization Settings allow you to select and customize themes, which set the appearance of the desktop environment. Personalization and theme settings include the desktop wallpaper, screen saver, color scheme, font, and properties for the Start menu and","options":["Personalization Settings","Windows Security","Windows 10 Desktop","Apps, Programs, and Features"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Personalization Settings: The Personalization Settings allow you to select and customize themes, which set the appearance of the desktop environment. Personalization and theme settings include the desktop wallpaper, screen saver, color scheme, font, and properties for the Start menu and"},{"id":"c2-0051","module":12,"lesson":"12D Cloud Apps","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nFile management is a critical part of using a computer. As a computer support professional, you will often have to assist users with locating files. In Windows, file management is performed using the File Explorer app. File Explorer enables you to open, copy, move, rename, view, and","context":"File management is a critical part of using a computer. As a computer support professional, you will often have to assist users with locating files. In Windows, file management is performed using the File Explorer app. File Explorer enables you to open, copy, move, rename, view, and","options":["File Explorer","Activation","Windows 11 Desktop","Windows supports several types of installable software:"],"answer":0,"explanation":"File Explorer: File management is a critical part of using a computer. As a computer support professional, you will often have to assist users with locating files. In Windows, file management is performed using the File Explorer app. File Explorer enables you to open, copy, move, rename, view, and"},{"id":"c2-0052","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows User Settings","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nIn Windows, access to data files is typically mediated by system objects. These are shown in the left-hand navigation pane in File Explorer. Some of the main system objects are: • User account- Contains personal data folders belonging to the signed-in account profile. For","context":"In Windows, access to data files is typically mediated by system objects. These are shown in the left-hand navigation pane in File Explorer. Some of the main system objects are: • User account- Contains personal data folders belonging to the signed-in account profile. For","options":["Windows Settings and Control Panel","Apps Settings","Device Settings","System Objects"],"answer":3,"explanation":"System Objects: In Windows, access to data files is typically mediated by system objects. These are shown in the left-hand navigation pane in File Explorer. Some of the main system objects are: • User account- Contains personal data folders belonging to the signed-in account profile. For"},{"id":"c2-0053","module":12,"lesson":"12B Windows System Settings","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nWhile the system objects represent logical storage areas, the actual data files are written to disk drives. Within the This PC object, drives are referred to by letters and optional labels. A \"drive\" can be a single physical disk or a partition on a disk, a shared network folder mapped to a drive","context":"While the system objects represent logical storage areas, the actual data files are written to disk drives. Within the This PC object, drives are referred to by letters and optional labels. A \"drive\" can be a single physical disk or a partition on a disk, a shared network folder mapped to a drive","options":["Windows Settings","Drives and Folders","Display and Sound Settings","Programs and Features"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Drives and Folders: While the system objects represent logical storage areas, the actual data files are written to disk drives. Within the This PC object, drives are referred to by letters and optional labels. A \"drive\" can be a single physical disk or a partition on a disk, a shared network folder mapped to a drive"},{"id":"c2-0054","module":12,"lesson":"12C Applications","question":"What is being described here?\n\nFile Explorer has configurable options for view settings and file search.","context":"File Explorer has configurable options for view settings and file search.","options":["Mail","File Explorer Options","Control Panel","The principal Display configuration settings are:"],"answer":1,"explanation":"File Explorer Options: File Explorer has configurable options for view settings and file search."},{"id":"c2-0055","module":12,"lesson":"12D Cloud Apps","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nYou can configure file search behavior on the Search tab of the File Explorer Options dialog.","context":"You can configure file search behavior on the Search tab of the File Explorer Options dialog.","options":["Tools Work Folders","Power Options","Gaming","Indexing Options"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Indexing Options: You can configure file search behavior on the Search tab of the File Explorer Options dialog."},{"id":"c2-0056","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows User Settings","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nThe System Settings page in the Settings app presents options for configuring input and output devices, power, remote desktop, notifications, and clipboard (data copying). There is also an About page listing key hardware and OS version information.","context":"The System Settings page in the Settings app presents options for configuring input and output devices, power, remote desktop, notifications, and clipboard (data copying). There is also an About page listing key hardware and OS version information.","options":["Accounts Settings","Network Settings","System Settings","Apps, Programs, and Features"],"answer":2,"explanation":"System Settings: The System Settings page in the Settings app presents options for configuring input and output devices, power, remote desktop, notifications, and clipboard (data copying). There is also an About page listing key hardware and OS version information."},{"id":"c2-0057","module":12,"lesson":"12B Windows System Settings","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nWindows Update hosts critical updates and security patches plus optional software and hardware device driver updates.","context":"Windows Update hosts critical updates and security patches plus optional software and hardware device driver updates.","options":["Windows Interfaces","Windows supports several types of installable software:","Windows Update","Windows Account Settings"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Windows Update: Windows Update hosts critical updates and security patches plus optional software and hardware device driver updates."},{"id":"c2-0058","module":12,"lesson":"12C Applications","question":"What is being described here?\n\nThe Windows Security page contains shortcuts to the management pages for the built-in Windows Defender virus/threat protection and firewall product.","context":"The Windows Security page contains shortcuts to the management pages for the built-in Windows Defender virus/threat protection and firewall product.","options":["Windows Security","Apps Settings","User Accounts Control Panel Applet","Windows 10 Desktop"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Windows Security: The Windows Security page contains shortcuts to the management pages for the built-in Windows Defender virus/threat protection and firewall product."},{"id":"c2-0059","module":12,"lesson":"12D Cloud Apps","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nMicrosoft Product Activation is an anti-piracy technology that verifies that software products are legitimately purchased. You must activate Windows within a given number of days after installation. After the grace period, certain features will be disabled until the system is activated","context":"Microsoft Product Activation is an anti-piracy technology that verifies that software products are legitimately purchased. You must activate Windows within a given number of days after installation. After the grace period, certain features will be disabled until the system is activated","options":["Windows 11 Desktop","Privacy and Security Settings","Activation","Programs and Features"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Activation: Microsoft Product Activation is an anti-piracy technology that verifies that software products are legitimately purchased. You must activate Windows within a given number of days after installation. After the grace period, certain features will be disabled until the system is activated"},{"id":"c2-0060","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows User Settings","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nMost Windows-compatible hardware devices use Plug and Play. This means that Windows automatically detects when a new device is connected, locates drivers for it, and installs and configures it with minimal user input. In some cases, you may need to install the hardware","context":"Most Windows-compatible hardware devices use Plug and Play. This means that Windows automatically detects when a new device is connected, locates drivers for it, and installs and configures it with minimal user input. In some cases, you may need to install the hardware","options":["Device Settings","Mail","Desktop Settings","Windows Settings and Control Panel"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Device Settings: Most Windows-compatible hardware devices use Plug and Play. This means that Windows automatically detects when a new device is connected, locates drivers for it, and installs and configures it with minimal user input. In some cases, you may need to install the hardware"},{"id":"c2-0061","module":12,"lesson":"12B Windows System Settings","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nThe principal Display configuration settings are: • Scale—A large high-resolution screen can use quite small font sizes for the user interface.","context":"The principal Display configuration settings are: • Scale—A large high-resolution screen can use quite small font sizes for the user interface.","options":["Windows Settings","Time and Language Settings","Display and Sound Settings","Gaming"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Display and Sound Settings: The principal Display configuration settings are: • Scale—A large high-resolution screen can use quite small font sizes for the user interface."},{"id":"c2-0062","module":12,"lesson":"12C Applications","question":"What is being described here?\n\n• Scale—A large high-resolution screen can use quite small font sizes for the user interface.","context":"• Scale—A large high-resolution screen can use quite small font sizes for the user interface.","options":["The Time and Language settings pages are used for two main purposes:","The principal Display configuration settings are:","Control Panel","Network Settings"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The principal Display configuration settings are:: • Scale—A large high-resolution screen can use quite small font sizes for the user interface."},{"id":"c2-0063","module":12,"lesson":"12D Cloud Apps","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nPower management allows Windows to selectively reduce or turn off the power supplied to hardware components. The computer can be configured to enter a power-saving mode automatically; for example, if there is no use of an input device for a set period. This is","context":"Power management allows Windows to selectively reduce or turn off the power supplied to hardware components. The computer can be configured to enter a power-saving mode automatically; for example, if there is no use of an input device for a set period. This is","options":["Tools Work Folders","Windows Interfaces","Personalization Settings","Power Options"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Power Options: Power management allows Windows to selectively reduce or turn off the power supplied to hardware components. The computer can be configured to enter a power-saving mode automatically; for example, if there is no use of an input device for a set period. This is"},{"id":"c2-0064","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows User Settings","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nWindows supports several types of installable software: The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 12: Configuring Windows • Windows Features are components of the operating system that can be enabled or disabled.","context":"Windows supports several types of installable software: The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 12: Configuring Windows • Windows Features are components of the operating system that can be enabled or disabled.","options":["Windows 10 Desktop","File Explorer","Accounts Settings","Apps, Programs, and Features"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Apps, Programs, and Features: Windows supports several types of installable software: The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 12: Configuring Windows • Windows Features are components of the operating system that can be enabled or disabled."},{"id":"c2-0065","module":12,"lesson":"12B Windows System Settings","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nThe Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 12: Configuring Windows • Windows Features are components of the operating system that can be enabled or disabled.","context":"The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 12: Configuring Windows • Windows Features are components of the operating system that can be enabled or disabled.","options":["Windows 11 Desktop","Windows supports several types of installable software:","Windows Account Settings","System Objects"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Windows supports several types of installable software:: The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 12: Configuring Windows • Windows Features are components of the operating system that can be enabled or disabled."},{"id":"c2-0066","module":12,"lesson":"12C Applications","question":"What is being described here?\n\nIn the Settings app, the Apps Settings group is used to view and remove installed apps and Windows Features. You can also configure which app should act as the default for opening, editing, and printing particular file types and manage which apps run at startup.","context":"In the Settings app, the Apps Settings group is used to view and remove installed apps and Windows Features. You can also configure which app should act as the default for opening, editing, and printing particular file types and manage which apps run at startup.","options":["Windows Settings and Control Panel","Drives and Folders","Apps Settings","User Accounts Control Panel Applet"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Apps Settings: In the Settings app, the Apps Settings group is used to view and remove installed apps and Windows Features. You can also configure which app should act as the default for opening, editing, and printing particular file types and manage which apps run at startup."},{"id":"c2-0067","module":12,"lesson":"12D Cloud Apps","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nThe Programs and Features Control Panel applet is the legacy software management interface.","context":"The Programs and Features Control Panel applet is the legacy software management interface.","options":["Privacy and Security Settings","File Explorer Options","Programs and Features","Windows Settings"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Programs and Features: The Programs and Features Control Panel applet is the legacy software management interface."},{"id":"c2-0068","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows User Settings","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nThe Mail Applet in Control Panel is added if the Microsoft Outlook client email application is installed on the computer. It can be used to add email accounts/profiles and manage the .OST and .PST data files used to cache and archive messages. Detailed configuration of the email","context":"The Mail Applet in Control Panel is added if the Microsoft Outlook client email application is installed on the computer. It can be used to add email accounts/profiles and manage the .OST and .PST data files used to cache and archive messages. Detailed configuration of the email","options":["Mail","Indexing Options","Control Panel","Desktop Settings"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Mail: The Mail Applet in Control Panel is added if the Microsoft Outlook client email application is installed on the computer. It can be used to add email accounts/profiles and manage the .OST and .PST data files used to cache and archive messages. Detailed configuration of the email"},{"id":"c2-0069","module":12,"lesson":"12B Windows System Settings","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nThe Gaming settings page is used to toggle game mode on and off. Game mode suspends Windows Update and dedicates resources to supporting the 3-D performance and frame rate of the active game app rather than other software or background services.","context":"The Gaming settings page is used to toggle game mode on and off. Game mode suspends Windows Update and dedicates resources to supporting the 3-D performance and frame rate of the active game app rather than other software or background services.","options":["System Settings","Time and Language Settings","Gaming","Tools Work Folders"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Gaming: The Gaming settings page is used to toggle game mode on and off. Game mode suspends Windows Update and dedicates resources to supporting the 3-D performance and frame rate of the active game app rather than other software or background services."},{"id":"c2-0070","module":12,"lesson":"12C Applications","question":"What is being described here?\n\nA Windows host can be configured with one or more types of network adapters. Adapter types include Ethernet, Wi-Fi, cellular radio, and virtual private network (VPN). Each adapter must be configured with Internet Protocol (IP) address information. Each network that an adapter is used","context":"A Windows host can be configured with one or more types of network adapters. Adapter types include Ethernet, Wi-Fi, cellular radio, and virtual private network (VPN). Each adapter must be configured with Internet Protocol (IP) address information. Each network that an adapter is used","options":["Network Settings","Windows Update","Accounts Settings","The Time and Language settings pages are used for two main purposes:"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Network Settings: A Windows host can be configured with one or more types of network adapters. Adapter types include Ethernet, Wi-Fi, cellular radio, and virtual private network (VPN). Each adapter must be configured with Internet Protocol (IP) address information. Each network that an adapter is used"},{"id":"c2-0071","module":13,"lesson":"13A Management Consoles","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nDevice Manager (devmgmt.msc) lets you view and edit installed hardware properties, change configuration settings, update drivers, and remove or disable devices.","context":"Device Manager (devmgmt.msc) lets you view and edit installed hardware properties, change configuration settings, update drivers, and remove or disable devices.","options":["Device Manager","Editing the Registry","Local Users and Groups Console","Changing the Current Directory"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Device Manager: Device Manager (devmgmt.msc) lets you view and edit installed hardware properties, change configuration settings, update drivers, and remove or disable devices."},{"id":"c2-0072","module":13,"lesson":"13B Command-Line Tools","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nWindows may identify a device's type and function but fail to find a driver, resulting in an \"Unknown Device\" or a \"generic\" type with a yellow exclamation mark in Device Manager. If the device never worked, ensure compatibility of the device and driver with the OS. Manufacturers","context":"Windows may identify a device's type and function but fail to find a driver, resulting in an \"Unknown Device\" or a \"generic\" type with a yellow exclamation mark in Device Manager. If the device never worked, ensure compatibility of the device and driver with the OS. Manufacturers","options":["Updating and Troubleshooting Devices","Here are some shortcuts:","Certificate Manager","Custom Microsoft Management Consoles"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Updating and Troubleshooting Devices: Windows may identify a device's type and function but fail to find a driver, resulting in an \"Unknown Device\" or a \"generic\" type with a yellow exclamation mark in Device Manager. If the device never worked, ensure compatibility of the device and driver with the OS. Manufacturers"},{"id":"c2-0073","module":13,"lesson":"13C Windows Networking","question":"What is being described here?\n\nFor Plug and Play, hot-swappable devices, you can remove them without uninstalling. Before removing a storage device, close any active applications, click the Safely Remove Hardware icon in the taskbar, and select the option to stop or eject the device.","context":"For Plug and Play, hot-swappable devices, you can remove them without uninstalling. Before removing a storage device, close any active applications, click the Safely Remove Hardware icon in the taskbar, and select the option to stop or eject the device.","options":["Changing the Current Drive","Command Prompt","Key subfolders include:","Removing, Uninstalling, and Disabling Devices"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Removing, Uninstalling, and Disabling Devices: For Plug and Play, hot-swappable devices, you can remove them without uninstalling. Before removing a storage device, close any active applications, click the Safely Remove Hardware icon in the taskbar, and select the option to stop or eject the device."},{"id":"c2-0074","module":13,"lesson":"13A Management Consoles","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nThe disk subsystem stores all data generated by the operating system and applications. As the primary store of so much data, ensuring its reliability and performance is a critical management task.","context":"The disk subsystem stores all data generated by the operating system and applications. As the primary store of so much data, ensuring its reliability and performance is a critical management task.","options":["Disk Management Console","Group Policy Editor","Administrative Command Prompt","File Management Commands"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Disk Management Console: The disk subsystem stores all data generated by the operating system and applications. As the primary store of so much data, ensuring its reliability and performance is a critical management task."},{"id":"c2-0075","module":13,"lesson":"13B Command-Line Tools","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nOf all the computer's subsystems, disk drives, and the file system probably require the most attention to keep in optimum working order. File storage is subject to three main problems: • Fragmentation: On a hard disk, files ideally occupy contiguous clusters. Over time, they","context":"Of all the computer's subsystems, disk drives, and the file system probably require the most attention to keep in optimum working order. File storage is subject to three main problems: • Fragmentation: On a hard disk, files ideally occupy contiguous clusters. Over time, they","options":["Copying Directory Structures","Disk Maintenance Tools","Command Syntax","Registry Editor"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Disk Maintenance Tools: Of all the computer's subsystems, disk drives, and the file system probably require the most attention to keep in optimum working order. File storage is subject to three main problems: • Fragmentation: On a hard disk, files ideally occupy contiguous clusters. Over time, they"},{"id":"c2-0076","module":13,"lesson":"13C Windows Networking","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nThe Defragment and Optimize Drives tool enhances HDD and SSD performance: • HDDs: On an HDD, defragmenting reorganizes file data into contiguous clusters, reducing the time the controller needs to seek across the disk to read a file.","context":"The Defragment and Optimize Drives tool enhances HDD and SSD performance: • HDDs: On an HDD, defragmenting reorganizes file data into contiguous clusters, reducing the time the controller needs to seek across the disk to read a file.","options":["Registry Keys","Getting Help","The command includes various switches for enhanced functionality:","Disk Defragmenter"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Disk Defragmenter: The Defragment and Optimize Drives tool enhances HDD and SSD performance: • HDDs: On an HDD, defragmenting reorganizes file data into contiguous clusters, reducing the time the controller needs to seek across the disk to read a file."},{"id":"c2-0077","module":13,"lesson":"13A Management Consoles","question":"What is being described here?\n\nThe Disk Clean-up tool identifies files safe for deletion to free up space, including those in the Recycle Bin and temporary files. Running it in administrator mode with the Clean up system files option reclaims space from caches like Windows Update and Defender.","context":"The Disk Clean-up tool identifies files safe for deletion to free up space, including those in the Recycle Bin and temporary files. Running it in administrator mode with the Clean up system files option reclaims space from caches like Windows Update and Defender.","options":["Disk Clean-up","Creating a Directory","Editing the Registry","Navigation Commands"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Disk Clean-up: The Disk Clean-up tool identifies files safe for deletion to free up space, including those in the Recycle Bin and temporary files. Running it in administrator mode with the Clean up system files option reclaims space from caches like Windows Update and Defender."},{"id":"c2-0078","module":13,"lesson":"13B Command-Line Tools","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nThe Task Scheduler automates commands and scripts, with many Windows processes having predefined schedules. Tasks can be set to run once at a future date/time or on a recurring schedule. They can involve simple applications (including switches, if necessary), batch files, or","context":"The Task Scheduler automates commands and scripts, with many Windows processes having predefined schedules. Tasks can be set to run once at a future date/time or on a recurring schedule. They can involve simple applications (including switches, if necessary), batch files, or","options":["Task Scheduler","Listing Files and Directories","Removing a Directory","Custom Microsoft Management Consoles"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Task Scheduler: The Task Scheduler automates commands and scripts, with many Windows processes having predefined schedules. Tasks can be set to run once at a future date/time or on a recurring schedule. They can involve simple applications (including switches, if necessary), batch files, or"},{"id":"c2-0079","module":13,"lesson":"13C Windows Networking","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nThe Local Users and Groups console offers an advanced interface for managing user accounts, including creating, modifying, disabling, deleting, and resetting passwords.","context":"The Local Users and Groups console offers an advanced interface for managing user accounts, including creating, modifying, disabling, deleting, and resetting passwords.","options":["Disk Management Commands","Local Users and Groups Console","Changing the Current Directory","Command Prompt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Local Users and Groups Console: The Local Users and Groups console offers an advanced interface for managing user accounts, including creating, modifying, disabling, deleting, and resetting passwords."},{"id":"c2-0080","module":13,"lesson":"13A Management Consoles","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nA digital certificate verifies the identity of a user, computer, or service, with validity guaranteed by the issuing certification authority (CA). The certificate manager console displays installed certificates and allows for requesting and importing new ones.","context":"A digital certificate verifies the identity of a user, computer, or service, with validity guaranteed by the issuing certification authority (CA). The certificate manager console displays installed certificates and allows for requesting and importing new ones.","options":["The diskpart Command","Administrative Command Prompt","Certificate Manager","Here are some shortcuts:"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Certificate Manager: A digital certificate verifies the identity of a user, computer, or service, with validity guaranteed by the issuing certification authority (CA). The certificate manager console displays installed certificates and allows for requesting and importing new ones."},{"id":"c2-0081","module":13,"lesson":"13B Command-Line Tools","question":"What is being described here?\n\n• Personal Folder: Stores certificates issued to the user account, used for network authentication, data encryption, and digital signatures.","context":"• Personal Folder: Stores certificates issued to the user account, used for network authentication, data encryption, and digital signatures.","options":["The format Command","Command Syntax","Key subfolders include:","Changing the Current Drive"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Key subfolders include:: • Personal Folder: Stores certificates issued to the user account, used for network authentication, data encryption, and digital signatures."},{"id":"c2-0082","module":13,"lesson":"13C Windows Networking","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nGUI tools like Settings and Control Panel modify user profiles and system configurations stored in the registry, but many registry settings aren't accessible through these tools. The Group Policy Editor offers a robust way to configure these Windows settings without directly editing","context":"GUI tools like Settings and Control Panel modify user profiles and system configurations stored in the registry, but many registry settings aren't accessible through these tools. The Group Policy Editor offers a robust way to configure these Windows settings without directly editing","options":["File Management Commands","Group Policy Editor","The chkdsk Command","Getting Help"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Group Policy Editor: GUI tools like Settings and Control Panel modify user profiles and system configurations stored in the registry, but many registry settings aren't accessible through these tools. The Group Policy Editor offers a robust way to configure these Windows settings without directly editing"},{"id":"c2-0083","module":13,"lesson":"13A Management Consoles","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nThe Windows registry is a remotely accessible database for storing configuration information for the OS, devices, and applications. Use the Registry Editor (regedit.msc) to view or edit the registry.","context":"The Windows registry is a remotely accessible database for storing configuration information for the OS, devices, and applications. Use the Registry Editor (regedit.msc) to view or edit the registry.","options":["Navigation Commands","Copying Directory Structures","There are three ways to use chkdsk :","Registry Editor"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Registry Editor: The Windows registry is a remotely accessible database for storing configuration information for the OS, devices, and applications. Use the Registry Editor (regedit.msc) to view or edit the registry."},{"id":"c2-0084","module":13,"lesson":"13B Command-Line Tools","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nThe Registry is organized into five root keys containing computer and user databases: The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 13: Managing Windows • HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE (HKLM): Manages system-wide settings.","context":"The Registry is organized into five root keys containing computer and user databases: The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 13: Managing Windows • HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE (HKLM): Manages system-wide settings.","options":["Listing Files and Directories","Registry Keys","Device Manager","The command includes various switches for enhanced functionality:"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Registry Keys: The Registry is organized into five root keys containing computer and user databases: The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 13: Managing Windows • HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE (HKLM): Manages system-wide settings."},{"id":"c2-0085","module":13,"lesson":"13C Windows Networking","question":"What is being described here?\n\nRoot keys contain subkeys and data items called value entries, similar to folders and files. A value entry includes the name, data type (such as string or binary), and the value itself. Use the Find tool to search for keys or values.","context":"Root keys contain subkeys and data items called value entries, similar to folders and files. A value entry includes the name, data type (such as string or binary), and the value itself. Use the Find tool to search for keys or values.","options":["Editing the Registry","Creating a Directory","Changing the Current Directory","Updating and Troubleshooting Devices"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Editing the Registry: Root keys contain subkeys and data items called value entries, similar to folders and files. A value entry includes the name, data type (such as string or binary), and the value itself. Use the Find tool to search for keys or values."},{"id":"c2-0086","module":13,"lesson":"13A Management Consoles","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nA Microsoft Management Console (MMC) is a container for snap-ins like Device Manager, Disk Management, Group Policy Editor, and Certificate Manager. Use the mmc command to customize and create a console with your chosen snap-ins. Save the console as an MSC file in the","context":"A Microsoft Management Console (MMC) is a container for snap-ins like Device Manager, Disk Management, Group Policy Editor, and Certificate Manager. Use the mmc command to customize and create a console with your chosen snap-ins. Save the console as an MSC file in the","options":["Removing, Uninstalling, and Disabling Devices","Removing a Directory","Here are some shortcuts:","Custom Microsoft Management Consoles"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Custom Microsoft Management Consoles: A Microsoft Management Console (MMC) is a container for snap-ins like Device Manager, Disk Management, Group Policy Editor, and Certificate Manager. Use the mmc command to customize and create a console with your chosen snap-ins. Save the console as an MSC file in the"},{"id":"c2-0087","module":13,"lesson":"13B Command-Line Tools","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nYou can execute any command from the Run dialog, but for a series of commands or viewing output, use the command shell. The command prompt processes legacy commands from early Windows versions.","context":"You can execute any command from the Run dialog, but for a series of commands or viewing output, use the command shell. The command prompt processes legacy commands from early Windows versions.","options":["Command Prompt","Disk Management Commands","Changing the Current Drive","Disk Management Console"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Command Prompt: You can execute any command from the Run dialog, but for a series of commands or viewing output, use the command shell. The command prompt processes legacy commands from early Windows versions."},{"id":"c2-0088","module":13,"lesson":"13C Windows Networking","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nSome commands require elevated privileges. If a command needs elevation, you'll see \"The requested operation requires elevation.\" To run as administrator, right-click the Command Prompt shortcut, select \"Run as administrator,\" and confirm the UAC prompt. Alternatively,","context":"Some commands require elevated privileges. If a command needs elevation, you'll see \"The requested operation requires elevation.\" To run as administrator, right-click the Command Prompt shortcut, select \"Run as administrator,\" and confirm the UAC prompt. Alternatively,","options":["File Management Commands","Administrative Command Prompt","The diskpart Command","Disk Maintenance Tools"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Administrative Command Prompt: Some commands require elevated privileges. If a command needs elevation, you'll see \"The requested operation requires elevation.\" To run as administrator, right-click the Command Prompt shortcut, select \"Run as administrator,\" and confirm the UAC prompt. Alternatively,"},{"id":"c2-0089","module":13,"lesson":"13A Management Consoles","question":"What is being described here?\n\nEnter commands at the prompt (>) using the command name, switches, and arguments with proper syntax. Press ENTER to execute the command. Required and optional arguments and switches are listed in the command syntax. Switches are usually preceded by a forward slash. If","context":"Enter commands at the prompt (>) using the command name, switches, and arguments with proper syntax. Press ENTER to execute the command. Required and optional arguments and switches are listed in the command syntax. Switches are usually preceded by a forward slash. If","options":["The format Command","Disk Defragmenter","Command Syntax","Copying Directory Structures"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Command Syntax: Enter commands at the prompt (>) using the command name, switches, and arguments with proper syntax. Press ENTER to execute the command. Required and optional arguments and switches are listed in the command syntax. Switches are usually preceded by a forward slash. If"},{"id":"c2-0090","module":13,"lesson":"13B Command-Line Tools","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nThe command prompt includes a basic help system. Type help and then press ENTER at the command prompt to see available commands or type help Command for syntax and switches used for the command. Use the /? switch for command-specific help, e.g., netstat /? displays","context":"The command prompt includes a basic help system. Type help and then press ENTER at the command prompt to see available commands or type help Command for syntax and switches used for the command. Use the /? switch for command-specific help, e.g., netstat /? displays","options":["Getting Help","The chkdsk Command","The command includes various switches for enhanced functionality:","Disk Clean-up"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Getting Help: The command prompt includes a basic help system. Type help and then press ENTER at the command prompt to see available commands or type help Command for syntax and switches used for the command. Use the /? switch for command-specific help, e.g., netstat /? displays"},{"id":"c2-0091","module":13,"lesson":"13C Windows Networking","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nThe string before the > in the command prompt indicates the current working directory.","context":"The string before the > in the command prompt indicates the current working directory.","options":["Task Scheduler","Creating a Directory","There are three ways to use chkdsk :","Navigation Commands"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Navigation Commands: The string before the > in the command prompt indicates the current working directory."},{"id":"c2-0092","module":13,"lesson":"13A Management Consoles","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nUse the dir command to list files and subdirectories in the current directory or a specified path.","context":"Use the dir command to list files and subdirectories in the current directory or a specified path.","options":["Local Users and Groups Console","Removing a Directory","Device Manager","Listing Files and Directories"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Listing Files and Directories: Use the dir command to list files and subdirectories in the current directory or a specified path."},{"id":"c2-0093","module":13,"lesson":"13B Command-Line Tools","question":"What is being described here?\n\nUse the cd command to change the working directory. Enter the full path to switch directories, such as cd C:\\Users\\David .","context":"Use the cd command to change the working directory. Enter the full path to switch directories, such as cd C:\\Users\\David .","options":["Certificate Manager","Updating and Troubleshooting Devices","Disk Management Commands","Changing the Current Directory"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Changing the Current Directory: Use the cd command to change the working directory. Enter the full path to switch directories, such as cd C:\\Users\\David ."},{"id":"c2-0094","module":13,"lesson":"13C Windows Networking","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\n• To move from C:\\Users\\David to C:\\Users\\David\\Documents, type: cd Documents • To move up to the parent directory from C:\\Users\\David\\Documents, type: cd ..","context":"• To move from C:\\Users\\David to C:\\Users\\David\\Documents, type: cd Documents • To move up to the parent directory from C:\\Users\\David\\Documents, type: cd ..","options":["Removing, Uninstalling, and Disabling Devices","The diskpart Command","Key subfolders include:","Here are some shortcuts:"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Here are some shortcuts:: • To move from C:\\Users\\David to C:\\Users\\David\\Documents, type: cd Documents • To move up to the parent directory from C:\\Users\\David\\Documents, type: cd .."},{"id":"c2-0095","module":13,"lesson":"13A Management Consoles","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nThe active, or in focus, drive is managed separately from the directory. To switch drives, enter the drive letter followed by a colon and press ENTER. For example, typing D: switches to the D drive, and the prompt will update to D:\\ , indicating that D is now the default drive.","context":"The active, or in focus, drive is managed separately from the directory. To switch drives, enter the drive letter followed by a colon and press ENTER. For example, typing D: switches to the D drive, and the prompt will update to D:\\ , indicating that D is now the default drive.","options":["Group Policy Editor","The format Command","Disk Management Console","Changing the Current Drive"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Changing the Current Drive: The active, or in focus, drive is managed separately from the directory. To switch drives, enter the drive letter followed by a colon and press ENTER. For example, typing D: switches to the D drive, and the prompt will update to D:\\ , indicating that D is now the default drive."},{"id":"c2-0096","module":13,"lesson":"13B Command-Line Tools","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nThe move command and copy command allow you to transfer files within a single directory.","context":"The move command and copy command allow you to transfer files within a single directory.","options":["File Management Commands","The chkdsk Command","Registry Editor","Disk Maintenance Tools"],"answer":0,"explanation":"File Management Commands: The move command and copy command allow you to transfer files within a single directory."},{"id":"c2-0097","module":13,"lesson":"13C Windows Networking","question":"What is being described here?\n\nThe robocopy command, or \"robust copy,\" is another file copy utility. Microsoft recommends using robocopy over xcopy for better handling of long file names and NTFS attributes.","context":"The robocopy command, or \"robust copy,\" is another file copy utility. Microsoft recommends using robocopy over xcopy for better handling of long file names and NTFS attributes.","options":["Disk Defragmenter","Registry Keys","There are three ways to use chkdsk :","Copying Directory Structures"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Copying Directory Structures: The robocopy command, or \"robust copy,\" is another file copy utility. Microsoft recommends using robocopy over xcopy for better handling of long file names and NTFS attributes."},{"id":"c2-0098","module":13,"lesson":"13A Management Consoles","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\n• /xf: Excludes files that match the specified names or paths.","context":"• /xf: Excludes files that match the specified names or paths.","options":["The command includes various switches for enhanced functionality:","Editing the Registry","Disk Clean-up","Device Manager"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The command includes various switches for enhanced functionality:: • /xf: Excludes files that match the specified names or paths."},{"id":"c2-0099","module":13,"lesson":"13B Command-Line Tools","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nTo create a directory, use the md or mkdir command. For example, type md Data to create a directory named Data in the current directory. To create a directory named Docs within a directory called Data on the A drive, while the current path is C:, type md A:\\Data\\Docs .","context":"To create a directory, use the md or mkdir command. For example, type md Data to create a directory named Data in the current directory. To create a directory named Docs within a directory called Data on the A drive, while the current path is C:, type md A:\\Data\\Docs .","options":["Custom Microsoft Management Consoles","Task Scheduler","Updating and Troubleshooting Devices","Creating a Directory"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Creating a Directory: To create a directory, use the md or mkdir command. For example, type md Data to create a directory named Data in the current directory. To create a directory named Docs within a directory called Data on the A drive, while the current path is C:, type md A:\\Data\\Docs ."},{"id":"c2-0100","module":13,"lesson":"13C Windows Networking","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nTo delete an empty directory, use rd Directory or rmdir Directory. If the directory contains files or subdirectories, use the /s switch to remove them. The /q switch can be used to suppress confirmation messages for quiet mode.","context":"To delete an empty directory, use rd Directory or rmdir Directory. If the directory contains files or subdirectories, use the /s switch to remove them. The /q switch can be used to suppress confirmation messages for quiet mode.","options":["Command Prompt","Removing, Uninstalling, and Disabling Devices","Local Users and Groups Console","Removing a Directory"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Removing a Directory: To delete an empty directory, use rd Directory or rmdir Directory. If the directory contains files or subdirectories, use the /s switch to remove them. The /q switch can be used to suppress confirmation messages for quiet mode."},{"id":"c2-0101","module":13,"lesson":"13A Management Consoles","question":"What is being described here?\n\nThe Disk Management snap-in is easy to use, but there are some circumstances where you may need to manage volumes at a command prompt.","context":"The Disk Management snap-in is easy to use, but there are some circumstances where you may need to manage volumes at a command prompt.","options":["Disk Management Console","Administrative Command Prompt","Disk Management Commands","Certificate Manager"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Disk Management Commands: The Disk Management snap-in is easy to use, but there are some circumstances where you may need to manage volumes at a command prompt."},{"id":"c2-0102","module":13,"lesson":"13B Command-Line Tools","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nThe diskpart command serves as the command-line interface for the Disk Management tool.","context":"The diskpart command serves as the command-line interface for the Disk Management tool.","options":["Disk Maintenance Tools","The diskpart Command","Key subfolders include:","Command Syntax"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The diskpart Command: The diskpart command serves as the command-line interface for the Disk Management tool."},{"id":"c2-0103","module":13,"lesson":"13C Windows Networking","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nThe format command creates a new file system on a drive, deleting all existing data. The basic command is format drive navigation input X: /fs:SYS, where X is the drive letter and SYS is the file system type (e.g., NTFS, FAT32, EXFAT). By default, it scans for bad sectors, which can be","context":"The format command creates a new file system on a drive, deleting all existing data. The basic command is format drive navigation input X: /fs:SYS, where X is the drive letter and SYS is the file system type (e.g., NTFS, FAT32, EXFAT). By default, it scans for bad sectors, which can be","options":["Getting Help","The format Command","Disk Defragmenter","Group Policy Editor"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The format Command: The format command creates a new file system on a drive, deleting all existing data. The basic command is format drive navigation input X: /fs:SYS, where X is the drive letter and SYS is the file system type (e.g., NTFS, FAT32, EXFAT). By default, it scans for bad sectors, which can be"},{"id":"c2-0104","module":13,"lesson":"13A Management Consoles","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nThe chkdsk command scans the file system and disk sectors for faults and attempts to repair detected problems. A version called autochk runs automatically if file system errors are detected at boot.","context":"The chkdsk command scans the file system and disk sectors for faults and attempts to repair detected problems. A version called autochk runs automatically if file system errors are detected at boot.","options":["Disk Clean-up","The chkdsk Command","Registry Editor","Navigation Commands"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The chkdsk Command: The chkdsk command scans the file system and disk sectors for faults and attempts to repair detected problems. A version called autochk runs automatically if file system errors are detected at boot."},{"id":"c2-0105","module":13,"lesson":"13B Command-Line Tools","question":"What is being described here?\n\n• chkdsk X : (where X is the drive letter but no switch is used) runs in read-only mode, reporting any errors that need repair.","context":"• chkdsk X : (where X is the drive letter but no switch is used) runs in read-only mode, reporting any errors that need repair.","options":["Listing Files and Directories","Task Scheduler","There are three ways to use chkdsk :","Registry Keys"],"answer":2,"explanation":"There are three ways to use chkdsk :: • chkdsk X : (where X is the drive letter but no switch is used) runs in read-only mode, reporting any errors that need repair."},{"id":"c2-0106","module":14,"lesson":"14A Network Troubleshooting","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nWindows can report several types of error states for a local network adapter. If the connection is reported as unplugged or disconnected, you need to check the cable or wireless network configuration. Two other states are reported if the link is available, but IP is not correctly","context":"Windows can report several types of error states for a local network adapter. If the connection is reported as unplugged or disconnected, you need to check the cable or wireless network configuration. Two other states are reported if the link is available, but IP is not correctly","options":["Default Log Files","Troubleshoot IP Configuration","Virtual Network Computing","Other Remote Access Tools"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Troubleshoot IP Configuration: Windows can report several types of error states for a local network adapter. If the connection is reported as unplugged or disconnected, you need to check the cable or wireless network configuration. Two other states are reported if the link is available, but IP is not correctly"},{"id":"c2-0107","module":14,"lesson":"14B Remote Access","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nIf there are persistent network problems with either a client or a server, one \"stock\" response is to try restarting the computer hardware. You can also try restarting just the application service.","context":"If there are persistent network problems with either a client or a server, one \"stock\" response is to try restarting the computer hardware. You can also try restarting just the application service.","options":["RDP Server and Security Settings","The Windows Logs folder contains four main log files:","Network Reset","Screen-sharing Software"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Network Reset: If there are persistent network problems with either a client or a server, one \"stock\" response is to try restarting the computer hardware. You can also try restarting just the application service."},{"id":"c2-0108","module":14,"lesson":"14C Performance Tools","question":"What is being described here?\n\nIf the link and IP configuration both seem to be correct, the problem may not lie with the local machine but somewhere in the overall network topology. You can test connections to servers such as file shares, printers, or email by trying to use them. One drawback of this method is that","context":"If the link and IP configuration both seem to be correct, the problem may not lie with the local machine but somewhere in the overall network topology. You can test connections to servers such as file shares, printers, or email by trying to use them. One drawback of this method is that","options":["Video-conferencing Software","Event Sources and Severity Levels","Troubleshoot Local Network Connectivity","Microsoft Remote Assistance"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Troubleshoot Local Network Connectivity: If the link and IP configuration both seem to be correct, the problem may not lie with the local machine but somewhere in the overall network topology. You can test connections to servers such as file shares, printers, or email by trying to use them. One drawback of this method is that"},{"id":"c2-0109","module":14,"lesson":"14D OS Troubleshooting","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nWhen a packet is forwarded to a remote network, each router in the path to the network counts as one hop. The path taken by a packet can be used to diagnose routing issues. The tracert command (pronounced trace route) line utility is used to trace the path a packet of information","context":"When a packet is forwarded to a remote network, each router in the path to the network counts as one hop. The path taken by a packet can be used to diagnose routing issues. The tracert command (pronounced trace route) line utility is used to trace the path a packet of information","options":["File Transfer Software","Task Manager Process Monitoring","Secure Shell","Troubleshoot Remote Network Connectivity"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Troubleshoot Remote Network Connectivity: When a packet is forwarded to a remote network, each router in the path to the network counts as one hop. The path taken by a packet can be used to diagnose routing issues. The tracert command (pronounced trace route) line utility is used to trace the path a packet of information"},{"id":"c2-0110","module":14,"lesson":"14A Network Troubleshooting","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nIf you cannot identify a problem with basic connectivity, you should start to suspect a problem at a higher layer of processing. There are three main additional \"layers\" where network services fail: • Security- A firewall or other security software or hardware might be blocking the connection","context":"If you cannot identify a problem with basic connectivity, you should start to suspect a problem at a higher layer of processing. There are three main additional \"layers\" where network services fail: • Security- A firewall or other security software or hardware might be blocking the connection","options":["OS vendors have developed other types of file transfer software:","Remote Monitoring and Desktop Management Tools","Task Manager Performance Monitoring","Troubleshoot Name Resolution"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Troubleshoot Name Resolution: If you cannot identify a problem with basic connectivity, you should start to suspect a problem at a higher layer of processing. There are three main additional \"layers\" where network services fail: • Security- A firewall or other security software or hardware might be blocking the connection"},{"id":"c2-0111","module":14,"lesson":"14B Remote Access","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nThe netstat command can be used to investigate open ports and connections on the local host.","context":"The netstat command can be used to investigate open ports and connections on the local host.","options":["Disk Monitoring","Simple Protocol for Independent Computing Environments (SPICE)","Troubleshoot Network Ports","Virtual Private Networks"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Troubleshoot Network Ports: The netstat command can be used to investigate open ports and connections on the local host."},{"id":"c2-0112","module":14,"lesson":"14C Performance Tools","question":"What is being described here?\n\nWith remote desktop, the target PC runs a graphical terminal server to accept connections from clients. This allows a user to work at the desktop of a different computer over the network.","context":"With remote desktop, the target PC runs a graphical terminal server to accept connections from clients. This allows a user to work at the desktop of a different computer over the network.","options":["Remote Desktop Tools","Other Remote Access Tools","System Information","Network Monitoring"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Remote Desktop Tools: With remote desktop, the target PC runs a graphical terminal server to accept connections from clients. This allows a user to work at the desktop of a different computer over the network."},{"id":"c2-0113","module":14,"lesson":"14D OS Troubleshooting","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nWindows uses the Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP) to implement terminal server and client functionality. To connect to a server via Remote Desktop, open the Remote Desktop Connection shortcut or run mstsc.exe . Enter the server's IP address or fully qualified domain name","context":"Windows uses the Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP) to implement terminal server and client functionality. To connect to a server via Remote Desktop, open the Remote Desktop Connection shortcut or run mstsc.exe . Enter the server's IP address or fully qualified domain name","options":["Screen-sharing Software","CPU and GPU Monitoring","Remote Desktop Protocol","Event Viewer"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Remote Desktop Protocol: Windows uses the Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP) to implement terminal server and client functionality. To connect to a server via Remote Desktop, open the Remote Desktop Connection shortcut or run mstsc.exe . Enter the server's IP address or fully qualified domain name"},{"id":"c2-0114","module":14,"lesson":"14A Network Troubleshooting","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nThere are alternatives to using RDP for remote access. For example, in macOS, you can use the Screen Sharing feature for remote desktop functionality. Screen Sharing is based on the Virtual Network Computing (VNC) protocol. You can use any VNC client to connect to a Screen Sharing","context":"There are alternatives to using RDP for remote access. For example, in macOS, you can use the Screen Sharing feature for remote desktop functionality. Screen Sharing is based on the Virtual Network Computing (VNC) protocol. You can use any VNC client to connect to a Screen Sharing","options":["Video-conferencing Software","Memory Monitoring","Default Log Files","Virtual Network Computing"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Virtual Network Computing: There are alternatives to using RDP for remote access. For example, in macOS, you can use the Screen Sharing feature for remote desktop functionality. Screen Sharing is based on the Virtual Network Computing (VNC) protocol. You can use any VNC client to connect to a Screen Sharing"},{"id":"c2-0115","module":14,"lesson":"14B Remote Access","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nA Remote Desktop server is not enabled by default. To change remote access settings, open the Remote Desktop page in the Settings app.","context":"A Remote Desktop server is not enabled by default. To change remote access settings, open the Remote Desktop page in the Settings app.","options":["Disk and Network Monitoring","The Windows Logs folder contains four main log files:","File Transfer Software","RDP Server and Security Settings"],"answer":3,"explanation":"RDP Server and Security Settings: A Remote Desktop server is not enabled by default. To change remote access settings, open the Remote Desktop page in the Settings app."},{"id":"c2-0116","module":14,"lesson":"14C Performance Tools","question":"What is being described here?\n\nRemote Assistance allows a user to ask for help from a technician or co-worker via an invitation file protected by a passcode. The helper can open the file to connect over RDP and join the session with the user. There is a chat feature, and the helper can request control of the desktop.","context":"Remote Assistance allows a user to ask for help from a technician or co-worker via an invitation file protected by a passcode. The helper can open the file to connect over RDP and join the session with the user. There is a chat feature, and the helper can request control of the desktop.","options":["Task Manager User Monitoring","Event Sources and Severity Levels","Microsoft Remote Assistance","OS vendors have developed other types of file transfer software:"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Microsoft Remote Assistance: Remote Assistance allows a user to ask for help from a technician or co-worker via an invitation file protected by a passcode. The helper can open the file to connect over RDP and join the session with the user. There is a chat feature, and the helper can request control of the desktop."},{"id":"c2-0117","module":14,"lesson":"14D OS Troubleshooting","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nSecure shell is also a remote access protocol, but it connects to a command interpreter rather than a desktop window manager. SSH uses TCP port 22 (by default). SSH uses encryption to protect each session. There are numerous commercial and open-source SSH products available","context":"Secure shell is also a remote access protocol, but it connects to a command interpreter rather than a desktop window manager. SSH uses TCP port 22 (by default). SSH uses encryption to protect each session. There are numerous commercial and open-source SSH products available","options":["Task Manager Process Monitoring","Secure Shell","The Users tab in Task Manager lets you:","Virtual Private Networks"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Secure Shell: Secure shell is also a remote access protocol, but it connects to a command interpreter rather than a desktop window manager. SSH uses TCP port 22 (by default). SSH uses encryption to protect each session. There are numerous commercial and open-source SSH products available"},{"id":"c2-0118","module":14,"lesson":"14A Network Troubleshooting","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nNetwork visibility refers to the challenge of ensuring that every host communicating on the network is authorized to be there and is running in a secure configuration. It is impractical for a technician to regularly locate and visit each device, so visibility depends on remote monitoring","context":"Network visibility refers to the challenge of ensuring that every host communicating on the network is authorized to be there and is running in a secure configuration. It is impractical for a technician to regularly locate and visit each device, so visibility depends on remote monitoring","options":["Remote Monitoring and Desktop Management Tools","System Information","Startup Processes and Services Console","Task Manager Performance Monitoring"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Remote Monitoring and Desktop Management Tools: Network visibility refers to the challenge of ensuring that every host communicating on the network is authorized to be there and is running in a secure configuration. It is impractical for a technician to regularly locate and visit each device, so visibility depends on remote monitoring"},{"id":"c2-0119","module":14,"lesson":"14B Remote Access","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nThe Simple Protocol for Independent Computing Environments provides a remote display system to monitor and interact with virtual machine environments from across the Internet.","context":"The Simple Protocol for Independent Computing Environments provides a remote display system to monitor and interact with virtual machine environments from across the Internet.","options":["Disk Monitoring","Event Viewer","Simple Protocol for Independent Computing Environments (SPICE)","Troubleshoot IP Configuration"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Simple Protocol for Independent Computing Environments (SPICE): The Simple Protocol for Independent Computing Environments provides a remote display system to monitor and interact with virtual machine environments from across the Internet."},{"id":"c2-0120","module":14,"lesson":"14C Performance Tools","question":"What is being described here?\n\nEnterprise monitoring suites are designed for environments with large numbers of desktops, and the cost can be prohibitive when managing just a few machines. Other protocols and software tools are available for accepting incoming connections to non-Windows devices and","context":"Enterprise monitoring suites are designed for environments with large numbers of desktops, and the cost can be prohibitive when managing just a few machines. Other protocols and software tools are available for accepting incoming connections to non-Windows devices and","options":["Network Reset","Network Monitoring","Default Log Files","Other Remote Access Tools"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Other Remote Access Tools: Enterprise monitoring suites are designed for environments with large numbers of desktops, and the cost can be prohibitive when managing just a few machines. Other protocols and software tools are available for accepting incoming connections to non-Windows devices and"},{"id":"c2-0121","module":14,"lesson":"14D OS Troubleshooting","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nThere are many third-party alternatives to the sort of screen sharing and remote-control functionality implemented by MSRA/Quick Assist. Examples include TeamViewer and LogMeIn.","context":"There are many third-party alternatives to the sort of screen sharing and remote-control functionality implemented by MSRA/Quick Assist. Examples include TeamViewer and LogMeIn.","options":["CPU and GPU Monitoring","The Windows Logs folder contains four main log files:","Screen-sharing Software","Troubleshoot Local Network Connectivity"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Screen-sharing Software: There are many third-party alternatives to the sort of screen sharing and remote-control functionality implemented by MSRA/Quick Assist. Examples include TeamViewer and LogMeIn."},{"id":"c2-0122","module":14,"lesson":"14A Network Troubleshooting","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nMost video conferencing or web-conferencing software, such as Microsoft Teams or Zoom, includes a screen-share client, and some also allow participants to be granted control of the share. The share can be configured as a single window or the whole desktop. The share","context":"Most video conferencing or web-conferencing software, such as Microsoft Teams or Zoom, includes a screen-share client, and some also allow participants to be granted control of the share. The share can be configured as a single window or the whole desktop. The share","options":["Video-conferencing Software","Troubleshoot Remote Network Connectivity","Memory Monitoring","Event Sources and Severity Levels"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Video-conferencing Software: Most video conferencing or web-conferencing software, such as Microsoft Teams or Zoom, includes a screen-share client, and some also allow participants to be granted control of the share. The share can be configured as a single window or the whole desktop. The share"},{"id":"c2-0123","module":14,"lesson":"14B Remote Access","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nSetting up a network file share can be relatively complex. You need to select a file-sharing protocol that all the connecting hosts can use and that allows configuring permissions on the share and provisioning user accounts that both the server and client recognize. Consequently,","context":"Setting up a network file share can be relatively complex. You need to select a file-sharing protocol that all the connecting hosts can use and that allows configuring permissions on the share and provisioning user accounts that both the server and client recognize. Consequently,","options":["Troubleshoot Name Resolution","Disk and Network Monitoring","File Transfer Software","Task Manager Process Monitoring"],"answer":2,"explanation":"File Transfer Software: Setting up a network file share can be relatively complex. You need to select a file-sharing protocol that all the connecting hosts can use and that allows configuring permissions on the share and provisioning user accounts that both the server and client recognize. Consequently,"},{"id":"c2-0124","module":14,"lesson":"14C Performance Tools","question":"What is being described here?\n\n• AirDrop—Supported by Apple iOS and macOS, this uses Bluetooth to establish a Wi-Fi Direct connection between the devices for the duration of the file transfer. The connection is secured by the Bluetooth pairing mechanism and Wi-Fi encryption.","context":"• AirDrop—Supported by Apple iOS and macOS, this uses Bluetooth to establish a Wi-Fi Direct connection between the devices for the duration of the file transfer. The connection is secured by the Bluetooth pairing mechanism and Wi-Fi encryption.","options":["OS vendors have developed other types of file transfer software:","Task Manager User Monitoring","Troubleshoot Network Ports","Task Manager Performance Monitoring"],"answer":0,"explanation":"OS vendors have developed other types of file transfer software:: • AirDrop—Supported by Apple iOS and macOS, this uses Bluetooth to establish a Wi-Fi Direct connection between the devices for the duration of the file transfer. The connection is secured by the Bluetooth pairing mechanism and Wi-Fi encryption."},{"id":"c2-0125","module":14,"lesson":"14D OS Troubleshooting","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nWhere remote desktop or SSH establishes a connection to a single host over the network, a virtual private network (VPN) establishes a tunneled link that joins your local computer to a remote network. The VPN could be used as an additional layer of security. For example, you","context":"Where remote desktop or SSH establishes a connection to a single host over the network, a virtual private network (VPN) establishes a tunneled link that joins your local computer to a remote network. The VPN could be used as an additional layer of security. For example, you","options":["Virtual Private Networks","The Users tab in Task Manager lets you:","Disk Monitoring","Remote Desktop Tools"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Virtual Private Networks: Where remote desktop or SSH establishes a connection to a single host over the network, a virtual private network (VPN) establishes a tunneled link that joins your local computer to a remote network. The VPN could be used as an additional layer of security. For example, you"},{"id":"c2-0126","module":14,"lesson":"14A Network Troubleshooting","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nThe System Information tool (msinfo32.exe) generates a comprehensive report on the system's hardware and software components. It provides an inventory of system resources, firmware and OS versions, driver file locations, environment variables, and network status.","context":"The System Information tool (msinfo32.exe) generates a comprehensive report on the system's hardware and software components. It provides an inventory of system resources, firmware and OS versions, driver file locations, environment variables, and network status.","options":["Startup Processes and Services Console","System Information","Remote Desktop Protocol","Network Monitoring"],"answer":1,"explanation":"System Information: The System Information tool (msinfo32.exe) generates a comprehensive report on the system's hardware and software components. It provides an inventory of system resources, firmware and OS versions, driver file locations, environment variables, and network status."},{"id":"c2-0127","module":14,"lesson":"14B Remote Access","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nWhen Windows detects a problem, it generates an error message to aid troubleshooting. You can look up these messages using the Microsoft Knowledge Base (support.microsoft.com) or third-party support sites.","context":"When Windows detects a problem, it generates an error message to aid troubleshooting. You can look up these messages using the Microsoft Knowledge Base (support.microsoft.com) or third-party support sites.","options":["Event Viewer","CPU and GPU Monitoring","Troubleshoot IP Configuration","Virtual Network Computing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Event Viewer: When Windows detects a problem, it generates an error message to aid troubleshooting. You can look up these messages using the Microsoft Knowledge Base (support.microsoft.com) or third-party support sites."},{"id":"c2-0128","module":14,"lesson":"14C Performance Tools","question":"What is being described here?\n\nThe Windows Logs folder contains four main log files: • System Log: Records events affecting the core OS, such as service load failures, hardware conflicts, driver load failures, and network issues.","context":"The Windows Logs folder contains four main log files: • System Log: Records events affecting the core OS, such as service load failures, hardware conflicts, driver load failures, and network issues.","options":["Network Reset","RDP Server and Security Settings","Default Log Files","Memory Monitoring"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Default Log Files: The Windows Logs folder contains four main log files: • System Log: Records events affecting the core OS, such as service load failures, hardware conflicts, driver load failures, and network issues."},{"id":"c2-0129","module":14,"lesson":"14D OS Troubleshooting","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\n• System Log: Records events affecting the core OS, such as service load failures, hardware conflicts, driver load failures, and network issues.","context":"• System Log: Records events affecting the core OS, such as service load failures, hardware conflicts, driver load failures, and network issues.","options":["Troubleshoot Local Network Connectivity","The Windows Logs folder contains four main log files:","Microsoft Remote Assistance","Disk and Network Monitoring"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Windows Logs folder contains four main log files:: • System Log: Records events affecting the core OS, such as service load failures, hardware conflicts, driver load failures, and network issues."},{"id":"c2-0130","module":14,"lesson":"14A Network Troubleshooting","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nEach event is generated by a source application and assigned an ID and severity level: The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 14: Supporting Windows • Critical: Highest priority issues, often indicating a halted or unresponsive process.","context":"Each event is generated by a source application and assigned an ID and severity level: The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 14: Supporting Windows • Critical: Highest priority issues, often indicating a halted or unresponsive process.","options":["Secure Shell","Task Manager User Monitoring","Troubleshoot Remote Network Connectivity","Event Sources and Severity Levels"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Event Sources and Severity Levels: Each event is generated by a source application and assigned an ID and severity level: The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 14: Supporting Windows • Critical: Highest priority issues, often indicating a halted or unresponsive process."},{"id":"c2-0131","module":14,"lesson":"14B Remote Access","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nTask Manager monitors your PC's key resources. Open it by pressing CTRL+SHIFT+ESC, right- clicking the taskbar or Start, or pressing CTRL+ALT+DEL and selecting Task Manager.","context":"Task Manager monitors your PC's key resources. Open it by pressing CTRL+SHIFT+ESC, right- clicking the taskbar or Start, or pressing CTRL+ALT+DEL and selecting Task Manager.","options":["Remote Monitoring and Desktop Management Tools","The Users tab in Task Manager lets you:","Troubleshoot Name Resolution","Task Manager Process Monitoring"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Task Manager Process Monitoring: Task Manager monitors your PC's key resources. Open it by pressing CTRL+SHIFT+ESC, right- clicking the taskbar or Start, or pressing CTRL+ALT+DEL and selecting Task Manager."},{"id":"c2-0132","module":14,"lesson":"14C Performance Tools","question":"What is being described here?\n\nThe Performance tab offers detailed information about the CPU, memory, disk, network, and graphics processing unit (GPU) subsystems. The App History tab displays usage information for Windows Store apps.","context":"The Performance tab offers detailed information about the CPU, memory, disk, network, and graphics processing unit (GPU) subsystems. The App History tab displays usage information for Windows Store apps.","options":["Task Manager Performance Monitoring","Troubleshoot Network Ports","Simple Protocol for Independent Computing Environments (SPICE)","Startup Processes and Services Console"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Task Manager Performance Monitoring: The Performance tab offers detailed information about the CPU, memory, disk, network, and graphics processing unit (GPU) subsystems. The App History tab displays usage information for Windows Store apps."},{"id":"c2-0133","module":14,"lesson":"14D OS Troubleshooting","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nThe Disk pages display type, capacity, and statistics for active time, response time, and read/ write speeds.","context":"The Disk pages display type, capacity, and statistics for active time, response time, and read/ write speeds.","options":["Other Remote Access Tools","Disk Monitoring","Troubleshoot IP Configuration","Remote Desktop Tools"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Disk Monitoring: The Disk pages display type, capacity, and statistics for active time, response time, and read/ write speeds."},{"id":"c2-0134","module":14,"lesson":"14A Network Troubleshooting","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nThe Ethernet or Wi-Fi tab displays send and receive throughput for the active network adapter, along with the IP address and MAC address. For active wireless adapters, it also shows the SSID, connection type (802.11 standard), and signal strength.","context":"The Ethernet or Wi-Fi tab displays send and receive throughput for the active network adapter, along with the IP address and MAC address. For active wireless adapters, it also shows the SSID, connection type (802.11 standard), and signal strength.","options":["Network Reset","Network Monitoring","Remote Desktop Protocol","Screen-sharing Software"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Network Monitoring: The Ethernet or Wi-Fi tab displays send and receive throughput for the active network adapter, along with the IP address and MAC address. For active wireless adapters, it also shows the SSID, connection type (802.11 standard), and signal strength."},{"id":"c2-0135","module":14,"lesson":"14B Remote Access","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nThe CPU page shows the number of cores and logical processors (HyperThreading), whether the system is multisocket, and whether virtualization is enabled. The statistics show overall utilization, system uptime, and a count of the number of processes, threads, and handles.","context":"The CPU page shows the number of cores and logical processors (HyperThreading), whether the system is multisocket, and whether virtualization is enabled. The statistics show overall utilization, system uptime, and a count of the number of processes, threads, and handles.","options":["Troubleshoot Local Network Connectivity","Video-conferencing Software","Virtual Network Computing","CPU and GPU Monitoring"],"answer":3,"explanation":"CPU and GPU Monitoring: The CPU page shows the number of cores and logical processors (HyperThreading), whether the system is multisocket, and whether virtualization is enabled. The statistics show overall utilization, system uptime, and a count of the number of processes, threads, and handles."},{"id":"c2-0136","module":14,"lesson":"14C Performance Tools","question":"What is being described here?\n\nThe Memory page reports which slots have modules installed and the speed. The usage statistics are broken down as follows: • In use refers to system (RAM) usage only.","context":"The Memory page reports which slots have modules installed and the speed. The usage statistics are broken down as follows: • In use refers to system (RAM) usage only.","options":["File Transfer Software","Memory Monitoring","Troubleshoot Remote Network Connectivity","RDP Server and Security Settings"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Memory Monitoring: The Memory page reports which slots have modules installed and the speed. The usage statistics are broken down as follows: • In use refers to system (RAM) usage only."},{"id":"c2-0137","module":14,"lesson":"14D OS Troubleshooting","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nDisk Monitoring The Disk pages report the type and capacity plus statistics for active time, response time, and read/write speeds.","context":"Disk Monitoring The Disk pages report the type and capacity plus statistics for active time, response time, and read/write speeds.","options":["Troubleshoot Name Resolution","Microsoft Remote Assistance","OS vendors have developed other types of file transfer software:","Disk and Network Monitoring"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Disk and Network Monitoring: Disk Monitoring The Disk pages report the type and capacity plus statistics for active time, response time, and read/write speeds."},{"id":"c2-0138","module":14,"lesson":"14A Network Troubleshooting","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nThe Users tab in Task Manager lets you: • View all logged-in users.","context":"The Users tab in Task Manager lets you: • View all logged-in users.","options":["Virtual Private Networks","Secure Shell","Task Manager User Monitoring","Troubleshoot Network Ports"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Task Manager User Monitoring: The Users tab in Task Manager lets you: • View all logged-in users."},{"id":"c2-0139","module":14,"lesson":"14B Remote Access","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\n• View all logged-in users. • Send messages to users.","context":"• View all logged-in users. • Send messages to users.","options":["Remote Monitoring and Desktop Management Tools","Remote Desktop Tools","The Users tab in Task Manager lets you:","System Information"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The Users tab in Task Manager lets you:: • View all logged-in users. • Send messages to users."},{"id":"c2-0140","module":14,"lesson":"14C Performance Tools","question":"What is being described here?\n\nThe Startup tab allows you to disable programs set to run at startup from the Startup folder or registry. Right-click the headers to view the startup type and each item's impact on boot times.","context":"The Startup tab allows you to disable programs set to run at startup from the Startup folder or registry. Right-click the headers to view the startup type and each item's impact on boot times.","options":["Simple Protocol for Independent Computing Environments (SPICE)","Event Viewer","Remote Desktop Protocol","Startup Processes and Services Console"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Startup Processes and Services Console: The Startup tab allows you to disable programs set to run at startup from the Startup folder or registry. Right-click the headers to view the startup type and each item's impact on boot times."},{"id":"c2-0141","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nA security control is a safeguard or prevention method to avoid, counteract, or minimize risks relating to personal or company property. For example, a firewall is a type of security control because it controls network communications by allowing only traffic that has specifically been","context":"A security control is a safeguard or prevention method to avoid, counteract, or minimize risks relating to personal or company property. For example, a firewall is a type of security control because it controls network communications by allowing only traffic that has specifically been","options":["Logical Security Controls","It works on the following lines:","User and Group Accounts","Symmetric Encryption"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Logical Security Controls: A security control is a safeguard or prevention method to avoid, counteract, or minimize risks relating to personal or company property. For example, a firewall is a type of security control because it controls network communications by allowing only traffic that has specifically been"},{"id":"c2-0142","module":15,"lesson":"15B Windows Security Settings","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\n• Confidentiality ensures that sensitive data is only accessible by authorized users.","context":"• Confidentiality ensures that sensitive data is only accessible by authorized users.","options":["Confidentiality, Integrity, and Availability:","One-Time Passwords","Asymmetric Encryption","Security Groups"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Confidentiality, Integrity, and Availability:: • Confidentiality ensures that sensitive data is only accessible by authorized users."},{"id":"c2-0143","module":15,"lesson":"15C Windows Shares","question":"What is being described here?\n\nA permission is a security setting that determines the level of access an account has to a particular resource. A permission is usually implemented as an access control list attached to each resource. Within an ACL, each access control entry (ACE) identifies a subject and the","context":"A permission is a security setting that determines the level of access an account has to a particular resource. A permission is usually implemented as an access control list attached to each resource. Within an ACL, each access control entry (ACE) identifies a subject and the","options":["There are a few types of one-time passwords including:","Local Users and Groups","Access Control Lists","Cryptographic Hashes"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Access Control Lists: A permission is a security setting that determines the level of access an account has to a particular resource. A permission is usually implemented as an access control list attached to each resource. Within an ACL, each access control entry (ACE) identifies a subject and the"},{"id":"c2-0144","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nACL security is typically founded on the principle of implicit deny. implicit deny means that unless there is a rule specifying that access should be granted, any request for access is denied. This principle can be seen clearly in firewall policies. A firewall filters access requests","context":"ACL security is typically founded on the principle of implicit deny. implicit deny means that unless there is a rule specifying that access should be granted, any request for access is denied. This principle can be seen clearly in firewall policies. A firewall filters access requests","options":["Digital Signatures and Key Exchange","Implicit Deny","User Account Control","Hard Token Authentication"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Implicit Deny: ACL security is typically founded on the principle of implicit deny. implicit deny means that unless there is a rule specifying that access should be granted, any request for access is denied. This principle can be seen clearly in firewall policies. A firewall filters access requests"},{"id":"c2-0145","module":15,"lesson":"15B Windows Security Settings","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nA complementary principle to implicit deny is that of least privilege. This means that a user should be granted the minimum possible access necessary to perform the job. This can be complex to apply in practice, however. Designing a permissions system that respects the","context":"A complementary principle to implicit deny is that of least privilege. This means that a user should be granted the minimum possible access necessary to perform the job. This can be complex to apply in practice, however. Designing a permissions system that respects the","options":["Digital Signatures","Windows authentication involves a complex architecture of","Principle of Least Privilege","Authentication Methods"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Principle of Least Privilege: A complementary principle to implicit deny is that of least privilege. This means that a user should be granted the minimum possible access necessary to perform the job. This can be complex to apply in practice, however. Designing a permissions system that respects the"},{"id":"c2-0146","module":15,"lesson":"15C Windows Shares","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nInformation security is assured by developing security policies and controls. Making a system more secure is also referred to as hardening it. Different security policies should cover every The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 15: Securing Windows","context":"Information security is assured by developing security policies and controls. Making a system more secure is also referred to as hardening it. Different security policies should cover every The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 15: Securing Windows","options":["Key Exchange","Multifactor Authentication","Information Security","Username and Password"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Information Security: Information security is assured by developing security policies and controls. Making a system more secure is also referred to as hardening it. Different security policies should cover every The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 15: Securing Windows"},{"id":"c2-0147","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security","question":"What is being described here?\n\nTo assist with workstation and network security assessments, you need to understand the types of threats that an organization is exposed to and how vulnerabilities can be exploited to launch attacks.","context":"To assist with workstation and network security assessments, you need to understand the types of threats that an organization is exposed to and how vulnerabilities can be exploited to launch attacks.","options":["Relationship between vulnerability, threat, and risk","An invalid multifactor authentication example would be:","Fingerprint Windows Hello PIN Security Key Password Picture Password","User and Group Accounts"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Relationship between vulnerability, threat, and risk: To assist with workstation and network security assessments, you need to understand the types of threats that an organization is exposed to and how vulnerabilities can be exploited to launch attacks."},{"id":"c2-0148","module":15,"lesson":"15B Windows Security Settings","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nMany logical security controls depend to some extent on the use of encryption technologies.","context":"Many logical security controls depend to some extent on the use of encryption technologies.","options":["Security Groups","2-Step Verification","Hashing and Encryption Concepts","Single Sign-On"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Hashing and Encryption Concepts: Many logical security controls depend to some extent on the use of encryption technologies."},{"id":"c2-0149","module":15,"lesson":"15C Windows Shares","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nA symmetric encryption cipher uses a single secret key to both encrypt and decrypt data. The secret key is so-called because it must be kept secret. If the key is lost or stolen, the security is breached. Consequently, the main problem with symmetric encryption is secure distribution","context":"A symmetric encryption cipher uses a single secret key to both encrypt and decrypt data. The secret key is so-called because it must be kept secret. If the key is lost or stolen, the security is breached. Consequently, the main problem with symmetric encryption is secure distribution","options":["Local Users and Groups","It works on the following lines:","Security Assertions Markup Language (SAML)","Symmetric Encryption"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Symmetric Encryption: A symmetric encryption cipher uses a single secret key to both encrypt and decrypt data. The secret key is so-called because it must be kept secret. If the key is lost or stolen, the security is breached. Consequently, the main problem with symmetric encryption is secure distribution"},{"id":"c2-0150","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nAn asymmetric encryption cipher uses a key pair. A key pair is a private key and a public key that are mathematically linked. For any given message, either key can perform either the encrypt or decrypt operation but not both. Only the paired key can reverse the operation. For","context":"An asymmetric encryption cipher uses a key pair. A key pair is a private key and a public key that are mathematically linked. For any given message, either key can perform either the encrypt or decrypt operation but not both. Only the paired key can reverse the operation. For","options":["One-Time Passwords","User Account Control","Asymmetric Encryption","Windows Domains and Active Directory"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Asymmetric Encryption: An asymmetric encryption cipher uses a key pair. A key pair is a private key and a public key that are mathematically linked. For any given message, either key can perform either the encrypt or decrypt operation but not both. Only the paired key can reverse the operation. For"},{"id":"c2-0151","module":15,"lesson":"15B Windows Security Settings","question":"What is being described here?\n\nA hash is a short representation of data. A hash function takes any amount of data as input and produces a fixed-length value as output. A cryptographic hash performs this process as a one- way function that makes it impossible to recover the original value from the hash. Cryptographic","context":"A hash is a short representation of data. A hash function takes any amount of data as input and produces a fixed-length value as output. A cryptographic hash performs this process as a one- way function that makes it impossible to recover the original value from the hash. Cryptographic","options":["Authentication Methods","Cryptographic Hashes","Domain Controllers","There are a few types of one-time passwords including:"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cryptographic Hashes: A hash is a short representation of data. A hash function takes any amount of data as input and produces a fixed-length value as output. A cryptographic hash performs this process as a one- way function that makes it impossible to recover the original value from the hash. Cryptographic"},{"id":"c2-0152","module":15,"lesson":"15C Windows Shares","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nCryptographic hashes and encryption ciphers have different roles in achieving the information security goals of confidentiality, integrity, and availability. Often two or more of these three different types are used together in the same product or technology.","context":"Cryptographic hashes and encryption ciphers have different roles in achieving the information security goals of confidentiality, integrity, and availability. Often two or more of these three different types are used together in the same product or technology.","options":["Multifactor Authentication","Hard Token Authentication","Member Servers","Digital Signatures and Key Exchange"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Digital Signatures and Key Exchange: Cryptographic hashes and encryption ciphers have different roles in achieving the information security goals of confidentiality, integrity, and availability. Often two or more of these three different types are used together in the same product or technology."},{"id":"c2-0153","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nA digital signature proves that a message or digital certificate has not been altered or spoofed.","context":"A digital signature proves that a message or digital certificate has not been altered or spoofed.","options":["Windows authentication involves a complex architecture of","Digital Signatures","Organizational Units","An invalid multifactor authentication example would be:"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Digital Signatures: A digital signature proves that a message or digital certificate has not been altered or spoofed."},{"id":"c2-0154","module":15,"lesson":"15B Windows Security Settings","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nKey exchange allows two hosts to know the same symmetric encryption key without any other host finding out what it is. A symmetric cipher is much faster than an asymmetric one, so it is often used to protect the actual data exchange in a session. Asymmetric encryption only","context":"Key exchange allows two hosts to know the same symmetric encryption key without any other host finding out what it is. A symmetric cipher is much faster than an asymmetric one, so it is often used to protect the actual data exchange in a session. Asymmetric encryption only","options":["Key Exchange","Username and Password","Logical Security Controls","2-Step Verification"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Key Exchange: Key exchange allows two hosts to know the same symmetric encryption key without any other host finding out what it is. A symmetric cipher is much faster than an asymmetric one, so it is often used to protect the actual data exchange in a session. Asymmetric encryption only"},{"id":"c2-0155","module":15,"lesson":"15C Windows Shares","question":"What is being described here?\n\nA user account is the principal means of controlling access to computer and network resources and assigning rights or privileges. In Windows, a user can be set up with a local account or a Microsoft account: • A local account is defined on that computer only. For example, PC1\\David is the","context":"A user account is the principal means of controlling access to computer and network resources and assigning rights or privileges. In Windows, a user can be set up with a local account or a Microsoft account: • A local account is defined on that computer only. For example, PC1\\David is the","options":["User and Group Accounts","Confidentiality, Integrity, and Availability:","Fingerprint Windows Hello PIN Security Key Password Picture Password","It works on the following lines:"],"answer":0,"explanation":"User and Group Accounts: A user account is the principal means of controlling access to computer and network resources and assigning rights or privileges. In Windows, a user can be set up with a local account or a Microsoft account: • A local account is defined on that computer only. For example, PC1\\David is the"},{"id":"c2-0156","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nA security group is a collection of user accounts. Security groups are used when assigning permissions and rights, as it is more efficient to assign permissions to a group than to assign them individually to each user. You can set up a number of custom groups with least privilege","context":"A security group is a collection of user accounts. Security groups are used when assigning permissions and rights, as it is more efficient to assign permissions to a group than to assign them individually to each user. You can set up a number of custom groups with least privilege","options":["One-Time Passwords","Security Groups","Access Control Lists","Single Sign-On"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Security Groups: A security group is a collection of user accounts. Security groups are used when assigning permissions and rights, as it is more efficient to assign permissions to a group than to assign them individually to each user. You can set up a number of custom groups with least privilege"},{"id":"c2-0157","module":15,"lesson":"15B Windows Security Settings","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nThe Local Users and Groups management console provides an interface for managing both user and group accounts. Use the shortcut menus and object Properties dialogs to create, disable, and delete accounts, change account properties, reset user passwords, create custom","context":"The Local Users and Groups management console provides an interface for managing both user and group accounts. Use the shortcut menus and object Properties dialogs to create, disable, and delete accounts, change account properties, reset user passwords, create custom","options":["Security Assertions Markup Language (SAML)","Local Users and Groups","Implicit Deny","There are a few types of one-time passwords including:"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Local Users and Groups: The Local Users and Groups management console provides an interface for managing both user and group accounts. Use the shortcut menus and object Properties dialogs to create, disable, and delete accounts, change account properties, reset user passwords, create custom"},{"id":"c2-0158","module":15,"lesson":"15C Windows Shares","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nJust-in-Time (JIT) access is a security practice in which users are granted access to resources only when needed and for only as long as it takes to complete the needed task.","context":"Just-in-Time (JIT) access is a security practice in which users are granted access to resources only when needed and for only as long as it takes to complete the needed task.","options":["Hard Token Authentication","Windows Domains and Active Directory","Principle of Least Privilege","User Account Control"],"answer":3,"explanation":"User Account Control: Just-in-Time (JIT) access is a security practice in which users are granted access to resources only when needed and for only as long as it takes to complete the needed task."},{"id":"c2-0159","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security","question":"What is being described here?\n\nIn an access control system, accounts are configured with permissions to access resources and (for privileged accounts) rights to change the system configuration. To access an account, the user must authenticate by supplying the correct credentials, proving that they are the valid","context":"In an access control system, accounts are configured with permissions to access resources and (for privileged accounts) rights to change the system configuration. To access an account, the user must authenticate by supplying the correct credentials, proving that they are the valid","options":["Authentication Methods","Domain Controllers","Information Security","Windows authentication involves a complex architecture of"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Authentication Methods: In an access control system, accounts are configured with permissions to access resources and (for privileged accounts) rights to change the system configuration. To access an account, the user must authenticate by supplying the correct credentials, proving that they are the valid"},{"id":"c2-0160","module":15,"lesson":"15B Windows Security Settings","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nUsing a single factor makes authentication less reliable. A password could be shared, a device token could be stolen, or a facial recognition system could be spoofed using a photograph.","context":"Using a single factor makes authentication less reliable. A password could be shared, a device token could be stolen, or a facial recognition system could be spoofed using a photograph.","options":["Username and Password","Multifactor Authentication","Member Servers","Relationship between vulnerability, threat, and risk"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Multifactor Authentication: Using a single factor makes authentication less reliable. A password could be shared, a device token could be stolen, or a facial recognition system could be spoofed using a photograph."},{"id":"c2-0161","module":15,"lesson":"15C Windows Shares","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\n• User submits a username/password (knowledge) and a PIN (knowledge) to sign in.","context":"• User submits a username/password (knowledge) and a PIN (knowledge) to sign in.","options":["An invalid multifactor authentication example would be:","Fingerprint Windows Hello PIN Security Key Password Picture Password","Organizational Units","Hashing and Encryption Concepts"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An invalid multifactor authentication example would be:: • User submits a username/password (knowledge) and a PIN (knowledge) to sign in."},{"id":"c2-0162","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\n2-step verification is a means of using a soft token to check that a sign-in request is authentic.","context":"2-step verification is a means of using a soft token to check that a sign-in request is authentic.","options":["2-Step Verification","Single Sign-On","Symmetric Encryption","Logical Security Controls"],"answer":0,"explanation":"2-Step Verification: 2-step verification is a means of using a soft token to check that a sign-in request is authentic."},{"id":"c2-0163","module":15,"lesson":"15B Windows Security Settings","question":"What is being described here?\n\n1. The user registers a trusted contact method with the app. This could be an email account or phone number, for instance.","context":"1. The user registers a trusted contact method with the app. This could be an email account or phone number, for instance.","options":["Security Assertions Markup Language (SAML)","Confidentiality, Integrity, and Availability:","Asymmetric Encryption","It works on the following lines:"],"answer":3,"explanation":"It works on the following lines:: 1. The user registers a trusted contact method with the app. This could be an email account or phone number, for instance."},{"id":"c2-0164","module":15,"lesson":"15C Windows Shares","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nThe soft tokens may also be referred to as a one-time password (OTP). The OTP is only valid for a single login session and a new unique passcode is generated for each login attempt. OTPs add an additional level of security because even if the user's password is compromised, the","context":"The soft tokens may also be referred to as a one-time password (OTP). The OTP is only valid for a single login session and a new unique passcode is generated for each login attempt. OTPs add an additional level of security because even if the user's password is compromised, the","options":["Windows Domains and Active Directory","Cryptographic Hashes","One-Time Passwords","Access Control Lists"],"answer":2,"explanation":"One-Time Passwords: The soft tokens may also be referred to as a one-time password (OTP). The OTP is only valid for a single login session and a new unique passcode is generated for each login attempt. OTPs add an additional level of security because even if the user's password is compromised, the"},{"id":"c2-0165","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nThe Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 15: Securing Windows • Time-based OTP (TOTP) - This OTP is only valid for a set amount of time, such as 1 minute, before it expires. If the OTP is not entered within the specified timeframe, a new OTP will","context":"The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 15: Securing Windows • Time-based OTP (TOTP) - This OTP is only valid for a set amount of time, such as 1 minute, before it expires. If the OTP is not entered within the specified timeframe, a new OTP will","options":["Digital Signatures and Key Exchange","Domain Controllers","There are a few types of one-time passwords including:","Implicit Deny"],"answer":2,"explanation":"There are a few types of one-time passwords including:: The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 15: Securing Windows • Time-based OTP (TOTP) - This OTP is only valid for a set amount of time, such as 1 minute, before it expires. If the OTP is not entered within the specified timeframe, a new OTP will"},{"id":"c2-0166","module":15,"lesson":"15B Windows Security Settings","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nA hard token works in the same sort of way as an authenticator app but is implemented as firmware in a smart card or USB thumb drive rather than running on a smartphone. The hard token is first registered with the service or network. When the user needs to authenticate,","context":"A hard token works in the same sort of way as an authenticator app but is implemented as firmware in a smart card or USB thumb drive rather than running on a smartphone. The hard token is first registered with the service or network. When the user needs to authenticate,","options":["Member Servers","Hard Token Authentication","Principle of Least Privilege","Digital Signatures"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Hard Token Authentication: A hard token works in the same sort of way as an authenticator app but is implemented as firmware in a smart card or USB thumb drive rather than running on a smartphone. The hard token is first registered with the service or network. When the user needs to authenticate,"},{"id":"c2-0167","module":15,"lesson":"15C Windows Shares","question":"What is being described here?\n\ncomponents (docs.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/security/windows-authentication/ credentials-processes-in-windows-authentication), but the following three scenarios are typical: • Windows local sign-in - The Local Security Authority (LSA) compares the submitted","context":"components (docs.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/security/windows-authentication/ credentials-processes-in-windows-authentication), but the following three scenarios are typical: • Windows local sign-in - The Local Security Authority (LSA) compares the submitted","options":["Information Security","Key Exchange","Windows authentication involves a complex architecture of","Organizational Units"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Windows authentication involves a complex architecture of: components (docs.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/security/windows-authentication/ credentials-processes-in-windows-authentication), but the following three scenarios are typical: • Windows local sign-in - The Local Security Authority (LSA) compares the submitted"},{"id":"c2-0168","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nA username and password credential is configured by creating the user account and choosing a password. The user can change the password by pressing CTRL+ALT+DELETE or using account settings. An administrator can also reset the password using Local Users and Groups.","context":"A username and password credential is configured by creating the user account and choosing a password. The user can change the password by pressing CTRL+ALT+DELETE or using account settings. An administrator can also reset the password using Local Users and Groups.","options":["User and Group Accounts","Username and Password","Relationship between vulnerability, threat, and risk","Logical Security Controls"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Username and Password: A username and password credential is configured by creating the user account and choosing a password. The user can change the password by pressing CTRL+ALT+DELETE or using account settings. An administrator can also reset the password using Local Users and Groups."},{"id":"c2-0169","module":15,"lesson":"15B Windows Security Settings","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nNote: A PIN must be configured to set up Windows Hello. The PIN acts as a backup mechanism in case other methods become unavailable. For example, a camera may fail to work and make facial recognition impossible, or a hardware token might be lost or temporarily unavailable.","context":"Note: A PIN must be configured to set up Windows Hello. The PIN acts as a backup mechanism in case other methods become unavailable. For example, a camera may fail to work and make facial recognition impossible, or a hardware token might be lost or temporarily unavailable.","options":["Confidentiality, Integrity, and Availability:","Fingerprint Windows Hello PIN Security Key Password Picture Password","Security Groups","Hashing and Encryption Concepts"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Fingerprint Windows Hello PIN Security Key Password Picture Password: Note: A PIN must be configured to set up Windows Hello. The PIN acts as a backup mechanism in case other methods become unavailable. For example, a camera may fail to work and make facial recognition impossible, or a hardware token might be lost or temporarily unavailable."},{"id":"c2-0170","module":15,"lesson":"15C Windows Shares","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nSingle sign-on means that a user authenticates once to a device or network to gain access to multiple applications or services. The Kerberos authentication and authorization model for Active Directory domain networks implements SSO. A user who has authenticated with","context":"Single sign-on means that a user authenticates once to a device or network to gain access to multiple applications or services. The Kerberos authentication and authorization model for Active Directory domain networks implements SSO. A user who has authenticated with","options":["Single Sign-On","Access Control Lists","Local Users and Groups","Symmetric Encryption"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Single Sign-On: Single sign-on means that a user authenticates once to a device or network to gain access to multiple applications or services. The Kerberos authentication and authorization model for Active Directory domain networks implements SSO. A user who has authenticated with"},{"id":"c2-0171","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security","question":"What is being described here?\n\nSecurity Assertions Markup Language (SAML) is a special type of SSO. With SAML, an Identity Provider (IdP) is used to pass user credentials to a service provider (SP). This process works as below: • The user attempts to login to the service provider, such as Google Workspace.","context":"Security Assertions Markup Language (SAML) is a special type of SSO. With SAML, an Identity Provider (IdP) is used to pass user credentials to a service provider (SP). This process works as below: • The user attempts to login to the service provider, such as Google Workspace.","options":["Security Assertions Markup Language (SAML)","User Account Control","Asymmetric Encryption","Implicit Deny"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Security Assertions Markup Language (SAML): Security Assertions Markup Language (SAML) is a special type of SSO. With SAML, an Identity Provider (IdP) is used to pass user credentials to a service provider (SP). This process works as below: • The user attempts to login to the service provider, such as Google Workspace."},{"id":"c2-0172","module":15,"lesson":"15B Windows Security Settings","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nA local account is only recognized by the local machine and cannot be used to access other computers. For example, if the user David needs access to multiple computers in a workgroup environment, a separate local account must be configured on each computer ( PC1\\David,","context":"A local account is only recognized by the local machine and cannot be used to access other computers. For example, if the user David needs access to multiple computers in a workgroup environment, a separate local account must be configured on each computer ( PC1\\David,","options":["Cryptographic Hashes","Authentication Methods","Windows Domains and Active Directory","Principle of Least Privilege"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Windows Domains and Active Directory: A local account is only recognized by the local machine and cannot be used to access other computers. For example, if the user David needs access to multiple computers in a workgroup environment, a separate local account must be configured on each computer ( PC1\\David,"},{"id":"c2-0173","module":15,"lesson":"15C Windows Shares","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nTo create a domain, you need at least one Windows Server computer configured as a domain controller (DC). A DC stores a database of network information called active directory. This database stores user, group, and computer objects.","context":"To create a domain, you need at least one Windows Server computer configured as a domain controller (DC). A DC stores a database of network information called active directory. This database stores user, group, and computer objects.","options":["Digital Signatures and Key Exchange","Multifactor Authentication","Domain Controllers","Information Security"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Domain Controllers: To create a domain, you need at least one Windows Server computer configured as a domain controller (DC). A DC stores a database of network information called active directory. This database stores user, group, and computer objects."},{"id":"c2-0174","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nA member server is any server-based system that has been joined to the domain but does not maintain a copy of the Active Directory database. A member server provides file and print and application server services, such as Exchange for email or SQL Server for database or line-of-","context":"A member server is any server-based system that has been joined to the domain but does not maintain a copy of the Active Directory database. A member server provides file and print and application server services, such as Exchange for email or SQL Server for database or line-of-","options":["Relationship between vulnerability, threat, and risk","Digital Signatures","Member Servers","An invalid multifactor authentication example would be:"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Member Servers: A member server is any server-based system that has been joined to the domain but does not maintain a copy of the Active Directory database. A member server provides file and print and application server services, such as Exchange for email or SQL Server for database or line-of-"},{"id":"c2-0175","module":15,"lesson":"15B Windows Security Settings","question":"What is being described here?\n\nAn organizational unit is a way of dividing a domain up into different administrative realms.","context":"An organizational unit is a way of dividing a domain up into different administrative realms.","options":["Key Exchange","Hashing and Encryption Concepts","Organizational Units","2-Step Verification"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Organizational Units: An organizational unit is a way of dividing a domain up into different administrative realms."},{"id":"c2-0176","module":16,"lesson":"16A Windows Editions","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nDesigned for domestic consumers and SOHO use; it cannot join a Windows domain network.","context":"Designed for domestic consumers and SOHO use; it cannot join a Windows domain network.","options":["Windows Home","Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc)","32-bit Versus 64-bit","32-bit Windows"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Windows Home: Designed for domestic consumers and SOHO use; it cannot join a Windows domain network."},{"id":"c2-0177","module":16,"lesson":"16B OS Installations & Upgrades","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nDesigned for small and medium-sized businesses and supports features such as Group Policy, BitLocker, and Remote Desktop host.","context":"Designed for small and medium-sized businesses and supports features such as Group Policy, BitLocker, and Remote Desktop host.","options":["BitLocker","Windows Pro","64-bit Windows","Desktop Styles"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Windows Pro: Designed for small and medium-sized businesses and supports features such as Group Policy, BitLocker, and Remote Desktop host."},{"id":"c2-0178","module":16,"lesson":"16A Windows Editions","question":"What is being described here?\n\nTailored for large organizations with advanced security, deployment, and management features and is available through volume licensing.","context":"Tailored for large organizations with advanced security, deployment, and management features and is available through volume licensing.","options":["Windows Enterprise","Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP)","Windows Home Edition","Windows 11"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Windows Enterprise: Tailored for large organizations with advanced security, deployment, and management features and is available through volume licensing."},{"id":"c2-0179","module":16,"lesson":"16B OS Installations & Upgrades","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nWindows editions created to comply with EU regulations by excluding certain pre-installed multimedia applications.","context":"Windows editions created to comply with EU regulations by excluding certain pre-installed multimedia applications.","options":["N versions","Pro for Workstations","In-place upgrade","Windows Home Licensing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"N versions: Windows editions created to comply with EU regulations by excluding certain pre-installed multimedia applications."},{"id":"c2-0180","module":16,"lesson":"16A Windows Editions","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nA Windows Home license pre-installed on a PC and valid only for that device.","context":"A Windows Home license pre-installed on a PC and valid only for that device.","options":["OEM license","Enterprise volume licensing","Windows Home offers two licensing models:","Clean install"],"answer":0,"explanation":"OEM license: A Windows Home license pre-installed on a PC and valid only for that device."},{"id":"c2-0181","module":16,"lesson":"16B OS Installations & Upgrades","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nA Windows Home license that can be transferred between computers but can be active on only one device at a time.","context":"A Windows Home license that can be transferred between computers but can be active on only one device at a time.","options":["Windows Home System Limitations","Feature update","Retail license","TPM"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Retail license: A Windows Home license that can be transferred between computers but can be active on only one device at a time."},{"id":"c2-0182","module":16,"lesson":"16A Windows Editions","question":"What is being described here?\n\nA network where devices share resources but are managed independently.","context":"A network where devices share resources but are managed independently.","options":["Windows Editions for Work and Education","32-bit Windows","1.3 Compare and contrast basic features of Microsoft Windows editions","Workgroup"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Workgroup: A network where devices share resources but are managed independently."},{"id":"c2-0183","module":16,"lesson":"16B OS Installations & Upgrades","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nA network where devices connect to a centralized Domain Controller for management and security policies.","context":"A network where devices connect to a centralized Domain Controller for management and security policies.","options":["Windows Versions","Domain","64-bit Windows","Domain Network Support"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Domain: A network where devices connect to a centralized Domain Controller for management and security policies."},{"id":"c2-0184","module":16,"lesson":"16A Windows Editions","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nA tool for configuring and enforcing OS and application settings across devices.","context":"A tool for configuring and enforcing OS and application settings across devices.","options":["Workgroup vs. Domain Networks","Windows 11","32-bit Versus 64-bit","Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc): A tool for configuring and enforcing OS and application settings across devices."},{"id":"c2-0185","module":16,"lesson":"16B OS Installations & Upgrades","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nA Windows feature that provides disk encryption to protect data.","context":"A Windows feature that provides disk encryption to protect data.","options":["Desktop Styles","BitLocker","Pro for Workstations","Notable Features of Windows Pro, Enterprise, and Education Editions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"BitLocker: A Windows feature that provides disk encryption to protect data."},{"id":"c2-0186","module":16,"lesson":"16A Windows Editions","question":"What is being described here?\n\nA remote connection protocol supported as a host feature by Windows editions other than Home.","context":"A remote connection protocol supported as a host feature by Windows editions other than Home.","options":["Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP)","Windows Pro and Enterprise Editions:","Enterprise volume licensing","Windows Home Edition"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP): A remote connection protocol supported as a host feature by Windows editions other than Home."},{"id":"c2-0187","module":16,"lesson":"16B OS Installations & Upgrades","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nAn upgrade that preserves applications, settings, and data when they are compatible.","context":"An upgrade that preserves applications, settings, and data when they are compatible.","options":["In-place upgrade","TPM","Windows Upgrade Paths and Feature Updates","Windows Home Licensing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In-place upgrade: An upgrade that preserves applications, settings, and data when they are compatible."},{"id":"c2-0188","module":16,"lesson":"16A Windows Editions","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nAn installation path that does not preserve the existing OS installation in place.","context":"An installation path that does not preserve the existing OS installation in place.","options":["Clean install","Windows Home offers two licensing models:","1.3 Compare and contrast basic features of Microsoft Windows editions","Edition Considerations:"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Clean install: An installation path that does not preserve the existing OS installation in place."},{"id":"c2-0189","module":16,"lesson":"16B OS Installations & Upgrades","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nA Windows update that introduces changes to features and functionality.","context":"A Windows update that introduces changes to features and functionality.","options":["Windows Versions","Windows Home","Windows Home System Limitations","Feature update"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Feature update: A Windows update that introduces changes to features and functionality."},{"id":"c2-0190","module":16,"lesson":"16A Windows Editions","question":"What is being described here?\n\nA Windows architecture with a 4 GB RAM limit for 32-bit editions.","context":"A Windows architecture with a 4 GB RAM limit for 32-bit editions.","options":["Windows Pro","Windows Editions for Work and Education","32-bit Versus 64-bit","32-bit Windows"],"answer":3,"explanation":"32-bit Windows: A Windows architecture with a 4 GB RAM limit for 32-bit editions."},{"id":"c2-0191","module":16,"lesson":"16B OS Installations & Upgrades","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nA Windows architecture that can support more RAM than 32-bit editions, with limits varying by license.","context":"A Windows architecture that can support more RAM than 32-bit editions, with limits varying by license.","options":["Domain Network Support","Desktop Styles","Windows Enterprise","64-bit Windows"],"answer":3,"explanation":"64-bit Windows: A Windows architecture that can support more RAM than 32-bit editions, with limits varying by license."},{"id":"c2-0192","module":16,"lesson":"16A Windows Editions","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nA Windows client OS available in Home, Pro, and Enterprise editions in the guide.","context":"A Windows client OS available in Home, Pro, and Enterprise editions in the guide.","options":["N versions","Windows 11","Windows Home Edition","Workgroup vs. Domain Networks"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Windows 11: A Windows client OS available in Home, Pro, and Enterprise editions in the guide."},{"id":"c2-0193","module":16,"lesson":"16B OS Installations & Upgrades","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nA Windows edition designed for advanced users and high-performance hardware.","context":"A Windows edition designed for advanced users and high-performance hardware.","options":["Pro for Workstations","OEM license","Notable Features of Windows Pro, Enterprise, and Education Editions","Windows Home Licensing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Pro for Workstations: A Windows edition designed for advanced users and high-performance hardware."},{"id":"c2-0194","module":16,"lesson":"16A Windows Editions","question":"What is being described here?\n\nThe licensing model through which Windows Enterprise and Education are available.","context":"The licensing model through which Windows Enterprise and Education are available.","options":["Windows Pro and Enterprise Editions:","Enterprise volume licensing","Retail license","Windows Home offers two licensing models:"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Enterprise volume licensing: The licensing model through which Windows Enterprise and Education are available."},{"id":"c2-0195","module":16,"lesson":"16B OS Installations & Upgrades","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nA hardware security requirement referenced for Windows installation and upgrade considerations.","context":"A hardware security requirement referenced for Windows installation and upgrade considerations.","options":["Workgroup","TPM","Windows Upgrade Paths and Feature Updates","Windows Home System Limitations"],"answer":1,"explanation":"TPM: A hardware security requirement referenced for Windows installation and upgrade considerations."},{"id":"c2-0196","module":16,"lesson":"16A Windows Editions","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nLearning Outcomes As you study this lesson, answer the following questions: • What are the key differences between Windows Home and Windows Pro editions, and which edition supports domain network integration?","context":"Learning Outcomes As you study this lesson, answer the following questions: • What are the key differences between Windows Home and Windows Pro editions, and which edition supports domain network integration?","options":["Domain","Windows Editions for Work and Education","1.3 Compare and contrast basic features of Microsoft Windows editions","Edition Considerations:"],"answer":2,"explanation":"1.3 Compare and contrast basic features of Microsoft Windows editions: Learning Outcomes As you study this lesson, answer the following questions: • What are the key differences between Windows Home and Windows Pro editions, and which edition supports domain network integration?"},{"id":"c2-0197","module":16,"lesson":"16B OS Installations & Upgrades","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nWindows 10 and Windows 11, available in Home, Pro, and Enterprise editions, represent the currently supported versions of the Windows client OS. These editions target different market sectors, such as home users and corporate environments. Additionally, both versions have","context":"Windows 10 and Windows 11, available in Home, Pro, and Enterprise editions, represent the currently supported versions of the Windows client OS. These editions target different market sectors, such as home users and corporate environments. Additionally, both versions have","options":["Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc)","Domain Network Support","Windows Versions","Windows Home"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Windows Versions: Windows 10 and Windows 11, available in Home, Pro, and Enterprise editions, represent the currently supported versions of the Windows client OS. These editions target different market sectors, such as home users and corporate environments. Additionally, both versions have"},{"id":"c2-0198","module":16,"lesson":"16A Windows Editions","question":"What is being described here?\n\nEach version and edition of Windows 10 was initially available in both 32-bit and 64-bit formats.","context":"Each version and edition of Windows 10 was initially available in both 32-bit and 64-bit formats.","options":["32-bit Versus 64-bit","Workgroup vs. Domain Networks","BitLocker","Windows Pro"],"answer":0,"explanation":"32-bit Versus 64-bit: Each version and edition of Windows 10 was initially available in both 32-bit and 64-bit formats."},{"id":"c2-0199","module":16,"lesson":"16B OS Installations & Upgrades","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nThe Windows user interface (UI) revolves around the desktop, Start menu, taskbar, and notification area. While these elements remain consistent, Windows versions, editions (such as Home, Pro, Pro for Workstations, and Enterprise), and feature updates often bring changes. For","context":"The Windows user interface (UI) revolves around the desktop, Start menu, taskbar, and notification area. While these elements remain consistent, Windows versions, editions (such as Home, Pro, Pro for Workstations, and Enterprise), and feature updates often bring changes. For","options":["Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP)","Windows Enterprise","Notable Features of Windows Pro, Enterprise, and Education Editions","Desktop Styles"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Desktop Styles: The Windows user interface (UI) revolves around the desktop, Start menu, taskbar, and notification area. While these elements remain consistent, Windows versions, editions (such as Home, Pro, Pro for Workstations, and Enterprise), and feature updates often bring changes. For"},{"id":"c2-0200","module":16,"lesson":"16A Windows Editions","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nDesigned for domestic consumers and small office/home office (SOHO) use, Windows Home lacks unique features and has fewer capabilities than other editions, notably unable to join a Windows domain network.","context":"Designed for domestic consumers and small office/home office (SOHO) use, Windows Home lacks unique features and has fewer capabilities than other editions, notably unable to join a Windows domain network.","options":["In-place upgrade","N versions","Windows Pro and Enterprise Editions:","Windows Home Edition"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Windows Home Edition: Designed for domestic consumers and small office/home office (SOHO) use, Windows Home lacks unique features and has fewer capabilities than other editions, notably unable to join a Windows domain network."},{"id":"c2-0201","module":16,"lesson":"16B OS Installations & Upgrades","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nWindows Home offers two licensing models: 1. Original Equipment Manufacturer (OEM) License: This license comes pre-installed on a PC or laptop and is valid only for that device. The computer vendor provides support. Most new devices can upgrade to Windows 11.","context":"Windows Home offers two licensing models: 1. Original Equipment Manufacturer (OEM) License: This license comes pre-installed on a PC or laptop and is valid only for that device. The computer vendor provides support. Most new devices can upgrade to Windows 11.","options":["Windows Upgrade Paths and Feature Updates","Clean install","OEM license","Windows Home Licensing"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Windows Home Licensing: Windows Home offers two licensing models: 1. Original Equipment Manufacturer (OEM) License: This license comes pre-installed on a PC or laptop and is valid only for that device. The computer vendor provides support. Most new devices can upgrade to Windows 11."},{"id":"c2-0202","module":16,"lesson":"16A Windows Editions","question":"What is being described here?\n\n1. Original Equipment Manufacturer (OEM) License: This license comes pre-installed on a PC or laptop and is valid only for that device. The computer vendor provides support. Most new devices can upgrade to Windows 11.","context":"1. Original Equipment Manufacturer (OEM) License: This license comes pre-installed on a PC or laptop and is valid only for that device. The computer vendor provides support. Most new devices can upgrade to Windows 11.","options":["Edition Considerations:","Feature update","Retail license","Windows Home offers two licensing models:"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Windows Home offers two licensing models:: 1. Original Equipment Manufacturer (OEM) License: This license comes pre-installed on a PC or laptop and is valid only for that device. The computer vendor provides support. Most new devices can upgrade to Windows 11."},{"id":"c2-0203","module":16,"lesson":"16B OS Installations & Upgrades","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nWindows Home supports multicore processing (up to 64 cores) and HyperThreading but does not support multiple CPUs. The 64-bit edition is limited to 128 GB of RAM.","context":"Windows Home supports multicore processing (up to 64 cores) and HyperThreading but does not support multiple CPUs. The 64-bit edition is limited to 128 GB of RAM.","options":["Windows Home","Workgroup","32-bit Windows","Windows Home System Limitations"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Windows Home System Limitations: Windows Home supports multicore processing (up to 64 cores) and HyperThreading but does not support multiple CPUs. The 64-bit edition is limited to 128 GB of RAM."},{"id":"c2-0204","module":16,"lesson":"16A Windows Editions","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\n• Windows Pro: Designed for small and medium-sized businesses, available through OEM or retail licensing. It offers features like Group Policy, BitLocker, and Remote Desktop host for enhanced network administration. The Pro for Workstations edition supports more advanced","context":"• Windows Pro: Designed for small and medium-sized businesses, available through OEM or retail licensing. It offers features like Group Policy, BitLocker, and Remote Desktop host for enhanced network administration. The Pro for Workstations edition supports more advanced","options":["Domain","Windows Editions for Work and Education","Windows Pro","64-bit Windows"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Windows Editions for Work and Education: • Windows Pro: Designed for small and medium-sized businesses, available through OEM or retail licensing. It offers features like Group Policy, BitLocker, and Remote Desktop host for enhanced network administration. The Pro for Workstations edition supports more advanced"},{"id":"c2-0205","module":16,"lesson":"16B OS Installations & Upgrades","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nThe primary distinction between Pro, Enterprise, and Education editions versus Windows Home is domain network support. These editions can join a domain, allowing centralized management of computers, user accounts, and policies via a Domain Controller (DC) server. This is essential","context":"The primary distinction between Pro, Enterprise, and Education editions versus Windows Home is domain network support. These editions can join a domain, allowing centralized management of computers, user accounts, and policies via a Domain Controller (DC) server. This is essential","options":["Windows 11","Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc)","Domain Network Support","Windows Enterprise"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Domain Network Support: The primary distinction between Pro, Enterprise, and Education editions versus Windows Home is domain network support. These editions can join a domain, allowing centralized management of computers, user accounts, and policies via a Domain Controller (DC) server. This is essential"},{"id":"c2-0206","module":16,"lesson":"16A Windows Editions","question":"What is being described here?\n\n• Workgroup: Devices share files and resources but are managed independently.","context":"• Workgroup: Devices share files and resources but are managed independently.","options":["N versions","Pro for Workstations","Workgroup vs. Domain Networks","BitLocker"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Workgroup vs. Domain Networks: • Workgroup: Devices share files and resources but are managed independently."},{"id":"c2-0207","module":16,"lesson":"16B OS Installations & Upgrades","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\n• Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc): Configures and enforces OS and application settings across devices, ensuring consistent configurations. Not available in Windows Home.","context":"• Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc): Configures and enforces OS and application settings across devices, ensuring consistent configurations. Not available in Windows Home.","options":["OEM license","Notable Features of Windows Pro, Enterprise, and Education Editions","Enterprise volume licensing","Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Notable Features of Windows Pro, Enterprise, and Education Editions: • Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc): Configures and enforces OS and application settings across devices, ensuring consistent configurations. Not available in Windows Home."},{"id":"c2-0208","module":16,"lesson":"16A Windows Editions","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\n• Windows Pro: Available as an OEM, retail/full packaged product (FPP), or through volume licensing, which offers discounts for bulk purchases and allows custom installation images for quick deployment. Windows Pro for Workstations includes all Pro features but supports","context":"• Windows Pro: Available as an OEM, retail/full packaged product (FPP), or through volume licensing, which offers discounts for bulk purchases and allows custom installation images for quick deployment. Windows Pro for Workstations includes all Pro features but supports","options":["TPM","In-place upgrade","Retail license","Windows Pro and Enterprise Editions:"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Windows Pro and Enterprise Editions:: • Windows Pro: Available as an OEM, retail/full packaged product (FPP), or through volume licensing, which offers discounts for bulk purchases and allows custom installation images for quick deployment. Windows Pro for Workstations includes all Pro features but supports"},{"id":"c2-0209","module":16,"lesson":"16B OS Installations & Upgrades","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nAn in-place upgrade allows you to upgrade to a new Windows version while preserving applications, settings, and data, provided they are compatible.","context":"An in-place upgrade allows you to upgrade to a new Windows version while preserving applications, settings, and data, provided they are compatible.","options":["Clean install","1.3 Compare and contrast basic features of Microsoft Windows editions","Workgroup","Windows Upgrade Paths and Feature Updates"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Windows Upgrade Paths and Feature Updates: An in-place upgrade allows you to upgrade to a new Windows version while preserving applications, settings, and data, provided they are compatible."},{"id":"c2-0210","module":16,"lesson":"16A Windows Editions","question":"What is being described here?\n\n• You can upgrade within the same or higher edition (e.g., Windows 10 Home to Windows 11 Home or Pro), but you cannot upgrade from Home to Enterprise directly.","context":"• You can upgrade within the same or higher edition (e.g., Windows 10 Home to Windows 11 Home or Pro), but you cannot upgrade from Home to Enterprise directly.","options":["Edition Considerations:","Feature update","Domain","Windows Versions"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Edition Considerations:: • You can upgrade within the same or higher edition (e.g., Windows 10 Home to Windows 11 Home or Pro), but you cannot upgrade from Home to Enterprise directly."},{"id":"c2-0211","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nSo far in this course, you worked mostly with the Microsoft Windows operating system. A CompTIA A+ technician should be capable of supporting diverse OS environments. The various operating systems you might encounter use different interfaces and command syntax, but the","context":"So far in this course, you worked mostly with the Microsoft Windows operating system. A CompTIA A+ technician should be capable of supporting diverse OS environments. The various operating systems you might encounter use different interfaces and command syntax, but the","options":["Text Editors","For example, numeric permission 0754 translates to:","Shells, Terminals, and Consoles","Filesystem Management"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Shells, Terminals, and Consoles: So far in this course, you worked mostly with the Microsoft Windows operating system. A CompTIA A+ technician should be capable of supporting diverse OS environments. The various operating systems you might encounter use different interfaces and command syntax, but the"},{"id":"c2-0212","module":17,"lesson":"17B Package & Network Mgmt","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nBefore the operating system loads, a bootloader initializes the system. It is responsible for loading the kernel into memory and starting the operating system. Bootloaders support multi-boot configurations, allowing multiple operating systems on the same device. Common","context":"Before the operating system loads, a bootloader initializes the system. It is responsible for loading the kernel into memory and starting the operating system. Bootloaders support multi-boot configurations, allowing multiple operating systems on the same device. Common","options":["Bootloaders","Navigation Commands","Modifying permissions using the chmod command","Key filesystem management tools include:"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Bootloaders: Before the operating system loads, a bootloader initializes the system. It is responsible for loading the kernel into memory and starting the operating system. Bootloaders support multi-boot configurations, allowing multiple operating systems on the same device. Common"},{"id":"c2-0213","module":17,"lesson":"17C macOS Features","question":"What is being described here?\n\nA shell provides a command-line environment for users to interact with the OS and applications.","context":"A shell provides a command-line environment for users to interact with the OS and applications.","options":["Linux","Shells and Terminals","Examples:","File Management Commands"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Shells and Terminals: A shell provides a command-line environment for users to interact with the OS and applications."},{"id":"c2-0214","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nLinux distros intended for client PCs usually start with a graphical desktop environment. This environment is powered by Xorg, an open-source implementation of the X Window System.","context":"Linux distros intended for client PCs usually start with a graphical desktop environment. This environment is powered by Xorg, an open-source implementation of the X Window System.","options":["User Account Management","Search Commands","2. Change both the owner and group: chown username:groupname file.txt","Desktop Environments"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Desktop Environments: Linux distros intended for client PCs usually start with a graphical desktop environment. This environment is powered by Xorg, an open-source implementation of the X Window System."},{"id":"c2-0215","module":17,"lesson":"17B Package & Network Mgmt","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nIn systems with a graphical environment, the X server runs on a virtual tty console, typically tty1. Users can switch between consoles using CTRL+ALT+Fx keys, with each console supporting different login prompts and shells.","context":"In systems with a graphical environment, the X server runs on a virtual tty console, typically tty1. Users can switch between consoles using CTRL+ALT+Fx keys, with each console supporting different login prompts and shells.","options":["Distributions and Repositories","User Management Commands","Console Switching","Metacharacters and Escaping"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Console Switching: In systems with a graphical environment, the X server runs on a virtual tty console, typically tty1. Users can switch between consoles using CTRL+ALT+Fx keys, with each console supporting different login prompts and shells."},{"id":"c2-0216","module":17,"lesson":"17C macOS Features","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\n• Command: The first \"word\" is the command, which can be a full or relative path to an executable, or simply the name of an executable located in a directory specified by the PATH environment variable. The command is recognized up to the first space character.","context":"• Command: The first \"word\" is the command, which can be a full or relative path to an executable, or simply the name of an executable located in a directory specified by the PATH environment variable. The command is recognized up to the first space character.","options":["Linux commands follow a standard format:","There are three ways to escape strings:","Group Management Commands","Listing package manager sources in Ubuntu Linux"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Linux commands follow a standard format:: • Command: The first \"word\" is the command, which can be a full or relative path to an executable, or simply the name of an executable located in a directory specified by the PATH environment variable. The command is recognized up to the first space character."},{"id":"c2-0217","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"What is being described here?\n\nIn Linux, commands, parameters, and file and directory names are all case-sensitive. For instance, ls -l file.data and ls -L File.data will yield different results. Typing a command name with incorrect capitalization will result in an error message.","context":"In Linux, commands, parameters, and file and directory names are all case-sensitive. For instance, ls -l file.data and ls -L File.data will yield different results. Typing a command name with incorrect capitalization will result in an error message.","options":["Filesystem Management","Process Monitoring Commands","Case Sensitivity","File Permissions Commands"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Case Sensitivity: In Linux, commands, parameters, and file and directory names are all case-sensitive. For instance, ls -l file.data and ls -L File.data will yield different results. Typing a command name with incorrect capitalization will result in an error message."},{"id":"c2-0218","module":17,"lesson":"17B Package & Network Mgmt","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nTo view a Linux command's function and syntax, use the --help option. Since the help output can be lengthy, it's common to pipe it to the more command for viewing one page at a time, e.g.,ls --help | more . Alternatively, use the man command to access detailed manual pages for","context":"To view a Linux command's function and syntax, use the --help option. Since the help output can be lengthy, it's common to pipe it to the more command for viewing one page at a time, e.g.,ls --help | more . Alternatively, use the man command to access detailed manual pages for","options":["Key filesystem management tools include:","Help System","Listing the state of running processes","For example, using ls -l for a long directory listing:"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Help System: To view a Linux command's function and syntax, use the --help option. Since the help output can be lengthy, it's common to pipe it to the more command for viewing one page at a time, e.g.,ls --help | more . Alternatively, use the man command to access detailed manual pages for"},{"id":"c2-0219","module":17,"lesson":"17C macOS Features","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nMost Linux files are in plain text format and can be easily edited. There are many text editors available. For those familiar with Windows, the nano editor is a simple option. To open or create a file, use nano filepath , or nano -l filepath to display line numbers. Navigate with the cursor","context":"Most Linux files are in plain text format and can be easily edited. There are many text editors available. For those familiar with Windows, the nano editor is a simple option. To open or create a file, use nano filepath , or nano -l filepath to display line numbers. Navigate with the cursor","options":["File Management Commands","Text Editors","Various keystrokes execute process management actions, including:","For example, numeric permission 0754 translates to:"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Text Editors: Most Linux files are in plain text format and can be easily edited. There are many text editors available. For those familiar with Windows, the nano editor is a simple option. To open or create a file, use nano filepath , or nano -l filepath to display line numbers. Navigate with the cursor"},{"id":"c2-0220","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nIn Linux, everything is represented as a file within a unified file system. The first fixed disk is typically /dev/sda , while additional devices, like a USB drive, appear as /dev/sdb .","context":"In Linux, everything is represented as a file within a unified file system. The first fixed disk is typically /dev/sda , while additional devices, like a USB drive, appear as /dev/sdb .","options":["Modifying permissions using the chmod command","User Account Management","Navigation Commands","Network Management Commands"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Navigation Commands: In Linux, everything is represented as a file within a unified file system. The first fixed disk is typically /dev/sda , while additional devices, like a USB drive, appear as /dev/sdb ."},{"id":"c2-0221","module":17,"lesson":"17B Package & Network Mgmt","question":"What is being described here?\n\nKey commands for navigating the Linux file system include pwd,cd,ls, and cat.","context":"Key commands for navigating the Linux file system include pwd,cd,ls, and cat.","options":["Example usage:","Linux","Examples:","User Management Commands"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Linux: Key commands for navigating the Linux file system include pwd,cd,ls, and cat."},{"id":"c2-0222","module":17,"lesson":"17C macOS Features","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nLinux supports very fast and accurate file system informational search commands.","context":"Linux supports very fast and accurate file system informational search commands.","options":["Example entry in /etc/hosts:","2. Change both the owner and group: chown username:groupname file.txt","Search Commands","Group Management Commands"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Search Commands: Linux supports very fast and accurate file system informational search commands."},{"id":"c2-0223","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nIn Linux, escaping means using a special character (usually a backslash \\) to indicate that the following character should be treated as a literal rather than interpreted in its usual, special way. This is necessary when dealing with metacharacters, which have specific meanings in the","context":"In Linux, escaping means using a special character (usually a backslash \\) to indicate that the following character should be treated as a literal rather than interpreted in its usual, special way. This is necessary when dealing with metacharacters, which have specific meanings in the","options":["Metacharacters and Escaping","Distributions and Repositories","127.0.0.1 localhost","File Permissions Commands"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Metacharacters and Escaping: In Linux, escaping means using a special character (usually a backslash \\) to indicate that the following character should be treated as a literal rather than interpreted in its usual, special way. This is necessary when dealing with metacharacters, which have specific meanings in the"},{"id":"c2-0224","module":17,"lesson":"17B Package & Network Mgmt","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\n• Backslash (\\): Escapes the next character only. For example,\\* treats * as a literal, and \\\\ treats \\ as a literal.","context":"• Backslash (\\): Escapes the next character only. For example,\\* treats * as a literal, and \\\\ treats \\ as a literal.","options":["Shells, Terminals, and Consoles","For example, using ls -l for a long directory listing:","There are three ways to escape strings:","Listing package manager sources in Ubuntu Linux"],"answer":2,"explanation":"There are three ways to escape strings:: • Backslash (\\): Escapes the next character only. For example,\\* treats * as a literal, and \\\\ treats \\ as a literal."},{"id":"c2-0225","module":17,"lesson":"17C macOS Features","question":"What is being described here?\n\nFilesystem management involves organizing, maintaining, and accessing data stored on disk drives. It includes tasks such as mounting filesystems, checking and repairing them, and configuring their behavior.","context":"Filesystem management involves organizing, maintaining, and accessing data stored on disk drives. It includes tasks such as mounting filesystems, checking and repairing them, and configuring their behavior.","options":["For example, numeric permission 0754 translates to:","Filesystem Management","Process Monitoring Commands","Bootloaders"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Filesystem Management: Filesystem management involves organizing, maintaining, and accessing data stored on disk drives. It includes tasks such as mounting filesystems, checking and repairing them, and configuring their behavior."},{"id":"c2-0226","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\n• Mounting: To access a filesystem, it must be mounted, which means attaching it to a directory in the existing filesystem hierarchy. The mount command is used for this purpose.","context":"• Mounting: To access a filesystem, it must be mounted, which means attaching it to a directory in the existing filesystem hierarchy. The mount command is used for this purpose.","options":["Key filesystem management tools include:","Listing the state of running processes","Shells and Terminals","Modifying permissions using the chmod command"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Key filesystem management tools include:: • Mounting: To access a filesystem, it must be mounted, which means attaching it to a directory in the existing filesystem hierarchy. The mount command is used for this purpose."},{"id":"c2-0227","module":17,"lesson":"17B Package & Network Mgmt","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nFile management commands are used to move, copy, and delete data.","context":"File management commands are used to move, copy, and delete data.","options":["Various keystrokes execute process management actions, including:","File Management Commands","Desktop Environments","Examples:"],"answer":1,"explanation":"File Management Commands: File management commands are used to move, copy, and delete data."},{"id":"c2-0228","module":17,"lesson":"17C macOS Features","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nIn Linux, the root account, or superuser, has full administrative privileges and can perform any action on the system. It should be used only when absolutely necessary. During setup, most Linux distributions prompt you to create a regular user account for daily tasks. Instead of","context":"In Linux, the root account, or superuser, has full administrative privileges and can perform any action on the system. It should be used only when absolutely necessary. During setup, most Linux distributions prompt you to create a regular user account for daily tasks. Instead of","options":["Console Switching","Network Management Commands","2. Change both the owner and group: chown username:groupname file.txt","User Account Management"],"answer":3,"explanation":"User Account Management: In Linux, the root account, or superuser, has full administrative privileges and can perform any action on the system. It should be used only when absolutely necessary. During setup, most Linux distributions prompt you to create a regular user account for daily tasks. Instead of"},{"id":"c2-0229","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"What is being described here?\n\nUser settings are stored in the /etc/passwd file, while group settings are in the /etc/group file.","context":"User settings are stored in the /etc/passwd file, while group settings are in the /etc/group file.","options":["User Management Commands","Example usage:","Distributions and Repositories","Linux commands follow a standard format:"],"answer":0,"explanation":"User Management Commands: User settings are stored in the /etc/passwd file, while group settings are in the /etc/group file."},{"id":"c2-0230","module":17,"lesson":"17B Package & Network Mgmt","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nEach user account can be assigned to groups to manage file permissions. Use the groupadd, groupmod, and groupdel commands to manage group memberships. A user can belong to multiple groups but has only one effective group ID at a time, listed in /etc/passwd. The","context":"Each user account can be assigned to groups to manage file permissions. Use the groupadd, groupmod, and groupdel commands to manage group memberships. A user can belong to multiple groups but has only one effective group ID at a time, listed in /etc/passwd. The","options":["Listing package manager sources in Ubuntu Linux","Example entry in /etc/hosts:","Group Management Commands","Case Sensitivity"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Group Management Commands: Each user account can be assigned to groups to manage file permissions. Use the groupadd, groupmod, and groupdel commands to manage group memberships. A user can belong to multiple groups but has only one effective group ID at a time, listed in /etc/passwd. The"},{"id":"c2-0231","module":17,"lesson":"17C macOS Features","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nEach file in Linux has a set of permissions that determine user access levels. The permissions system includes three rights: • Read (r): Allows viewing the contents of a file or directory.","context":"Each file in Linux has a set of permissions that determine user access levels. The permissions system includes three rights: • Read (r): Allows viewing the contents of a file or directory.","options":["127.0.0.1 localhost","Help System","Process Monitoring Commands","File Permissions Commands"],"answer":3,"explanation":"File Permissions Commands: Each file in Linux has a set of permissions that determine user access levels. The permissions system includes three rights: • Read (r): Allows viewing the contents of a file or directory."},{"id":"c2-0232","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\n• drwxr-xr-x 2 bobby admins Desktop : The owner (bobby) has full (rwx) permissions, while the group (admins) and others have read and execute (r-x) permissions.","context":"• drwxr-xr-x 2 bobby admins Desktop : The owner (bobby) has full (rwx) permissions, while the group (admins) and others have read and execute (r-x) permissions.","options":["Shells, Terminals, and Consoles","Text Editors","For example, using ls -l for a long directory listing:","Listing the state of running processes"],"answer":2,"explanation":"For example, using ls -l for a long directory listing:: • drwxr-xr-x 2 bobby admins Desktop : The owner (bobby) has full (rwx) permissions, while the group (admins) and others have read and execute (r-x) permissions."},{"id":"c2-0233","module":17,"lesson":"17B Package & Network Mgmt","question":"What is being described here?\n\nThe Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 17: Supporting Other OS • 7: Owner has all rights (4+2+1) • 5: Group has read and execute (4+0+1) • 4: Others have read (4+0+0) The leading zero indicates octal format but can often be omitted. Another common combination","context":"The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 17: Supporting Other OS • 7: Owner has all rights (4+2+1) • 5: Group has read and execute (4+0+1) • 4: Others have read (4+0+0) The leading zero indicates octal format but can often be omitted. Another common combination","options":["Various keystrokes execute process management actions, including:","Bootloaders","Navigation Commands","For example, numeric permission 0754 translates to:"],"answer":3,"explanation":"For example, numeric permission 0754 translates to:: The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 17: Supporting Other OS • 7: Owner has all rights (4+2+1) • 5: Group has read and execute (4+0+1) • 4: Others have read (4+0+0) The leading zero indicates octal format but can often be omitted. Another common combination"},{"id":"c2-0234","module":17,"lesson":"17C macOS Features","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nchown Command The chown command allows the superuser or sudoers to change the owner of a file or directory. Regular users cannot use chown , even if they own the file, but they can change the group using the chgrp command.","context":"chown Command The chown command allows the superuser or sudoers to change the owner of a file or directory. Regular users cannot use chown , even if they own the file, but they can change the group using the chgrp command.","options":["Shells and Terminals","Modifying permissions using the chmod command","Network Management Commands","Linux"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Modifying permissions using the chmod command: chown Command The chown command allows the superuser or sudoers to change the owner of a file or directory. Regular users cannot use chown , even if they own the file, but they can change the group using the chgrp command."},{"id":"c2-0235","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\n1. Change the owner of a file: chown username file.txt 2. Change both the owner and group: chown username:groupname file.txt 3. Change ownership recursively for a directory and its contents: chown -R username:groupname /path/to/directory 4. Change only the group (using :): chown :groupname file.txt","context":"1. Change the owner of a file: chown username file.txt 2. Change both the owner and group: chown username:groupname file.txt 3. Change ownership recursively for a directory and its contents: chown -R username:groupname /path/to/directory 4. Change only the group (using :): chown :groupname file.txt","options":["Example usage:","Examples:","Desktop Environments","Search Commands"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Examples:: 1. Change the owner of a file: chown username file.txt 2. Change both the owner and group: chown username:groupname file.txt 3. Change ownership recursively for a directory and its contents: chown -R username:groupname /path/to/directory 4. Change only the group (using :): chown :groupname file.txt"},{"id":"c2-0236","module":17,"lesson":"17B Package & Network Mgmt","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\n3. Change ownership recursively for a directory and its contents: chown -R username:groupname /path/to/directory 4. Change only the group (using :): chown :groupname file.txt The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 17: Supporting Other OS","context":"3. Change ownership recursively for a directory and its contents: chown -R username:groupname /path/to/directory 4. Change only the group (using :): chown :groupname file.txt The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 17: Supporting Other OS","options":["Example entry in /etc/hosts:","Metacharacters and Escaping","Console Switching","2. Change both the owner and group: chown username:groupname file.txt"],"answer":3,"explanation":"2. Change both the owner and group: chown username:groupname file.txt: 3. Change ownership recursively for a directory and its contents: chown -R username:groupname /path/to/directory 4. Change only the group (using :): chown :groupname file.txt The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 17: Supporting Other OS"},{"id":"c2-0237","module":17,"lesson":"17C macOS Features","question":"What is being described here?\n\nA distribution includes precompiled software packages deemed appropriate by the vendor or sponsor. These packages, along with updates, are posted to software repositories. Vendors often maintain multiple repositories, such as stable, beta, and unsupported packages.","context":"A distribution includes precompiled software packages deemed appropriate by the vendor or sponsor. These packages, along with updates, are posted to software repositories. Vendors often maintain multiple repositories, such as stable, beta, and unsupported packages.","options":["Distributions and Repositories","Linux commands follow a standard format:","There are three ways to escape strings:","127.0.0.1 localhost"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Distributions and Repositories: A distribution includes precompiled software packages deemed appropriate by the vendor or sponsor. These packages, along with updates, are posted to software repositories. Vendors often maintain multiple repositories, such as stable, beta, and unsupported packages."},{"id":"c2-0238","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nPackage managers must be configured with the web addresses of desired repositories, typically done automatically during setup. They handle installing, uninstalling, and updating software. Package integrity is verified using cryptographic hashes or signatures, such as MD5,","context":"Package managers must be configured with the web addresses of desired repositories, typically done automatically during setup. They handle installing, uninstalling, and updating software. Package integrity is verified using cryptographic hashes or signatures, such as MD5,","options":["Case Sensitivity","Listing package manager sources in Ubuntu Linux","Shells, Terminals, and Consoles","Filesystem Management"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Listing package manager sources in Ubuntu Linux: Package managers must be configured with the web addresses of desired repositories, typically done automatically during setup. They handle installing, uninstalling, and updating software. Package integrity is verified using cryptographic hashes or signatures, such as MD5,"},{"id":"c2-0239","module":17,"lesson":"17B Package & Network Mgmt","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nEvery process in Linux is assigned a unique process ID (PID) upon starting, allowing the system and users to identify it. This PID is a non-negative integer that increments with each new process. PID 1 is reserved for the initial daemon, the first process to start, serving as the parent","context":"Every process in Linux is assigned a unique process ID (PID) upon starting, allowing the system and users to identify it. This PID is a non-negative integer that increments with each new process. PID 1 is reserved for the initial daemon, the first process to start, serving as the parent","options":["Help System","Bootloaders","Process Monitoring Commands","Key filesystem management tools include:"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Process Monitoring Commands: Every process in Linux is assigned a unique process ID (PID) upon starting, allowing the system and users to identify it. This PID is a non-negative integer that increments with each new process. PID 1 is reserved for the initial daemon, the first process to start, serving as the parent"},{"id":"c2-0240","module":17,"lesson":"17C macOS Features","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nVarious keystrokes execute process management actions, including: • ENTER: Refresh the status of all processes.","context":"Various keystrokes execute process management actions, including: • ENTER: Refresh the status of all processes.","options":["File Management Commands","Listing the state of running processes","Shells and Terminals","Text Editors"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Listing the state of running processes: Various keystrokes execute process management actions, including: • ENTER: Refresh the status of all processes."},{"id":"c2-0241","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"What is being described here?\n\n• ENTER: Refresh the status of all processes. • SHIFT+N: Sort processes in decreasing PID order.","context":"• ENTER: Refresh the status of all processes. • SHIFT+N: Sort processes in decreasing PID order.","options":["Navigation Commands","Various keystrokes execute process management actions, including:","User Account Management","Desktop Environments"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Various keystrokes execute process management actions, including:: • ENTER: Refresh the status of all processes. • SHIFT+N: Sort processes in decreasing PID order."},{"id":"c2-0242","module":17,"lesson":"17B Package & Network Mgmt","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nIn Linux, Ethernet interfaces were traditionally named eth0, eth1, etc., but modern distributions often use names like enp0s3 or ens33, based on the system's hardware topology.","context":"In Linux, Ethernet interfaces were traditionally named eth0, eth1, etc., but modern distributions often use names like enp0s3 or ens33, based on the system's hardware topology.","options":["Linux","User Management Commands","Console Switching","Network Management Commands"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Network Management Commands: In Linux, Ethernet interfaces were traditionally named eth0, eth1, etc., but modern distributions often use names like enp0s3 or ens33, based on the system's hardware topology."},{"id":"c2-0243","module":17,"lesson":"17C macOS Features","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nip addr show # Display all network interfaces and their IP addresses ip link set enp0s3 up # Bring up the network interface enp0s3 ip route show # Display the routing table /etc/hosts The/etc/hosts file is a simple text file that maps hostnames to IP addresses. It is used for local","context":"ip addr show # Display all network interfaces and their IP addresses ip link set enp0s3 up # Bring up the network interface enp0s3 ip route show # Display the routing table /etc/hosts The/etc/hosts file is a simple text file that maps hostnames to IP addresses. It is used for local","options":["Example usage:","Group Management Commands","Search Commands","Linux commands follow a standard format:"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Example usage:: ip addr show # Display all network interfaces and their IP addresses ip link set enp0s3 up # Bring up the network interface enp0s3 ip route show # Display the routing table /etc/hosts The/etc/hosts file is a simple text file that maps hostnames to IP addresses. It is used for local"},{"id":"c2-0244","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\n127.0.0.1 localhost 192.168.1.10 myserver.local /etc/resolv.conf The /etc/resolv.conf file contains information that defines how DNS (Domain Name System) resolution is handled. It specifies the DNS servers that the system should query to resolve domain names into IP addresses.","context":"127.0.0.1 localhost 192.168.1.10 myserver.local /etc/resolv.conf The /etc/resolv.conf file contains information that defines how DNS (Domain Name System) resolution is handled. It specifies the DNS servers that the system should query to resolve domain names into IP addresses.","options":["Metacharacters and Escaping","Case Sensitivity","File Permissions Commands","Example entry in /etc/hosts:"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Example entry in /etc/hosts:: 127.0.0.1 localhost 192.168.1.10 myserver.local /etc/resolv.conf The /etc/resolv.conf file contains information that defines how DNS (Domain Name System) resolution is handled. It specifies the DNS servers that the system should query to resolve domain names into IP addresses."},{"id":"c2-0245","module":17,"lesson":"17B Package & Network Mgmt","question":"What is being described here?\n\n192.168.1.10 myserver.local /etc/resolv.conf The /etc/resolv.conf file contains information that defines how DNS (Domain Name System) resolution is handled. It specifies the DNS servers that the system should query to resolve domain names into IP addresses.","context":"192.168.1.10 myserver.local /etc/resolv.conf The /etc/resolv.conf file contains information that defines how DNS (Domain Name System) resolution is handled. It specifies the DNS servers that the system should query to resolve domain names into IP addresses.","options":["For example, using ls -l for a long directory listing:","Help System","There are three ways to escape strings:","127.0.0.1 localhost"],"answer":3,"explanation":"127.0.0.1 localhost: 192.168.1.10 myserver.local /etc/resolv.conf The /etc/resolv.conf file contains information that defines how DNS (Domain Name System) resolution is handled. It specifies the DNS servers that the system should query to resolve domain names into IP addresses."},{"id":"c2-0246","module":18,"lesson":"18A Attacks & Threats","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nA vulnerability is some fault or weakness in a system that could be exploited by a threat actor. Vulnerabilities can arise due to a very wide range of causes. Some of these causes include improperly configured or installed hardware or software, delays in applying and","context":"A vulnerability is some fault or weakness in a system that could be exploited by a threat actor. Vulnerabilities can arise due to a very wide range of causes. Some of these causes include improperly configured or installed hardware or software, delays in applying and","options":["Cross-site Scripting Attacks","Vulnerabilities","Dumpster Diving","External versus Internal Threats"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Vulnerabilities: A vulnerability is some fault or weakness in a system that could be exploited by a threat actor. Vulnerabilities can arise due to a very wide range of causes. Some of these causes include improperly configured or installed hardware or software, delays in applying and"},{"id":"c2-0247","module":18,"lesson":"18B Wireless Security","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nA configuration baseline is a set of recommendations for deploying a computer in a hardened configuration to minimize the risk that there could be vulnerabilities. There are baselines for different operating systems and different server and client roles. For example, a web server","context":"A configuration baseline is a set of recommendations for deploying a computer in a hardened configuration to minimize the risk that there could be vulnerabilities. There are baselines for different operating systems and different server and client roles. For example, a web server","options":["Non-compliant Systems","Footprinting Threats","Shoulder Surfing","SQL Injection Attacks"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Non-compliant Systems: A configuration baseline is a set of recommendations for deploying a computer in a hardened configuration to minimize the risk that there could be vulnerabilities. There are baselines for different operating systems and different server and client roles. For example, a web server"},{"id":"c2-0248","module":18,"lesson":"18C SOHO Router Security","question":"What is being described here?\n\nA baseline will recommend specific technical security controls to ensure a secure configuration.","context":"A baseline will recommend specific technical security controls to ensure a secure configuration.","options":["Tailgating and Piggybacking","Wi-Fi Protected Access","Spoofing Threats","Unprotected Systems"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Unprotected Systems: A baseline will recommend specific technical security controls to ensure a secure configuration."},{"id":"c2-0249","module":18,"lesson":"18A Attacks & Threats","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nA software vulnerability is a fault in design or in code that can cause an application security system to be circumvented or that will cause the application to crash. The most serious vulnerabilities allow the attacker to execute arbitrary code on the system, which could allow","context":"A software vulnerability is a fault in design or in code that can cause an application security system to be circumvented or that will cause the application to crash. The most serious vulnerabilities allow the attacker to execute arbitrary code on the system, which could allow","options":["Software and Zero-day Vulnerabilities","On-path Attacks","Phishing and Evil Twins","Temporal Key Integrity Protocol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Software and Zero-day Vulnerabilities: A software vulnerability is a fault in design or in code that can cause an application security system to be circumvented or that will cause the application to crash. The most serious vulnerabilities allow the attacker to execute arbitrary code on the system, which could allow"},{"id":"c2-0250","module":18,"lesson":"18B Wireless Security","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nWhile zero-day exploits can be extremely destructive, they are relatively rare events. A greater threat is the large number of unpatched or legacy systems in use.","context":"While zero-day exploits can be extremely destructive, they are relatively rare events. A greater threat is the large number of unpatched or legacy systems in use.","options":["Denial of Service Attack","Unpatched and End of Life Operating Systems","Some phishing variants are referred to by specific names:","Advanced Encryption Standard (AES)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Unpatched and End of Life Operating Systems: While zero-day exploits can be extremely destructive, they are relatively rare events. A greater threat is the large number of unpatched or legacy systems in use."},{"id":"c2-0251","module":18,"lesson":"18C SOHO Router Security","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nBring your own device is a provisioning model that allows employees to use personal mobile devices to access corporate systems and data. In this scenario, it is very difficult for the security team to identify secure configuration baselines for each type of device and mobile","context":"Bring your own device is a provisioning model that allows employees to use personal mobile devices to access corporate systems and data. In this scenario, it is very difficult for the security team to identify secure configuration baselines for each type of device and mobile","options":["Threat Types","Distributed DoS Attacks and Botnets","WPA2","Bring Your Own Device Vulnerabilities"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Bring Your Own Device Vulnerabilities: Bring your own device is a provisioning model that allows employees to use personal mobile devices to access corporate systems and data. In this scenario, it is very difficult for the security team to identify secure configuration baselines for each type of device and mobile"},{"id":"c2-0252","module":18,"lesson":"18A Attacks & Threats","question":"What is being described here?\n\nThreat actors can use a diverse range of techniques to compromise a security system. A prerequisite of many types of attacks is to obtain information about the network and its security controls. Social engineering—or hacking the human—refers to techniques that persuade or","context":"Threat actors can use a diverse range of techniques to compromise a security system. A prerequisite of many types of attacks is to obtain information about the network and its security controls. Social engineering—or hacking the human—refers to techniques that persuade or","options":["Social Engineering","Wi-Fi Authentication Methods","Password Attacks","External versus Internal Threats"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Social Engineering: Threat actors can use a diverse range of techniques to compromise a security system. A prerequisite of many types of attacks is to obtain information about the network and its security controls. Social engineering—or hacking the human—refers to techniques that persuade or"},{"id":"c2-0253","module":18,"lesson":"18B Wireless Security","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nImpersonation means that the social engineer develops a pretext scenario to allow himself or herself an opportunity to interact with an employee. A classic impersonation pretext is for the threat actor to phone into a department pretending to be calling from IT support, claim","context":"Impersonation means that the social engineer develops a pretext scenario to allow himself or herself an opportunity to interact with an employee. A classic impersonation pretext is for the threat actor to phone into a department pretending to be calling from IT support, claim","options":["Footprinting Threats","WPA2 Pre-Shared Key Authentication","Impersonation","Hashcat password cracking utility"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Impersonation: Impersonation means that the social engineer develops a pretext scenario to allow himself or herself an opportunity to interact with an employee. A classic impersonation pretext is for the threat actor to phone into a department pretending to be calling from IT support, claim"},{"id":"c2-0254","module":18,"lesson":"18C SOHO Router Security","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nTo make a pretext seem genuine, the threat actor must obtain privileged information about the organization or an individual. For example, an impersonation pretext is much more effective if the attacker knows the user's name. As most companies are set up toward customer","context":"To make a pretext seem genuine, the threat actor must obtain privileged information about the organization or an individual. For example, an impersonation pretext is much more effective if the attacker knows the user's name. As most companies are set up toward customer","options":["Cross-site Scripting Attacks","WPA3 Personal Authentication","Dumpster Diving","Spoofing Threats"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Dumpster Diving: To make a pretext seem genuine, the threat actor must obtain privileged information about the organization or an individual. For example, an impersonation pretext is much more effective if the attacker knows the user's name. As most companies are set up toward customer"},{"id":"c2-0255","module":18,"lesson":"18A Attacks & Threats","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nA shoulder surfing attack means that the threat actor learns a password or PIN (or other secure information) by watching the user type it. Despite the name, the attacker may not have to be in proximity to the target—they could use high-powered binoculars or CCTV to directly observe","context":"A shoulder surfing attack means that the threat actor learns a password or PIN (or other secure information) by watching the user type it. Despite the name, the attacker may not have to be in proximity to the target—they could use high-powered binoculars or CCTV to directly observe","options":["Shoulder Surfing","SQL Injection Attacks","Enterprise Authentication Protocols","On-path Attacks"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Shoulder Surfing: A shoulder surfing attack means that the threat actor learns a password or PIN (or other secure information) by watching the user type it. Despite the name, the attacker may not have to be in proximity to the target—they could use high-powered binoculars or CCTV to directly observe"},{"id":"c2-0256","module":18,"lesson":"18B Wireless Security","question":"What is being described here?\n\nTailgaiting is a means of entering a secure area without authorization by following closely behind the person who has been allowed to open the door or checkpoint. Piggybacking is a similar situation but means that the attacker enters a secure area with an employee's","context":"Tailgaiting is a means of entering a secure area without authorization by following closely behind the person who has been allowed to open the door or checkpoint. Piggybacking is a similar situation but means that the attacker enters a secure area with an employee's","options":["Denial of Service Attack","Enterprise authentication uses the following general workflow:","Tailgating and Piggybacking","Wi-Fi Protected Access"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Tailgating and Piggybacking: Tailgaiting is a means of entering a secure area without authorization by following closely behind the person who has been allowed to open the door or checkpoint. Piggybacking is a similar situation but means that the attacker enters a secure area with an employee's"},{"id":"c2-0257","module":18,"lesson":"18C SOHO Router Security","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nPhishing uses social engineering techniques to make spoofed electronic communications seem authentic to the victim. A phishing message might try to convince the user to perform some action, such as installing malware disguised as an antivirus program or allowing a threat","context":"Phishing uses social engineering techniques to make spoofed electronic communications seem authentic to the victim. A phishing message might try to convince the user to perform some action, such as installing malware disguised as an antivirus program or allowing a threat","options":["Distributed DoS Attacks and Botnets","Phishing and Evil Twins","RADIUS, TACACS+, and Kerberos","Temporal Key Integrity Protocol"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Phishing and Evil Twins: Phishing uses social engineering techniques to make spoofed electronic communications seem authentic to the victim. A phishing message might try to convince the user to perform some action, such as installing malware disguised as an antivirus program or allowing a threat"},{"id":"c2-0258","module":18,"lesson":"18A Attacks & Threats","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\n• Spear-phishing occurs when the attacker has some information that makes the target more likely to be fooled by the attack. The threat actor might know the name of a document that the target is editing, for instance, and send a malicious copy, or the phishing email might","context":"• Spear-phishing occurs when the attacker has some information that makes the target more likely to be fooled by the attack. The threat actor might know the name of a document that the target is editing, for instance, and send a malicious copy, or the phishing email might","options":["Some phishing variants are referred to by specific names:","Password Attacks","Advanced Encryption Standard (AES)","RADIUS"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Some phishing variants are referred to by specific names:: • Spear-phishing occurs when the attacker has some information that makes the target more likely to be fooled by the attack. The threat actor might know the name of a document that the target is editing, for instance, and send a malicious copy, or the phishing email might"},{"id":"c2-0259","module":18,"lesson":"18B Wireless Security","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nHistorically, cybersecurity techniques were highly dependent on the identification of \"static\" known threats, such as computer viruses. This type of threat leaves a programming code signature in the file that it infects that is relatively straightforward to identify with automated","context":"Historically, cybersecurity techniques were highly dependent on the identification of \"static\" known threats, such as computer viruses. This type of threat leaves a programming code signature in the file that it infects that is relatively straightforward to identify with automated","options":["WPA2","Threat Types","Hashcat password cracking utility","Vulnerabilities"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Threat Types: Historically, cybersecurity techniques were highly dependent on the identification of \"static\" known threats, such as computer viruses. This type of threat leaves a programming code signature in the file that it infects that is relatively straightforward to identify with automated"},{"id":"c2-0260","module":18,"lesson":"18C SOHO Router Security","question":"What is being described here?\n\nAn external threat actor is one who has no account or authorized access to the target system.","context":"An external threat actor is one who has no account or authorized access to the target system.","options":["Wi-Fi Authentication Methods","Cross-site Scripting Attacks","Non-compliant Systems","External versus Internal Threats"],"answer":3,"explanation":"External versus Internal Threats: An external threat actor is one who has no account or authorized access to the target system."},{"id":"c2-0261","module":18,"lesson":"18A Attacks & Threats","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nFootprinting is an information-gathering threat in which the attacker attempts to learn about the configuration of the network and security systems. A threat actor will perform reconnaissance and research about the target, gathering publicly available information, scanning network ports","context":"Footprinting is an information-gathering threat in which the attacker attempts to learn about the configuration of the network and security systems. A threat actor will perform reconnaissance and research about the target, gathering publicly available information, scanning network ports","options":["Footprinting Threats","SQL Injection Attacks","WPA2 Pre-Shared Key Authentication","Unprotected Systems"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Footprinting Threats: Footprinting is an information-gathering threat in which the attacker attempts to learn about the configuration of the network and security systems. A threat actor will perform reconnaissance and research about the target, gathering publicly available information, scanning network ports"},{"id":"c2-0262","module":18,"lesson":"18B Wireless Security","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nA spoofing threat is any type of attack where the threat actor can masquerade as a trusted user or computer. Spoofing can mean cloning a valid MAC or IP address, using a false digital certificate, creating an email message that imitates a legitimate one, or performing social","context":"A spoofing threat is any type of attack where the threat actor can masquerade as a trusted user or computer. Spoofing can mean cloning a valid MAC or IP address, using a false digital certificate, creating an email message that imitates a legitimate one, or performing social","options":["Spoofing Threats","Software and Zero-day Vulnerabilities","Wi-Fi Protected Access","WPA3 Personal Authentication"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Spoofing Threats: A spoofing threat is any type of attack where the threat actor can masquerade as a trusted user or computer. Spoofing can mean cloning a valid MAC or IP address, using a false digital certificate, creating an email message that imitates a legitimate one, or performing social"},{"id":"c2-0263","module":18,"lesson":"18C SOHO Router Security","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nAn is a specific type of spoofing where the threat actor can covertly intercept traffic between two hosts or networks. This allows the threat actor to read and possibly modify the packets. An on-path attack is often designed to try to recover password hashes. An evil twin is one example","context":"An is a specific type of spoofing where the threat actor can covertly intercept traffic between two hosts or networks. This allows the threat actor to read and possibly modify the packets. An on-path attack is often designed to try to recover password hashes. An evil twin is one example","options":["On-path Attacks","Enterprise Authentication Protocols","Temporal Key Integrity Protocol","Unpatched and End of Life Operating Systems"],"answer":0,"explanation":"On-path Attacks: An is a specific type of spoofing where the threat actor can covertly intercept traffic between two hosts or networks. This allows the threat actor to read and possibly modify the packets. An on-path attack is often designed to try to recover password hashes. An evil twin is one example"},{"id":"c2-0264","module":18,"lesson":"18A Attacks & Threats","question":"What is being described here?\n\nA denial of service attack attack causes a service at a given host to fail or to become unavailable to legitimate users. Typically, a DoS attack tries to overload a service by bombarding it with spoofed requests. It is also possible for DoS attacks to exploit design failures","context":"A denial of service attack attack causes a service at a given host to fail or to become unavailable to legitimate users. Typically, a DoS attack tries to overload a service by bombarding it with spoofed requests. It is also possible for DoS attacks to exploit design failures","options":["Advanced Encryption Standard (AES)","Denial of Service Attack","Enterprise authentication uses the following general workflow:","Bring Your Own Device Vulnerabilities"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Denial of Service Attack: A denial of service attack attack causes a service at a given host to fail or to become unavailable to legitimate users. Typically, a DoS attack tries to overload a service by bombarding it with spoofed requests. It is also possible for DoS attacks to exploit design failures"},{"id":"c2-0265","module":18,"lesson":"18B Wireless Security","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nNetwork-based DoS attacks are normally accomplished by flooding the server with bogus requests. They rely on the attacker having access to greater bandwidth than the target or on the target being required to devote more resources to each connection than the attacker. This","context":"Network-based DoS attacks are normally accomplished by flooding the server with bogus requests. They rely on the attacker having access to greater bandwidth than the target or on the target being required to devote more resources to each connection than the attacker. This","options":["Social Engineering","Distributed DoS Attacks and Botnets","RADIUS, TACACS+, and Kerberos","WPA2"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Distributed DoS Attacks and Botnets: Network-based DoS attacks are normally accomplished by flooding the server with bogus requests. They rely on the attacker having access to greater bandwidth than the target or on the target being required to devote more resources to each connection than the attacker. This"},{"id":"c2-0266","module":18,"lesson":"18C SOHO Router Security","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nOn-path and malware attacks can be difficult to perpetrate. Many network intrusions occur because a threat actor simply obtains credentials to access the network. Also, when threat actors gain some sort of access via an on-path or malware attack, they are likely to attempt to","context":"On-path and malware attacks can be difficult to perpetrate. Many network intrusions occur because a threat actor simply obtains credentials to access the network. Also, when threat actors gain some sort of access via an on-path or malware attack, they are likely to attempt to","options":["RADIUS","Password Attacks","Impersonation","Wi-Fi Authentication Methods"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Password Attacks: On-path and malware attacks can be difficult to perpetrate. Many network intrusions occur because a threat actor simply obtains credentials to access the network. Also, when threat actors gain some sort of access via an on-path or malware attack, they are likely to attempt to"},{"id":"c2-0267","module":18,"lesson":"18A Attacks & Threats","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nCross-site Scripting Attacks Many network services are now deployed as web applications. The HTTP/HTTPS web protocol is based on servers responding to client requests. A developer can extend the basic protocol with software code and information stored in databases to implement a dynamic web app,","context":"Cross-site Scripting Attacks Many network services are now deployed as web applications. The HTTP/HTTPS web protocol is based on servers responding to client requests. A developer can extend the basic protocol with software code and information stored in databases to implement a dynamic web app,","options":["Hashcat password cracking utility","Dumpster Diving","Vulnerabilities","WPA2 Pre-Shared Key Authentication"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Hashcat password cracking utility: Cross-site Scripting Attacks Many network services are now deployed as web applications. The HTTP/HTTPS web protocol is based on servers responding to client requests. A developer can extend the basic protocol with software code and information stored in databases to implement a dynamic web app,"},{"id":"c2-0268","module":18,"lesson":"18B Wireless Security","question":"What is being described here?\n\nMany network services are now deployed as web applications. The HTTP/HTTPS web protocol is based on servers responding to client requests. A developer can extend the basic protocol with software code and information stored in databases to implement a dynamic web app,","context":"Many network services are now deployed as web applications. The HTTP/HTTPS web protocol is based on servers responding to client requests. A developer can extend the basic protocol with software code and information stored in databases to implement a dynamic web app,","options":["Non-compliant Systems","Cross-site Scripting Attacks","Shoulder Surfing","WPA3 Personal Authentication"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cross-site Scripting Attacks: Many network services are now deployed as web applications. The HTTP/HTTPS web protocol is based on servers responding to client requests. A developer can extend the basic protocol with software code and information stored in databases to implement a dynamic web app,"},{"id":"c2-0269","module":18,"lesson":"18C SOHO Router Security","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nA web application is likely to use Structured Query Language (SQL) to read and write information from a database. SQL statements perform operations such as selecting data (SELECT), inserting data (INSERT), deleting data (DELETE), and updating data (UPDATE). In","context":"A web application is likely to use Structured Query Language (SQL) to read and write information from a database. SQL statements perform operations such as selecting data (SELECT), inserting data (INSERT), deleting data (DELETE), and updating data (UPDATE). In","options":["Unprotected Systems","SQL Injection Attacks","Tailgating and Piggybacking","Enterprise Authentication Protocols"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SQL Injection Attacks: A web application is likely to use Structured Query Language (SQL) to read and write information from a database. SQL statements perform operations such as selecting data (SELECT), inserting data (INSERT), deleting data (DELETE), and updating data (UPDATE). In"},{"id":"c2-0270","module":18,"lesson":"18A Attacks & Threats","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nWireless LANs require careful configuration to make the connection and transmissions over the link secure. The main problem with wireless is that because it is unguided, there is no way to prevent anything within range from listening to the signals. If the wireless traffic is unencrypted,","context":"Wireless LANs require careful configuration to make the connection and transmissions over the link secure. The main problem with wireless is that because it is unguided, there is no way to prevent anything within range from listening to the signals. If the wireless traffic is unencrypted,","options":["Enterprise authentication uses the following general workflow:","Wi-Fi Protected Access","Software and Zero-day Vulnerabilities","Phishing and Evil Twins"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Wi-Fi Protected Access: Wireless LANs require careful configuration to make the connection and transmissions over the link secure. The main problem with wireless is that because it is unguided, there is no way to prevent anything within range from listening to the signals. If the wireless traffic is unencrypted,"},{"id":"c2-0271","module":18,"lesson":"18B Wireless Security","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nThe first version of Wi-Fi Protected Access was designed to fix critical vulnerabilities in the earlier wired equivalent privacy (WEP) standard. Like WEP, version 1 of WPA uses the RC4 symmetric cipher to encrypt traffic but adds a mechanism called the Temporal Key Integrity","context":"The first version of Wi-Fi Protected Access was designed to fix critical vulnerabilities in the earlier wired equivalent privacy (WEP) standard. Like WEP, version 1 of WPA uses the RC4 symmetric cipher to encrypt traffic but adds a mechanism called the Temporal Key Integrity","options":["Unpatched and End of Life Operating Systems","Some phishing variants are referred to by specific names:","Temporal Key Integrity Protocol","RADIUS, TACACS+, and Kerberos"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Temporal Key Integrity Protocol: The first version of Wi-Fi Protected Access was designed to fix critical vulnerabilities in the earlier wired equivalent privacy (WEP) standard. Like WEP, version 1 of WPA uses the RC4 symmetric cipher to encrypt traffic but adds a mechanism called the Temporal Key Integrity"},{"id":"c2-0272","module":18,"lesson":"18C SOHO Router Security","question":"What is being described here?\n\nAdvanced Encryption Standard (AES) is an encryption method that uses symmetric keys and block ciphers to encrypt data. This means that the data is divided into blocks of 128-bits and each block is encrypted independently.","context":"Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) is an encryption method that uses symmetric keys and block ciphers to encrypt data. This means that the data is divided into blocks of 128-bits and each block is encrypted independently.","options":["Bring Your Own Device Vulnerabilities","Advanced Encryption Standard (AES)","Threat Types","RADIUS"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Advanced Encryption Standard (AES): Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) is an encryption method that uses symmetric keys and block ciphers to encrypt data. This means that the data is divided into blocks of 128-bits and each block is encrypted independently."},{"id":"c2-0273","module":18,"lesson":"18A Attacks & Threats","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nNeither WEP nor the original WPA version is considered secure enough for continued use.","context":"Neither WEP nor the original WPA version is considered secure enough for continued use.","options":["WPA2","Vulnerabilities","External versus Internal Threats","Social Engineering"],"answer":0,"explanation":"WPA2: Neither WEP nor the original WPA version is considered secure enough for continued use."},{"id":"c2-0274","module":18,"lesson":"18B Wireless Security","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nWi-Fi authentication comes in three types: open, personal, and enterprise. Within the personal authentication category, there are two methods: WPA2 pre-shared key (PSK) authentication and WPA3 Simultaneous Authentication of Equals.","context":"Wi-Fi authentication comes in three types: open, personal, and enterprise. Within the personal authentication category, there are two methods: WPA2 pre-shared key (PSK) authentication and WPA3 Simultaneous Authentication of Equals.","options":["Non-compliant Systems","Footprinting Threats","Impersonation","Wi-Fi Authentication Methods"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Wi-Fi Authentication Methods: Wi-Fi authentication comes in three types: open, personal, and enterprise. Within the personal authentication category, there are two methods: WPA2 pre-shared key (PSK) authentication and WPA3 Simultaneous Authentication of Equals."},{"id":"c2-0275","module":18,"lesson":"18C SOHO Router Security","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nIn WPA2, pre-shared key authentication uses a passphrase to generate the key that is used to encrypt communications. It is also referred to as group authentication because a group of users shares the same passphrase. When the access point is set to WPA2-PSK mode, the","context":"In WPA2, pre-shared key authentication uses a passphrase to generate the key that is used to encrypt communications. It is also referred to as group authentication because a group of users shares the same passphrase. When the access point is set to WPA2-PSK mode, the","options":["WPA2 Pre-Shared Key Authentication","Dumpster Diving","Unprotected Systems","Spoofing Threats"],"answer":0,"explanation":"WPA2 Pre-Shared Key Authentication: In WPA2, pre-shared key authentication uses a passphrase to generate the key that is used to encrypt communications. It is also referred to as group authentication because a group of users shares the same passphrase. When the access point is set to WPA2-PSK mode, the"},{"id":"c2-0276","module":18,"lesson":"18A Attacks & Threats","question":"What is being described here?\n\nWhile WPA3 still uses passphrase-based group authentication of stations in personal mode, it changes the method by which this secret is used to agree session keys. In WPA3, the simultaneous authentication of equals (SAE) protocol replaces the PSK mechanism.","context":"While WPA3 still uses passphrase-based group authentication of stations in personal mode, it changes the method by which this secret is used to agree session keys. In WPA3, the simultaneous authentication of equals (SAE) protocol replaces the PSK mechanism.","options":["WPA3 Personal Authentication","Shoulder Surfing","On-path Attacks","Software and Zero-day Vulnerabilities"],"answer":0,"explanation":"WPA3 Personal Authentication: While WPA3 still uses passphrase-based group authentication of stations in personal mode, it changes the method by which this secret is used to agree session keys. In WPA3, the simultaneous authentication of equals (SAE) protocol replaces the PSK mechanism."},{"id":"c2-0277","module":18,"lesson":"18B Wireless Security","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nThe main problems with personal modes of authentication are that the distribution of the passphrase cannot be secured properly and that the access point administrator may choose an insecure passphrase. Personal authentication also fails to provide accounting because all","context":"The main problems with personal modes of authentication are that the distribution of the passphrase cannot be secured properly and that the access point administrator may choose an insecure passphrase. Personal authentication also fails to provide accounting because all","options":["Denial of Service Attack","Unpatched and End of Life Operating Systems","Tailgating and Piggybacking","Enterprise Authentication Protocols"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Enterprise Authentication Protocols: The main problems with personal modes of authentication are that the distribution of the passphrase cannot be secured properly and that the access point administrator may choose an insecure passphrase. Personal authentication also fails to provide accounting because all"},{"id":"c2-0278","module":18,"lesson":"18C SOHO Router Security","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\n1. When a wireless station (a supplicant) requests an association, the AP enables the channel for EAPoW traffic only.","context":"1. When a wireless station (a supplicant) requests an association, the AP enables the channel for EAPoW traffic only.","options":["Enterprise authentication uses the following general workflow:","Phishing and Evil Twins","Distributed DoS Attacks and Botnets","Bring Your Own Device Vulnerabilities"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Enterprise authentication uses the following general workflow:: 1. When a wireless station (a supplicant) requests an association, the AP enables the channel for EAPoW traffic only."},{"id":"c2-0279","module":18,"lesson":"18A Attacks & Threats","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nEnterprise authentication uses an AAA server and network directory. These components can be implemented by several different protocols.","context":"Enterprise authentication uses an AAA server and network directory. These components can be implemented by several different protocols.","options":["RADIUS, TACACS+, and Kerberos","Password Attacks","Some phishing variants are referred to by specific names:","Social Engineering"],"answer":0,"explanation":"RADIUS, TACACS+, and Kerberos: Enterprise authentication uses an AAA server and network directory. These components can be implemented by several different protocols."},{"id":"c2-0280","module":18,"lesson":"18B Wireless Security","question":"What is being described here?\n\nRemote Authentication Dial-in User Service is one way of implementing the AAA server when configuring enterprise authentication. The wireless access point is configured as a client of the RADIUS server. Rather than storing and validating user credentials directly, it forwards this","context":"Remote Authentication Dial-in User Service is one way of implementing the AAA server when configuring enterprise authentication. The wireless access point is configured as a client of the RADIUS server. Rather than storing and validating user credentials directly, it forwards this","options":["Impersonation","RADIUS","Hashcat password cracking utility","Threat Types"],"answer":1,"explanation":"RADIUS: Remote Authentication Dial-in User Service is one way of implementing the AAA server when configuring enterprise authentication. The wireless access point is configured as a client of the RADIUS server. Rather than storing and validating user credentials directly, it forwards this"},{"id":"c2-0281","module":19,"lesson":"19A Account Security","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nOne of the first pillars of workstation security is ensuring that only authorized users can operate the computers connected to the network. Effective user security depends on strong credential management, effective account policies, and best practice end-user behavior.","context":"One of the first pillars of workstation security is ensuring that only authorized users can operate the computers connected to the network. Effective user security depends on strong credential management, effective account policies, and best practice end-user behavior.","options":["Trusted/Untrusted Software Sources","Encrypting File System","Browser Extensions and Plug-ins","Password Best Practices"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Password Best Practices: One of the first pillars of workstation security is ensuring that only authorized users can operate the computers connected to the network. Effective user security depends on strong credential management, effective account policies, and best practice end-user behavior."},{"id":"c2-0282","module":19,"lesson":"19B Workstation Security","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nThe following rules are easy for users to apply and make passwords more difficult to crack: • Make the password sufficiently long—12+ character length is suitable for an ordinary user account. Administrative accounts should have longer passwords.","context":"The following rules are easy for users to apply and make passwords more difficult to crack: • Make the password sufficiently long—12+ character length is suitable for an ordinary user account. Administrative accounts should have longer passwords.","options":["Add-ons come in several different types:","Windows BitLocker and BitLocker To Go","Password Rules","AutoRun and AutoPlay"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Password Rules: The following rules are easy for users to apply and make passwords more difficult to crack: • Make the password sufficiently long—12+ character length is suitable for an ordinary user account. Administrative accounts should have longer passwords."},{"id":"c2-0283","module":19,"lesson":"19C Browser Security","question":"What is being described here?\n\nAccount management policies are used to determine what rights and privileges each employee should be assigned. These policies should be guided by the principle of least privilege.","context":"Account management policies are used to determine what rights and privileges each employee should be assigned. These policies should be guided by the principle of least privilege.","options":["Restrict User Permissions","Browser Selection and Installation","Windows Defender Antivirus","Browser Patching"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Restrict User Permissions: Account management policies are used to determine what rights and privileges each employee should be assigned. These policies should be guided by the principle of least privilege."},{"id":"c2-0284","module":19,"lesson":"19A Account Security","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nThe root or superuser in Linux or the Administrator user account in Windows is the default system owner. These default accounts have no practical limitations and consequently are the ultimate target for threat actors. In many cases, these default accounts are disabled during the","context":"The root or superuser in Linux or the Administrator user account in Windows is the default system owner. These default accounts have no practical limitations and consequently are the ultimate target for threat actors. In many cases, these default accounts are disabled during the","options":["Windows Defender Antivirus Updated Definitions","Trusted Sources","Secure Connections and Valid Certificates","Change Default Administrator Account and Password"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Change Default Administrator Account and Password: The root or superuser in Linux or the Administrator user account in Windows is the default system owner. These default accounts have no practical limitations and consequently are the ultimate target for threat actors. In many cases, these default accounts are disabled during the"},{"id":"c2-0285","module":19,"lesson":"19B Workstation Security","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nA guest account allows unauthenticated access to the computer and may provide some sort of network access too. In current versions of Windows, the Guest account is disabled by default and cannot be used to sign-in. It is only enabled to facilitate password-less file sharing in a","context":"A guest account allows unauthenticated access to the computer and may provide some sort of network access too. In current versions of Windows, the Guest account is disabled by default and cannot be used to sign-in. It is only enabled to facilitate password-less file sharing in a","options":["Activating and Deactivating Windows Defender Antivirus","Untrusted Sources","Browser Privacy Settings","Disable Guest Account"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Disable Guest Account: A guest account allows unauthenticated access to the computer and may provide some sort of network access too. In current versions of Windows, the Guest account is disabled by default and cannot be used to sign-in. It is only enabled to facilitate password-less file sharing in a"},{"id":"c2-0286","module":19,"lesson":"19C Browser Security","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nAccount policies supplement best practice behavior by enforcing requirements as controls imposed by the OS. On a standalone workstation, password and account policies can be configured via the Local Security Policy snap-in ( secpol.msc) or the Group Policy Editor","context":"Account policies supplement best practice behavior by enforcing requirements as controls imposed by the OS. On a standalone workstation, password and account policies can be configured via the Local Security Policy snap-in ( secpol.msc) or the Group Policy Editor","options":["Account Policies","Browser Settings","Windows Defender Firewall","Malware Vectors"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Account Policies: Account policies supplement best practice behavior by enforcing requirements as controls imposed by the OS. On a standalone workstation, password and account policies can be configured via the Local Security Policy snap-in ( secpol.msc) or the Group Policy Editor"},{"id":"c2-0287","module":19,"lesson":"19A Account Security","question":"What is being described here?\n\nServices are used by the operating system and hardware to increase the functionality of the computer. Some services you may be familiar with may include update services, file and print services, and even network services like DHCP and DNS.","context":"Services are used by the operating system and hardware to increase the functionality of the computer. Some services you may be familiar with may include update services, file and print services, and even network services like DHCP and DNS.","options":["Sign-in and Browser Data Synchronization","Malware Payloads","Unused Services","Encrypting File System"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Unused Services: Services are used by the operating system and hardware to increase the functionality of the computer. Some services you may be familiar with may include update services, file and print services, and even network services like DHCP and DNS."},{"id":"c2-0288","module":19,"lesson":"19B Workstation Security","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nAuthentication and authorization policies give subjects the right to sign on to a computer and network and (potentially) to make changes to the system configuration. This places a certain amount of trust in the user to exercise those rights responsibly. Users can act maliciously,","context":"Authentication and authorization policies give subjects the right to sign on to a computer and network and (potentially) to make changes to the system configuration. This places a certain amount of trust in the user to exercise those rights responsibly. Users can act maliciously,","options":["Password Manager","Windows BitLocker and BitLocker To Go","Backdoors","Execution Control"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Execution Control: Authentication and authorization policies give subjects the right to sign on to a computer and network and (potentially) to make changes to the system configuration. This places a certain amount of trust in the user to exercise those rights responsibly. Users can act maliciously,"},{"id":"c2-0289","module":19,"lesson":"19C Browser Security","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nTo prevent the spread of malware such as Trojans, it is necessary to restrict the ability of users to run unapproved program code, especially code that can modify the OS, such as an application installer. Windows uses the system of Administrator and Standard user accounts,","context":"To prevent the spread of malware such as Trojans, it is necessary to restrict the ability of users to run unapproved program code, especially code that can modify the OS, such as an application installer. Windows uses the system of Administrator and Standard user accounts,","options":["Browser Extensions and Plug-ins","Browser Selection and Installation","Adware","Trusted/Untrusted Software Sources"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Trusted/Untrusted Software Sources: To prevent the spread of malware such as Trojans, it is necessary to restrict the ability of users to run unapproved program code, especially code that can modify the OS, such as an application installer. Windows uses the system of Administrator and Standard user accounts,"},{"id":"c2-0290","module":19,"lesson":"19A Account Security","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nOne of the problems with legacy versions of Windows is that when an optical disc is inserted or a USB drive is attached, Windows would automatically run commands defined in an autorun.inf file stored in the root of the drive. A typical autorun.inf would define an icon for a disk and","context":"One of the problems with legacy versions of Windows is that when an optical disc is inserted or a USB drive is attached, Windows would automatically run commands defined in an autorun.inf file stored in the root of the drive. A typical autorun.inf would define an icon for a disk and","options":["Add-ons come in several different types:","AutoRun and AutoPlay","Spyware and Keyloggers","Trusted Sources"],"answer":1,"explanation":"AutoRun and AutoPlay: One of the problems with legacy versions of Windows is that when an optical disc is inserted or a USB drive is attached, Windows would automatically run commands defined in an autorun.inf file stored in the root of the drive. A typical autorun.inf would define an icon for a disk and"},{"id":"c2-0291","module":19,"lesson":"19B Workstation Security","question":"What is being described here?\n\nEven with UAC and execution control, there are still plenty of ways for malware to install onto a PC. A program might use particularly effective social engineering techniques to persuade the user to bypass the normal checks. The malware might exploit a vulnerability to execute without","context":"Even with UAC and execution control, there are still plenty of ways for malware to install onto a PC. A program might use particularly effective social engineering techniques to persuade the user to bypass the normal checks. The malware might exploit a vulnerability to execute without","options":["Browser Patching","Windows Defender Antivirus","Untrusted Sources","Rootkits"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Windows Defender Antivirus: Even with UAC and execution control, there are still plenty of ways for malware to install onto a PC. A program might use particularly effective social engineering techniques to persuade the user to bypass the normal checks. The malware might exploit a vulnerability to execute without"},{"id":"c2-0292","module":19,"lesson":"19C Browser Security","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nIt is particularly important that antivirus software be updated regularly. Two types of updates are generally necessary: • Definitions are information about new viruses or malware. These updates may be made available daily or even hourly.","context":"It is particularly important that antivirus software be updated regularly. Two types of updates are generally necessary: • Definitions are information about new viruses or malware. These updates may be made available daily or even hourly.","options":["Ransomware and Cryptominers","Secure Connections and Valid Certificates","Browser Settings","Windows Defender Antivirus Updated Definitions"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Windows Defender Antivirus Updated Definitions: It is particularly important that antivirus software be updated regularly. Two types of updates are generally necessary: • Definitions are information about new viruses or malware. These updates may be made available daily or even hourly."},{"id":"c2-0293","module":19,"lesson":"19A Account Security","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nThe nature of malware means that there should be no simple means of deactivating an antivirus product, or the malware could easily circumvent it. Defender Antivirus can be disabled temporarily by toggling the Real-time protection button. It will re-activate itself after a short","context":"The nature of malware means that there should be no simple means of deactivating an antivirus product, or the malware could easily circumvent it. Defender Antivirus can be disabled temporarily by toggling the Real-time protection button. It will re-activate itself after a short","options":["Troubleshoot PC Security Symptoms","Sign-in and Browser Data Synchronization","Activating and Deactivating Windows Defender Antivirus","Browser Privacy Settings"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Activating and Deactivating Windows Defender Antivirus: The nature of malware means that there should be no simple means of deactivating an antivirus product, or the malware could easily circumvent it. Defender Antivirus can be disabled temporarily by toggling the Real-time protection button. It will re-activate itself after a short"},{"id":"c2-0294","module":19,"lesson":"19B Workstation Security","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nWhere the antivirus product protects against threats in the file system, Windows Defender Firewall implements a personal/host firewall to filter inbound and outbound network traffic. The basic Settings app interface allows you to activate or deactivate the firewall for a given network","context":"Where the antivirus product protects against threats in the file system, Windows Defender Firewall implements a personal/host firewall to filter inbound and outbound network traffic. The basic Settings app interface allows you to activate or deactivate the firewall for a given network","options":["Malware Vectors","Password Manager","Windows Defender Firewall","Password Best Practices"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Windows Defender Firewall: Where the antivirus product protects against threats in the file system, Windows Defender Firewall implements a personal/host firewall to filter inbound and outbound network traffic. The basic Settings app interface allows you to activate or deactivate the firewall for a given network"},{"id":"c2-0295","module":19,"lesson":"19C Browser Security","question":"What is being described here?\n\nWhen data is hosted on a file system, it can be protected by the operating system's security model. Each file or folder can be configured with an access control list (ACL), describing the permissions that principals have on the file. These permissions are enforced only when the OS","context":"When data is hosted on a file system, it can be protected by the operating system's security model. Each file or folder can be configured with an access control list (ACL), describing the permissions that principals have on the file. These permissions are enforced only when the OS","options":["Encrypting File System","Malware Payloads","Browser Extensions and Plug-ins","Password Rules"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Encrypting File System: When data is hosted on a file system, it can be protected by the operating system's security model. Each file or folder can be configured with an access control list (ACL), describing the permissions that principals have on the file. These permissions are enforced only when the OS"},{"id":"c2-0296","module":19,"lesson":"19A Account Security","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nAn alternative to file encryption is to use a full disk encryption (FDE) product. The Windows BitLocker disk encryption product is available with all editions of Windows except for the Home edition.","context":"An alternative to file encryption is to use a full disk encryption (FDE) product. The Windows BitLocker disk encryption product is available with all editions of Windows except for the Home edition.","options":["Restrict User Permissions","Add-ons come in several different types:","Backdoors","Windows BitLocker and BitLocker To Go"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Windows BitLocker and BitLocker To Go: An alternative to file encryption is to use a full disk encryption (FDE) product. The Windows BitLocker disk encryption product is available with all editions of Windows except for the Home edition."},{"id":"c2-0297","module":19,"lesson":"19B Workstation Security","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nMicrosoft’s Internet Explorer (IE) used to be dominant in the browser market, but alternatives such as Google's Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, and Opera have replaced it. IE itself is no longer supported. Edge, Microsoft’s replacement browser, now uses the same underlying Chromium","context":"Microsoft’s Internet Explorer (IE) used to be dominant in the browser market, but alternatives such as Google's Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, and Opera have replaced it. IE itself is no longer supported. Edge, Microsoft’s replacement browser, now uses the same underlying Chromium","options":["Adware","Change Default Administrator Account and Password","Browser Selection and Installation","Browser Patching"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Browser Selection and Installation: Microsoft’s Internet Explorer (IE) used to be dominant in the browser market, but alternatives such as Google's Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, and Opera have replaced it. IE itself is no longer supported. Edge, Microsoft’s replacement browser, now uses the same underlying Chromium"},{"id":"c2-0298","module":19,"lesson":"19C Browser Security","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nAs the browser is a security-critical type of software, it is particularly important to use a trusted source, such as an app store. If installed as a desktop application, care should be taken to The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 19: Managing Security Settings","context":"As the browser is a security-critical type of software, it is particularly important to use a trusted source, such as an app store. If installed as a desktop application, care should be taken to The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 19: Managing Security Settings","options":["Disable Guest Account","Secure Connections and Valid Certificates","Trusted Sources","Spyware and Keyloggers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Trusted Sources: As the browser is a security-critical type of software, it is particularly important to use a trusted source, such as an app store. If installed as a desktop application, care should be taken to The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 19: Managing Security Settings"},{"id":"c2-0299","module":19,"lesson":"19A Account Security","question":"What is being described here?\n\nUsing a browser from an untrusted source where the publisher of the installer cannot be verified through a digital signature or hash is a security risk and likely to expose the user to unwanted adverts, search engines, and even spyware and redirection attacks. Some PC","context":"Using a browser from an untrusted source where the publisher of the installer cannot be verified through a digital signature or hash is a security risk and likely to expose the user to unwanted adverts, search engines, and even spyware and redirection attacks. Some PC","options":["Untrusted Sources","Account Policies","Rootkits","Browser Privacy Settings"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Untrusted Sources: Using a browser from an untrusted source where the publisher of the installer cannot be verified through a digital signature or hash is a security risk and likely to expose the user to unwanted adverts, search engines, and even spyware and redirection attacks. Some PC"},{"id":"c2-0300","module":19,"lesson":"19B Workstation Security","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nEach browser maintains its own settings that are accessed via its Meatball (…) or Hamburger (⇨) menu button. Alternatively, you can open the internal URL, such as chrome://settings, edge://settings, or about:preferences (Firefox). The settings configure options such as","context":"Each browser maintains its own settings that are accessed via its Meatball (…) or Hamburger (⇨) menu button. Alternatively, you can open the internal URL, such as chrome://settings, edge://settings, or about:preferences (Firefox). The settings configure options such as","options":["Ransomware and Cryptominers","Malware Vectors","Unused Services","Browser Settings"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Browser Settings: Each browser maintains its own settings that are accessed via its Meatball (…) or Hamburger (⇨) menu button. Alternatively, you can open the internal URL, such as chrome://settings, edge://settings, or about:preferences (Firefox). The settings configure options such as"},{"id":"c2-0301","module":19,"lesson":"19C Browser Security","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nA browser sign-in allows the user to synchronize settings between instances of the browser software on different devices. As well as the browser settings, items that can be synced include bookmarks, history, saved autofill entries, and passwords. Settings and data for specific","context":"A browser sign-in allows the user to synchronize settings between instances of the browser software on different devices. As well as the browser settings, items that can be synced include bookmarks, history, saved autofill entries, and passwords. Settings and data for specific","options":["Execution Control","Malware Payloads","Sign-in and Browser Data Synchronization","Troubleshoot PC Security Symptoms"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Sign-in and Browser Data Synchronization: A browser sign-in allows the user to synchronize settings between instances of the browser software on different devices. As well as the browser settings, items that can be synced include bookmarks, history, saved autofill entries, and passwords. Settings and data for specific"},{"id":"c2-0302","module":19,"lesson":"19A Account Security","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nA typical user might be faced with having to remember dozens of sign-ins for different services and resort to using the same password for each. This is insecure because just one site breach could result in the compromise of all the user's digital identities. Each major browser now","context":"A typical user might be faced with having to remember dozens of sign-ins for different services and resort to using the same password for each. This is insecure because just one site breach could result in the compromise of all the user's digital identities. Each major browser now","options":["Trusted/Untrusted Software Sources","Password Best Practices","Password Manager","Backdoors"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Password Manager: A typical user might be faced with having to remember dozens of sign-ins for different services and resort to using the same password for each. This is insecure because just one site breach could result in the compromise of all the user's digital identities. Each major browser now"},{"id":"c2-0303","module":19,"lesson":"19B Workstation Security","question":"What is being described here?\n\nA browser add-on is some type of code that adds to the basic functionality of the software.","context":"A browser add-on is some type of code that adds to the basic functionality of the software.","options":["Password Rules","AutoRun and AutoPlay","Adware","Browser Extensions and Plug-ins"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Browser Extensions and Plug-ins: A browser add-on is some type of code that adds to the basic functionality of the software."},{"id":"c2-0304","module":19,"lesson":"19C Browser Security","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\n• Browser extensions add or change a browser feature via its application programming interface (API). For example, an extension might install a toolbar or change menu options.","context":"• Browser extensions add or change a browser feature via its application programming interface (API). For example, an extension might install a toolbar or change menu options.","options":["Spyware and Keyloggers","Restrict User Permissions","Windows Defender Antivirus","Add-ons come in several different types:"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Add-ons come in several different types:: • Browser extensions add or change a browser feature via its application programming interface (API). For example, an extension might install a toolbar or change menu options."},{"id":"c2-0305","module":19,"lesson":"19A Account Security","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nMaintaining a system that is up to date helps to ensure it remains protected against new threats.","context":"Maintaining a system that is up to date helps to ensure it remains protected against new threats.","options":["Browser Patching","Change Default Administrator Account and Password","Rootkits","Windows Defender Antivirus Updated Definitions"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Browser Patching: Maintaining a system that is up to date helps to ensure it remains protected against new threats."},{"id":"c2-0306","module":19,"lesson":"19B Workstation Security","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nThe web uses Transport Layer Security (TLS) and digital certificate to implement a secure connection. A secure connection validates the identity of the host running a site and encrypts communications to protect against snooping. The identity of a web server computer for a given","context":"The web uses Transport Layer Security (TLS) and digital certificate to implement a secure connection. A secure connection validates the identity of the host running a site and encrypts communications to protect against snooping. The identity of a web server computer for a given","options":["Activating and Deactivating Windows Defender Antivirus","Secure Connections and Valid Certificates","Disable Guest Account","Ransomware and Cryptominers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Secure Connections and Valid Certificates: The web uses Transport Layer Security (TLS) and digital certificate to implement a secure connection. A secure connection validates the identity of the host running a site and encrypts communications to protect against snooping. The identity of a web server computer for a given"},{"id":"c2-0307","module":19,"lesson":"19C Browser Security","question":"What is being described here?\n\nThe marketing value of online advertising has created an entire industry focused on creating profiles of individual search and browsing habits. The main function of privacy controls is to govern sites' use of these tracking tools, such as cookies. A cookie is a text file used to store","context":"The marketing value of online advertising has created an entire industry focused on creating profiles of individual search and browsing habits. The main function of privacy controls is to govern sites' use of these tracking tools, such as cookies. A cookie is a text file used to store","options":["Troubleshoot PC Security Symptoms","Account Policies","Browser Privacy Settings","Windows Defender Firewall"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Browser Privacy Settings: The marketing value of online advertising has created an entire industry focused on creating profiles of individual search and browsing habits. The main function of privacy controls is to govern sites' use of these tracking tools, such as cookies. A cookie is a text file used to store"},{"id":"c2-0308","module":19,"lesson":"19A Account Security","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nMalware is usually simply defined as software that does something bad, from the perspective of the system owner. The more detailed classification of different malware types helps to identify the likely source and impact of a security incident. Some malware classifications focus on the","context":"Malware is usually simply defined as software that does something bad, from the perspective of the system owner. The more detailed classification of different malware types helps to identify the likely source and impact of a security incident. Some malware classifications focus on the","options":["Encrypting File System","Malware Vectors","Unused Services","Password Best Practices"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Malware Vectors: Malware is usually simply defined as software that does something bad, from the perspective of the system owner. The more detailed classification of different malware types helps to identify the likely source and impact of a security incident. Some malware classifications focus on the"},{"id":"c2-0309","module":19,"lesson":"19B Workstation Security","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nClassifying malware by payload is a way of identifying what type of actions the code performs other than simply replicating or persisting on a host.","context":"Classifying malware by payload is a way of identifying what type of actions the code performs other than simply replicating or persisting on a host.","options":["Password Rules","Malware Payloads","Execution Control","Windows BitLocker and BitLocker To Go"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Malware Payloads: Classifying malware by payload is a way of identifying what type of actions the code performs other than simply replicating or persisting on a host."},{"id":"c2-0310","module":19,"lesson":"19C Browser Security","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nModern malware is usually designed to implement some type of backdoor, also referred to as a remote access trojan. Once the malware is installed, it allows the threat actor to access the PC, upload/exfiltrate data files, and install additional malware tools. This could allow the attacker to","context":"Modern malware is usually designed to implement some type of backdoor, also referred to as a remote access trojan. Once the malware is installed, it allows the threat actor to access the PC, upload/exfiltrate data files, and install additional malware tools. This could allow the attacker to","options":["Browser Selection and Installation","Backdoors","Restrict User Permissions","Trusted/Untrusted Software Sources"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Backdoors: Modern malware is usually designed to implement some type of backdoor, also referred to as a remote access trojan. Once the malware is installed, it allows the threat actor to access the PC, upload/exfiltrate data files, and install additional malware tools. This could allow the attacker to"},{"id":"c2-0311","module":19,"lesson":"19A Account Security","question":"What is being described here?\n\nAdware is a specialized malware used to display unwanted and unsolicited advertisements on your workstation or device. This can be accomplished by storing cookies in your browser or redirecting connection requests to an advertisement server. These Potentially Unwanted","context":"Adware is a specialized malware used to display unwanted and unsolicited advertisements on your workstation or device. This can be accomplished by storing cookies in your browser or redirecting connection requests to an advertisement server. These Potentially Unwanted","options":["AutoRun and AutoPlay","Change Default Administrator Account and Password","Adware","Trusted Sources"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Adware: Adware is a specialized malware used to display unwanted and unsolicited advertisements on your workstation or device. This can be accomplished by storing cookies in your browser or redirecting connection requests to an advertisement server. These Potentially Unwanted"},{"id":"c2-0312","module":19,"lesson":"19B Workstation Security","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nSpyware is malware that can perform browser reconfigurations, such as allowing tracking cookies, changing default search providers, opening arbitrary pages at startup, adding bookmarks, and so on. Spyware might also be able to monitor local application activity, take","context":"Spyware is malware that can perform browser reconfigurations, such as allowing tracking cookies, changing default search providers, opening arbitrary pages at startup, adding bookmarks, and so on. Spyware might also be able to monitor local application activity, take","options":["Untrusted Sources","Windows Defender Antivirus","Spyware and Keyloggers","Disable Guest Account"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Spyware and Keyloggers: Spyware is malware that can perform browser reconfigurations, such as allowing tracking cookies, changing default search providers, opening arbitrary pages at startup, adding bookmarks, and so on. Spyware might also be able to monitor local application activity, take"},{"id":"c2-0313","module":19,"lesson":"19C Browser Security","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nIn Windows, malware can only be manually installed with local administrator privileges. This means the user must be confident enough in the installer package to enter the credentials or accept the User Account Control (UAC) prompt. Additionally, Windows tries to protect the","context":"In Windows, malware can only be manually installed with local administrator privileges. This means the user must be confident enough in the installer package to enter the credentials or accept the User Account Control (UAC) prompt. Additionally, Windows tries to protect the","options":["Account Policies","Browser Settings","Rootkits","Windows Defender Antivirus Updated Definitions"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Rootkits: In Windows, malware can only be manually installed with local administrator privileges. This means the user must be confident enough in the installer package to enter the credentials or accept the User Account Control (UAC) prompt. Additionally, Windows tries to protect the"},{"id":"c2-0314","module":19,"lesson":"19A Account Security","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nRansomware is a type of malware that tries to extort money from the victim. One class of ransomware will display threatening messages, such as requiring Windows to be reactivated or suggesting that the computer has been locked by the police because it was used to view child","context":"Ransomware is a type of malware that tries to extort money from the victim. One class of ransomware will display threatening messages, such as requiring Windows to be reactivated or suggesting that the computer has been locked by the police because it was used to view child","options":["Activating and Deactivating Windows Defender Antivirus","Ransomware and Cryptominers","Unused Services","Sign-in and Browser Data Synchronization"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Ransomware and Cryptominers: Ransomware is a type of malware that tries to extort money from the victim. One class of ransomware will display threatening messages, such as requiring Windows to be reactivated or suggesting that the computer has been locked by the police because it was used to view child"},{"id":"c2-0315","module":19,"lesson":"19B Workstation Security","question":"What is being described here?\n\nThe multiple classifications for malware vectors and payloads mean that there can be very many different symptoms of security issues. In very general terms, any sort of activity or configuration change that was not initiated by the user is a good reason to suspect malware","context":"The multiple classifications for malware vectors and payloads mean that there can be very many different symptoms of security issues. In very general terms, any sort of activity or configuration change that was not initiated by the user is a good reason to suspect malware","options":["Windows Defender Firewall","Password Manager","Execution Control","Troubleshoot PC Security Symptoms"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Troubleshoot PC Security Symptoms: The multiple classifications for malware vectors and payloads mean that there can be very many different symptoms of security issues. In very general terms, any sort of activity or configuration change that was not initiated by the user is a good reason to suspect malware"},{"id":"c2-0316","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nA mobile security mechanism that activates when a device is idle or the power button is pressed.","context":"A mobile security mechanism that activates when a device is idle or the power button is pressed.","options":["Configuration profiles","Mobile Device Management","Mobile firewall app","Screen lock"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Screen lock: A mobile security mechanism that activates when a device is idle or the power button is pressed."},{"id":"c2-0317","module":20,"lesson":"20B Mobile App Troubleshooting","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nA numeric credential that can be used for mobile screen-lock authentication.","context":"A numeric credential that can be used for mobile screen-lock authentication.","options":["2FA","BYOD","PIN","Two-factor Authentication"],"answer":2,"explanation":"PIN: A numeric credential that can be used for mobile screen-lock authentication."},{"id":"c2-0318","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"What is being described here?\n\nA biometric method using a fingerprint sensor for device authentication.","context":"A biometric method using a fingerprint sensor for device authentication.","options":["Mobile device encryption","COBO","Fingerprint authentication","Mobile Data Security"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Fingerprint authentication: A biometric method using a fingerprint sensor for device authentication."},{"id":"c2-0319","module":20,"lesson":"20B Mobile App Troubleshooting","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nA biometric method using a 3-D image of the user’s face for authentication.","context":"A biometric method using a 3-D image of the user’s face for authentication.","options":["Device Encryption","Facial recognition","Screen Locks","COPE"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Facial recognition: A biometric method using a 3-D image of the user’s face for authentication."},{"id":"c2-0320","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nA screen-lock method based on swiping a join-the-dots pattern.","context":"A screen-lock method based on swiping a join-the-dots pattern.","options":["CYOD","Pattern lock","Patching/OS Updates","Remote Backup Applications"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pattern lock: A screen-lock method based on swiping a join-the-dots pattern."},{"id":"c2-0321","module":20,"lesson":"20B Mobile App Troubleshooting","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nSoftware that can protect mobile devices against malware, phishing, and software exploits.","context":"Software that can protect mobile devices against malware, phishing, and software exploits.","options":["Mobile security software","Locator Apps and Remote Wipe","Antivirus/Anti-malware Apps","Mobile Device Management (MDM)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Mobile security software: Software that can protect mobile devices against malware, phishing, and software exploits."},{"id":"c2-0322","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"What is being described here?\n\nKeeping the mobile operating system updated with patches and new OS versions.","context":"Keeping the mobile operating system updated with patches and new OS versions.","options":["Firewall Apps","Mobile Device Troubleshooting Tools","Mobile OS patching","Configuration profiles"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Mobile OS patching: Keeping the mobile operating system updated with patches and new OS versions."},{"id":"c2-0323","module":20,"lesson":"20B Mobile App Troubleshooting","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nSecurity software that can filter content, block known phishing sites, and monitor app permissions and usage.","context":"Security software that can filter content, block known phishing sites, and monitor app permissions and usage.","options":["Enterprise Mobility Management","2FA","Reboot","Mobile antivirus/anti-malware"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Mobile antivirus/anti-malware: Security software that can filter content, block known phishing sites, and monitor app permissions and usage."},{"id":"c2-0324","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nAn app that can monitor app activity and prevent connections to specific ports or IP addresses.","context":"An app that can monitor app activity and prevent connections to specific ports or IP addresses.","options":["Mobile Device Management","Mobile firewall app","Factory Reset","Mobile device encryption"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Mobile firewall app: An app that can monitor app activity and prevent connections to specific ports or IP addresses."},{"id":"c2-0325","module":20,"lesson":"20B Mobile App Troubleshooting","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nA deployment model where employees use their own devices subject to company requirements and oversight.","context":"A deployment model where employees use their own devices subject to company requirements and oversight.","options":["Screen Locks","Battery Life Issues","Two-factor Authentication","BYOD"],"answer":3,"explanation":"BYOD: A deployment model where employees use their own devices subject to company requirements and oversight."},{"id":"c2-0326","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"What is being described here?\n\nA deployment model where the company owns the device and it is used solely for business purposes.","context":"A deployment model where the company owns the device and it is used solely for business purposes.","options":["Troubleshoot Device and OS Issues","Patching/OS Updates","COBO","Mobile Data Security"],"answer":2,"explanation":"COBO: A deployment model where the company owns the device and it is used solely for business purposes."},{"id":"c2-0327","module":20,"lesson":"20B Mobile App Troubleshooting","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nA deployment model where the company owns the device but employees can use it for personal activities under policy.","context":"A deployment model where the company owns the device but employees can use it for personal activities under policy.","options":["Antivirus/Anti-malware Apps","OS Fails to Update","COPE","Device Encryption"],"answer":2,"explanation":"COPE: A deployment model where the company owns the device but employees can use it for personal activities under policy."},{"id":"c2-0328","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nA deployment model where employees choose from a list of approved devices.","context":"A deployment model where employees choose from a list of approved devices.","options":["Firewall Apps","Remote Backup Applications","CYOD","5. Check for sufficient free space:"],"answer":2,"explanation":"CYOD: A deployment model where employees choose from a list of approved devices."},{"id":"c2-0329","module":20,"lesson":"20B Mobile App Troubleshooting","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nSoftware designed to apply security policies to mobile devices in an enterprise.","context":"Software designed to apply security policies to mobile devices in an enterprise.","options":["Mobile Device Management (MDM)","Locator Apps and Remote Wipe","Enterprise Mobility Management","Screen lock"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Mobile Device Management (MDM): Software designed to apply security policies to mobile devices in an enterprise."},{"id":"c2-0330","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"What is being described here?\n\nMDM profiles that define and enforce security requirements and device settings.","context":"MDM profiles that define and enforce security requirements and device settings.","options":["Mobile Device Management","PIN","Configuration profiles","Mobile Device Troubleshooting Tools"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Configuration profiles: MDM profiles that define and enforce security requirements and device settings."},{"id":"c2-0331","module":20,"lesson":"20B Mobile App Troubleshooting","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nAuthentication requiring two different kinds of credentials.","context":"Authentication requiring two different kinds of credentials.","options":["2FA","Two-factor Authentication","Reboot","Fingerprint authentication"],"answer":0,"explanation":"2FA: Authentication requiring two different kinds of credentials."},{"id":"c2-0332","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nA security mechanism used to protect data on smartphones and tablets if a device is lost or stolen.","context":"A security mechanism used to protect data on smartphones and tablets if a device is lost or stolen.","options":["Factory Reset","Mobile device encryption","Facial recognition","Mobile Data Security"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Mobile device encryption: A security mechanism used to protect data on smartphones and tablets if a device is lost or stolen."},{"id":"c2-0333","module":20,"lesson":"20B Mobile App Troubleshooting","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nIf threat actors gain access to smartphones or tablets, they can retrieve vast amounts of information to facilitate further attacks. This includes confidential data, cached passwords for email, VPNs, and websites, as well as contacts and message histories (SMS, email, and IM),","context":"If threat actors gain access to smartphones or tablets, they can retrieve vast amounts of information to facilitate further attacks. This includes confidential data, cached passwords for email, VPNs, and websites, as well as contacts and message histories (SMS, email, and IM),","options":["Device Encryption","Screen Locks","Pattern lock","Battery Life Issues"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Screen Locks: If threat actors gain access to smartphones or tablets, they can retrieve vast amounts of information to facilitate further attacks. This includes confidential data, cached passwords for email, VPNs, and websites, as well as contacts and message histories (SMS, email, and IM),"},{"id":"c2-0334","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"What is being described here?\n\nKeeping a mobile OS and its apps updated with patches and new OS versions is crucial, similar to desktop computers.","context":"Keeping a mobile OS and its apps updated with patches and new OS versions is crucial, similar to desktop computers.","options":["Mobile security software","Patching/OS Updates","Troubleshoot Device and OS Issues","Remote Backup Applications"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Patching/OS Updates: Keeping a mobile OS and its apps updated with patches and new OS versions is crucial, similar to desktop computers."},{"id":"c2-0335","module":20,"lesson":"20B Mobile App Troubleshooting","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nModern smartphones are vulnerable to software exploits and malware, especially if apps are installed from untrusted sources. The evolving nature of mobile OS threats makes it challenging to maintain pattern databases or use heuristics effectively.","context":"Modern smartphones are vulnerable to software exploits and malware, especially if apps are installed from untrusted sources. The evolving nature of mobile OS threats makes it challenging to maintain pattern databases or use heuristics effectively.","options":["Antivirus/Anti-malware Apps","Mobile OS patching","Locator Apps and Remote Wipe","OS Fails to Update"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Antivirus/Anti-malware Apps: Modern smartphones are vulnerable to software exploits and malware, especially if apps are installed from untrusted sources. The evolving nature of mobile OS threats makes it challenging to maintain pattern databases or use heuristics effectively."},{"id":"c2-0336","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nFirewall apps for mobile devices monitor app activity and prevent connections to specific ports or IP addresses. Firewalls need higher permission levels (root) to control other apps, making installation challenging. \"No-root\" firewalls create a local virtual private network (VPN) interface","context":"Firewall apps for mobile devices monitor app activity and prevent connections to specific ports or IP addresses. Firewalls need higher permission levels (root) to control other apps, making installation challenging. \"No-root\" firewalls create a local virtual private network (VPN) interface","options":["Mobile antivirus/anti-malware","5. Check for sufficient free space:","Firewall Apps","Mobile Device Troubleshooting Tools"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Firewall Apps: Firewall apps for mobile devices monitor app activity and prevent connections to specific ports or IP addresses. Firewalls need higher permission levels (root) to control other apps, making installation challenging. \"No-root\" firewalls create a local virtual private network (VPN) interface"},{"id":"c2-0337","module":20,"lesson":"20B Mobile App Troubleshooting","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nMobile devices have become essential for email, daily management tasks, and accessing business processes and cloud applications. A mobile device deployment model includes the policies and procedures that define how employees receive mobile devices and applications:","context":"Mobile devices have become essential for email, daily management tasks, and accessing business processes and cloud applications. A mobile device deployment model includes the policies and procedures that define how employees receive mobile devices and applications:","options":["Mobile firewall app","Reboot","Screen lock","Enterprise Mobility Management"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Enterprise Mobility Management: Mobile devices have become essential for email, daily management tasks, and accessing business processes and cloud applications. A mobile device deployment model includes the policies and procedures that define how employees receive mobile devices and applications:"},{"id":"c2-0338","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"What is being described here?\n\nMobile device management (MDM) is a class of software designed to apply security policies to the use of mobile devices in the enterprise. This software can be used to manage enterprise- owned devices as well as bring your own device (BYOD) user-owned smartphones.","context":"Mobile device management (MDM) is a class of software designed to apply security policies to the use of mobile devices in the enterprise. This software can be used to manage enterprise- owned devices as well as bring your own device (BYOD) user-owned smartphones.","options":["BYOD","PIN","Mobile Device Management","Factory Reset"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Mobile Device Management: Mobile device management (MDM) is a class of software designed to apply security policies to the use of mobile devices in the enterprise. This software can be used to manage enterprise- owned devices as well as bring your own device (BYOD) user-owned smartphones."},{"id":"c2-0339","module":20,"lesson":"20B Mobile App Troubleshooting","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nMost smartphones and tablets are single-user devices. Access control can be implemented by configuring a screen lock that can only be bypassed using the correct password, personal identification number (PIN), or swipe pattern. Many devices now support biometric","context":"Most smartphones and tablets are single-user devices. Access control can be implemented by configuring a screen lock that can only be bypassed using the correct password, personal identification number (PIN), or swipe pattern. Many devices now support biometric","options":["COBO","Two-factor Authentication","Fingerprint authentication","Battery Life Issues"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Two-factor Authentication: Most smartphones and tablets are single-user devices. Access control can be implemented by configuring a screen lock that can only be bypassed using the correct password, personal identification number (PIN), or swipe pattern. Many devices now support biometric"},{"id":"c2-0340","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nIf a mobile device is lost or stolen, mechanisms exist to recover it and prevent misuse or data loss.","context":"If a mobile device is lost or stolen, mechanisms exist to recover it and prevent misuse or data loss.","options":["Mobile Data Security","Troubleshoot Device and OS Issues","COPE","Facial recognition"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Mobile Data Security: If a mobile device is lost or stolen, mechanisms exist to recover it and prevent misuse or data loss."},{"id":"c2-0341","module":20,"lesson":"20B Mobile App Troubleshooting","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nAll but the earliest versions of mobile device OSs for smartphones and tablets provide some type of default encryption.","context":"All but the earliest versions of mobile device OSs for smartphones and tablets provide some type of default encryption.","options":["CYOD","Device Encryption","OS Fails to Update","Pattern lock"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Device Encryption: All but the earliest versions of mobile device OSs for smartphones and tablets provide some type of default encryption."},{"id":"c2-0342","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"What is being described here?\n\nMost mobile devices are linked to vendor cloud services (iCloud for iOS, Google Sync for Android, OneDrive for Microsoft) for automatic remote backup of data, apps, and settings.","context":"Most mobile devices are linked to vendor cloud services (iCloud for iOS, Google Sync for Android, OneDrive for Microsoft) for automatic remote backup of data, apps, and settings.","options":["Mobile Device Management (MDM)","Remote Backup Applications","Mobile security software","5. Check for sufficient free space:"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Remote Backup Applications: Most mobile devices are linked to vendor cloud services (iCloud for iOS, Google Sync for Android, OneDrive for Microsoft) for automatic remote backup of data, apps, and settings."},{"id":"c2-0343","module":20,"lesson":"20B Mobile App Troubleshooting","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nMost smartphones and many tablets are equipped with GPS receivers, which determine a device's position using satellite information. GPS requires line-of-sight and does not work indoors. High-accuracy location services use GPS along with Wi-Fi access points and Bluetooth","context":"Most smartphones and many tablets are equipped with GPS receivers, which determine a device's position using satellite information. GPS requires line-of-sight and does not work indoors. High-accuracy location services use GPS along with Wi-Fi access points and Bluetooth","options":["Mobile OS patching","Screen lock","Configuration profiles","Locator Apps and Remote Wipe"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Locator Apps and Remote Wipe: Most smartphones and many tablets are equipped with GPS receivers, which determine a device's position using satellite information. GPS requires line-of-sight and does not work indoors. High-accuracy location services use GPS along with Wi-Fi access points and Bluetooth"},{"id":"c2-0344","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nWhen troubleshooting mobile devices, the Settings app is frequently used. Its layout differs between iOS and Android and can vary with different versions.","context":"When troubleshooting mobile devices, the Settings app is frequently used. Its layout differs between iOS and Android and can vary with different versions.","options":["Mobile Device Troubleshooting Tools","2FA","PIN","Mobile antivirus/anti-malware"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Mobile Device Troubleshooting Tools: When troubleshooting mobile devices, the Settings app is frequently used. Its layout differs between iOS and Android and can vary with different versions."},{"id":"c2-0345","module":20,"lesson":"20B Mobile App Troubleshooting","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nRebooting a mobile device can often resolve temporary performance or stability issues. Unlike leaving the device in sleep mode, powering it off closes all applications and clears RAM without affecting stored data and settings. This soft reset is effective for restoring unresponsive or","context":"Rebooting a mobile device can often resolve temporary performance or stability issues. Unlike leaving the device in sleep mode, powering it off closes all applications and clears RAM without affecting stored data and settings. This soft reset is effective for restoring unresponsive or","options":["Mobile firewall app","Reboot","Fingerprint authentication","Mobile device encryption"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Reboot: Rebooting a mobile device can often resolve temporary performance or stability issues. Unlike leaving the device in sleep mode, powering it off closes all applications and clears RAM without affecting stored data and settings. This soft reset is effective for restoring unresponsive or"},{"id":"c2-0346","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"What is being described here?\n\nA factory reset erases all user data, apps, and settings from a device. Afterward, the device must be manually set up with a new user account and apps or restored from a backup. Ensure the device is fully charged or connected to a power source before performing a reset.","context":"A factory reset erases all user data, apps, and settings from a device. Afterward, the device must be manually set up with a new user account and apps or restored from a backup. Ensure the device is fully charged or connected to a power source before performing a reset.","options":["Factory Reset","Facial recognition","BYOD","Screen Locks"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Factory Reset: A factory reset erases all user data, apps, and settings from a device. Afterward, the device must be manually set up with a new user account and apps or restored from a backup. Ensure the device is fully charged or connected to a power source before performing a reset."},{"id":"c2-0347","module":20,"lesson":"20B Mobile App Troubleshooting","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nBattery life issues on mobile devices could come from a variety of factors. Background app activity can significantly drain the battery, so check which apps consume the most power and disable unnecessary background processes. Adjust screen brightness and timeout settings to","context":"Battery life issues on mobile devices could come from a variety of factors. Background app activity can significantly drain the battery, so check which apps consume the most power and disable unnecessary background processes. Adjust screen brightness and timeout settings to","options":["Pattern lock","Battery Life Issues","Patching/OS Updates","COBO"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Battery Life Issues: Battery life issues on mobile devices could come from a variety of factors. Background app activity can significantly drain the battery, so check which apps consume the most power and disable unnecessary background processes. Adjust screen brightness and timeout settings to"},{"id":"c2-0348","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nIf rebooting doesn't resolve the issue, follow these troubleshooting steps. If problems persist, consider a factory reset.","context":"If rebooting doesn't resolve the issue, follow these troubleshooting steps. If problems persist, consider a factory reset.","options":["Troubleshoot Device and OS Issues","COPE","Antivirus/Anti-malware Apps","Mobile security software"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Troubleshoot Device and OS Issues: If rebooting doesn't resolve the issue, follow these troubleshooting steps. If problems persist, consider a factory reset."},{"id":"c2-0349","module":20,"lesson":"20B Mobile App Troubleshooting","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nAn OS update failure can expose the device to security vulnerabilities. Follow these steps: 1. Verify update compatibility with your device model on the vendor's website.","context":"An OS update failure can expose the device to security vulnerabilities. Follow these steps: 1. Verify update compatibility with your device model on the vendor's website.","options":["OS Fails to Update","Mobile OS patching","CYOD","Firewall Apps"],"answer":0,"explanation":"OS Fails to Update: An OS update failure can expose the device to security vulnerabilities. Follow these steps: 1. Verify update compatibility with your device model on the vendor's website."},{"id":"c2-0350","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"What is being described here?\n\n• On iOS: Go to Settings > General > Storage. • On Android: Go to Settings > Storage.","context":"• On iOS: Go to Settings > General > Storage. • On Android: Go to Settings > Storage.","options":["5. Check for sufficient free space:","Mobile antivirus/anti-malware","Mobile Device Management (MDM)","Enterprise Mobility Management"],"answer":0,"explanation":"5. Check for sufficient free space:: • On iOS: Go to Settings > General > Storage. • On Android: Go to Settings > Storage."},{"id":"c2-0351","module":21,"lesson":"21A Data Backup & Recovery","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nData backup is a system maintenance task that enables you to store copies of critical data for safekeeping. Backup protects against loss of data due to disasters such as file corruption or hardware failure. Data recovery is a task that enables you to restore user access to lost or","context":"Data backup is a system maintenance task that enables you to store copies of critical data for safekeeping. Backup protects against loss of data due to disasters such as file corruption or hardware failure. Data recovery is a task that enables you to restore user access to lost or","options":["Personally Identifiable Information","End-User License Agreements","Backup Operations","Grandfather - Father - Son (GFS) Scheme"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Backup Operations: Data backup is a system maintenance task that enables you to store copies of critical data for safekeeping. Backup protects against loss of data due to disasters such as file corruption or hardware failure. Data recovery is a task that enables you to restore user access to lost or"},{"id":"c2-0352","module":21,"lesson":"21B Data Handling Best Practices","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nWhen considering a file server or database server, the execution and frequency of backups must be carefully planned and guided by policies. Each backup job records data as it was at a certain point in time. As each backup job might take up a lot of space and there is","context":"When considering a file server or database server, the execution and frequency of backups must be carefully planned and guided by policies. Each backup job records data as it was at a certain point in time. As each backup job might take up a lot of space and there is","options":["Backup Methods","Personal Government-issued Information","License Compliance Monitoring","On Site versus Off Site Storage"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Backup Methods: When considering a file server or database server, the execution and frequency of backups must be carefully planned and guided by policies. Each backup job records data as it was at a certain point in time. As each backup job might take up a lot of space and there is"},{"id":"c2-0353","module":21,"lesson":"21C Artificial Intelligence","question":"What is being described here?\n\n• Frequency is the period between backup jobs. The frequency configuration reflects how much lost work can be tolerated. For example, if employees can recall and input the previous day’s work on document files, a daily backup will meet the requirement. If the edits are much","context":"• Frequency is the period between backup jobs. The frequency configuration reflects how much lost work can be tolerated. For example, if employees can recall and input the previous day’s work on document files, a daily backup will meet the requirement. If the edits are much","options":["Open-source Licenses","Healthcare Data","3-2-1 Backup Rule","Two main factors govern backup operations:"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Two main factors govern backup operations:: • Frequency is the period between backup jobs. The frequency configuration reflects how much lost work can be tolerated. For example, if employees can recall and input the previous day’s work on document files, a daily backup will meet the requirement. If the edits are much"},{"id":"c2-0354","module":21,"lesson":"21A Data Backup & Recovery","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nThe requirements for backup frequency and retention must be managed against the capacity of the backup media and the time it takes to complete a backup job. These requirements are managed by using different types of jobs in a backup chain. The main types of backups are full","context":"The requirements for backup frequency and retention must be managed against the capacity of the backup media and the time it takes to complete a backup job. These requirements are managed by using different types of jobs in a backup chain. The main types of backups are full","options":["Credit Card Transactions","Digital Rights Management","Backup Testing and Recovery Best Practices","Backup Chains"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Backup Chains: The requirements for backup frequency and retention must be managed against the capacity of the backup media and the time it takes to complete a backup job. These requirements are managed by using different types of jobs in a backup chain. The main types of backups are full"},{"id":"c2-0355","module":21,"lesson":"21B Data Handling Best Practices","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nand Storage Complexity Requirement Full All selected data High Low (single job) Cleared regardless of when it was previously backed up Incremental New files and files Low High (multiple Cleared modified since last jobs) backup job Differential New files and files Moderate Moderate (two Not cleared","context":"and Storage Complexity Requirement Full All selected data High Low (single job) Cleared regardless of when it was previously backed up Incremental New files and files Low High (multiple Cleared modified since last jobs) backup job Differential New files and files Moderate Moderate (two Not cleared","options":["Non-Disclosure Agreements","Data Handling Best Practices","Type Data Selection Backup Job Time Recovery Archive Attribute","Recovery Options"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Type Data Selection Backup Job Time Recovery Archive Attribute: and Storage Complexity Requirement Full All selected data High Low (single job) Cleared regardless of when it was previously backed up Incremental New files and files Low High (multiple Cleared modified since last jobs) backup job Differential New files and files Moderate Moderate (two Not cleared"},{"id":"c2-0356","module":21,"lesson":"21C Artificial Intelligence","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nFull All selected data High Low (single job) Cleared regardless of when it was previously backed up Incremental New files and files Low High (multiple Cleared modified since last jobs) backup job Differential New files and files Moderate Moderate (two Not cleared","context":"Full All selected data High Low (single job) Cleared regardless of when it was previously backed up Incremental New files and files Low High (multiple Cleared modified since last jobs) backup job Differential New files and files Moderate Moderate (two Not cleared","options":["Regulated Data Classification","Incident Response","Data Retention Requirements","Requirement"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Requirement: Full All selected data High Low (single job) Cleared regardless of when it was previously backed up Incremental New files and files Low High (multiple Cleared modified since last jobs) backup job Differential New files and files Moderate Moderate (two Not cleared"},{"id":"c2-0357","module":21,"lesson":"21A Data Backup & Recovery","question":"What is being described here?\n\nA synthetic full backup is similar to a Full backup, but instead of scanning the system again to create the full backup, the system will use the original full backup and then add in the data from the incremental backups to create a new full up-to-date backup.","context":"A synthetic full backup is similar to a Full backup, but instead of scanning the system again to create the full backup, the system will use the original full backup and then add in the data from the incremental backups to create a new full up-to-date backup.","options":["Prohibited Content and Licensing Issues","Synthetic Full Backup","Personally Identifiable Information","Data Integrity and Preservation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Synthetic Full Backup: A synthetic full backup is similar to a Full backup, but instead of scanning the system again to create the full backup, the system will use the original full backup and then add in the data from the incremental backups to create a new full up-to-date backup."},{"id":"c2-0358","module":21,"lesson":"21B Data Handling Best Practices","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nA backup rotation scheme allows some media to be reused once the retention period of the job stored on it has expired. Rotation is most closely associated with the use of tape media but can be applied to disk devices too. There are many backup rotation schemes, but the most widely","context":"A backup rotation scheme allows some media to be reused once the retention period of the job stored on it has expired. Rotation is most closely associated with the use of tape media but can be applied to disk devices too. There are many backup rotation schemes, but the most widely","options":["Personal Government-issued Information","Prohibited Content","Backup Media Requirements","Order of Volatility"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Backup Media Requirements: A backup rotation scheme allows some media to be reused once the retention period of the job stored on it has expired. Rotation is most closely associated with the use of tape media but can be applied to disk devices too. There are many backup rotation schemes, but the most widely"},{"id":"c2-0359","module":21,"lesson":"21C Artificial Intelligence","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nThe GFS scheme labels the backup tapes in generations. Son tapes store the most recent data and have the shortest retention period (one week, for example). Grandfather tapes are the oldest and have the longest retention period (one year, for example). Assuming a single","context":"The GFS scheme labels the backup tapes in generations. Son tapes store the most recent data and have the shortest retention period (one week, for example). Grandfather tapes are the oldest and have the longest retention period (one year, for example). Assuming a single","options":["Healthcare Data","Grandfather - Father - Son (GFS) Scheme","End-User License Agreements","A general order of volatility from most to least volatile is:"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Grandfather - Father - Son (GFS) Scheme: The GFS scheme labels the backup tapes in generations. Son tapes store the most recent data and have the shortest retention period (one week, for example). Grandfather tapes are the oldest and have the longest retention period (one year, for example). Assuming a single"},{"id":"c2-0360","module":21,"lesson":"21A Data Backup & Recovery","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nOn site backup storage means that the production system and backup media are in the same location. This means that if a disaster strikes the facility, there is the risk of losing both the production and backup copies of the data.","context":"On site backup storage means that the production system and backup media are in the same location. This means that if a disaster strikes the facility, there is the risk of losing both the production and backup copies of the data.","options":["License Compliance Monitoring","On Site versus Off Site Storage","Chain of Custody","Credit Card Transactions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"On Site versus Off Site Storage: On site backup storage means that the production system and backup media are in the same location. This means that if a disaster strikes the facility, there is the risk of losing both the production and backup copies of the data."},{"id":"c2-0361","module":21,"lesson":"21B Data Handling Best Practices","question":"What is being described here?\n\nThe 3-2-1 backup rule is a best-practice maxim that you can apply to your backup procedures to verify that you are implementing a solution that can mitigate the widest possible range of disaster scenarios. It states that you should have three copies of your data (including the","context":"The 3-2-1 backup rule is a best-practice maxim that you can apply to your backup procedures to verify that you are implementing a solution that can mitigate the widest possible range of disaster scenarios. It states that you should have three copies of your data (including the","options":["3-2-1 Backup Rule","Open-source Licenses","Data Handling Best Practices","Data Destruction Methods"],"answer":0,"explanation":"3-2-1 Backup Rule: The 3-2-1 backup rule is a best-practice maxim that you can apply to your backup procedures to verify that you are implementing a solution that can mitigate the widest possible range of disaster scenarios. It states that you should have three copies of your data (including the"},{"id":"c2-0362","module":21,"lesson":"21C Artificial Intelligence","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nWhen you design a backup scheme, test it to make sure it's reliable. To test the backup: • Try restoring some of the backed-up data into a test directory, making sure you don't overwrite any data when doing so. Alternatively, use a virtual machine to test recovery","context":"When you design a backup scheme, test it to make sure it's reliable. To test the backup: • Try restoring some of the backed-up data into a test directory, making sure you don't overwrite any data when doing so. Alternatively, use a virtual machine to test recovery","options":["Erasing / Wiping","Data Retention Requirements","Digital Rights Management","Backup Testing and Recovery Best Practices"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Backup Testing and Recovery Best Practices: When you design a backup scheme, test it to make sure it's reliable. To test the backup: • Try restoring some of the backed-up data into a test directory, making sure you don't overwrite any data when doing so. Alternatively, use a virtual machine to test recovery"},{"id":"c2-0363","module":21,"lesson":"21A Data Backup & Recovery","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nWhen performing a recovery, you can choose to either perform an in-place (overwrite) recovery or recover to an alternate location.","context":"When performing a recovery, you can choose to either perform an in-place (overwrite) recovery or recover to an alternate location.","options":["Recovery Options","Low Level Format","Prohibited Content and Licensing Issues","Non-Disclosure Agreements"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Recovery Options: When performing a recovery, you can choose to either perform an in-place (overwrite) recovery or recover to an alternate location."},{"id":"c2-0364","module":21,"lesson":"21B Data Handling Best Practices","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nRegulated data is information that must be collected, processed, and stored in compliance with federal and/or state legislation. If a company processes regulated data collected from customers who reside in different countries, it must comply with the relevant legislation for each","context":"Regulated data is information that must be collected, processed, and stored in compliance with federal and/or state legislation. If a company processes regulated data collected from customers who reside in different countries, it must comply with the relevant legislation for each","options":["Backup Operations","Prohibited Content","Incident Response","Regulated Data Classification"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Regulated Data Classification: Regulated data is information that must be collected, processed, and stored in compliance with federal and/or state legislation. If a company processes regulated data collected from customers who reside in different countries, it must comply with the relevant legislation for each"},{"id":"c2-0365","module":21,"lesson":"21C Artificial Intelligence","question":"What is being described here?\n\nPersonally identifiable information (PII) is data that can be used to identify, contact, or locate an individual or, in the case of identity theft, to impersonate them. A cell phone number is a good example of PII. Others include name, date of birth, email address, street address, biometric","context":"Personally identifiable information (PII) is data that can be used to identify, contact, or locate an individual or, in the case of identity theft, to impersonate them. A cell phone number is a good example of PII. Others include name, date of birth, email address, street address, biometric","options":["Personally Identifiable Information","End-User License Agreements","Backup Methods","Data Integrity and Preservation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Personally Identifiable Information: Personally identifiable information (PII) is data that can be used to identify, contact, or locate an individual or, in the case of identity theft, to impersonate them. A cell phone number is a good example of PII. Others include name, date of birth, email address, street address, biometric"},{"id":"c2-0366","module":21,"lesson":"21A Data Backup & Recovery","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nPersonal government-issued information that is issued to individuals by federal or state governments is also PII. Examples include a social security number (SSN), passport, driving license, and birth/marriage certificates. Data collected and held by the US federal government","context":"Personal government-issued information that is issued to individuals by federal or state governments is also PII. Examples include a social security number (SSN), passport, driving license, and birth/marriage certificates. Data collected and held by the US federal government","options":["Two main factors govern backup operations:","Personal Government-issued Information","License Compliance Monitoring","Order of Volatility"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Personal Government-issued Information: Personal government-issued information that is issued to individuals by federal or state governments is also PII. Examples include a social security number (SSN), passport, driving license, and birth/marriage certificates. Data collected and held by the US federal government"},{"id":"c2-0367","module":21,"lesson":"21B Data Handling Best Practices","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nProtected-health-information refers to medical and insurance records plus associated hospital and laboratory test results. Healthcare data may be associated with a specific person or used as an anonymized or de-identified data set for analysis and research, such as in clinical trials to","context":"Protected-health-information refers to medical and insurance records plus associated hospital and laboratory test results. Healthcare data may be associated with a specific person or used as an anonymized or de-identified data set for analysis and research, such as in clinical trials to","options":["Healthcare Data","Open-source Licenses","A general order of volatility from most to least volatile is:","Backup Chains"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Healthcare Data: Protected-health-information refers to medical and insurance records plus associated hospital and laboratory test results. Healthcare data may be associated with a specific person or used as an anonymized or de-identified data set for analysis and research, such as in clinical trials to"},{"id":"c2-0368","module":21,"lesson":"21C Artificial Intelligence","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nThere are also industry-enforced regulations mandating data security. A good example is the Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard (PCI DSS) which governs the processing of credit card transactions and other bank card payments. It sets out protections that must","context":"There are also industry-enforced regulations mandating data security. A good example is the Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard (PCI DSS) which governs the processing of credit card transactions and other bank card payments. It sets out protections that must","options":["Credit Card Transactions","Digital Rights Management","Chain of Custody","Type Data Selection Backup Job Time Recovery Archive Attribute"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Credit Card Transactions: There are also industry-enforced regulations mandating data security. A good example is the Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard (PCI DSS) which governs the processing of credit card transactions and other bank card payments. It sets out protections that must"},{"id":"c2-0369","module":21,"lesson":"21A Data Backup & Recovery","question":"What is being described here?\n\nEmployees should be trained to identify PII and to handle personal or sensitive data appropriately. This means not making unauthorized copies or allowing the data to be seen or captured by any unauthorized persons. Examples of treating sensitive data carelessly include","context":"Employees should be trained to identify PII and to handle personal or sensitive data appropriately. This means not making unauthorized copies or allowing the data to be seen or captured by any unauthorized persons. Examples of treating sensitive data carelessly include","options":["Non-Disclosure Agreements","Requirement","Data Handling Best Practices","Data Destruction Methods"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Data Handling Best Practices: Employees should be trained to identify PII and to handle personal or sensitive data appropriately. This means not making unauthorized copies or allowing the data to be seen or captured by any unauthorized persons. Examples of treating sensitive data carelessly include"},{"id":"c2-0370","module":21,"lesson":"21B Data Handling Best Practices","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nAnother issue for regulated data is its retention on both file and database servers and in backup files: • Regulation might set a maximum period for the retention of data. For example, if a company collects a customer’s address and credit card information to fulfill an order and the customer","context":"Another issue for regulated data is its retention on both file and database servers and in backup files: • Regulation might set a maximum period for the retention of data. For example, if a company collects a customer’s address and credit card information to fulfill an order and the customer","options":["Data Retention Requirements","Erasing / Wiping","Synthetic Full Backup","Incident Response"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Data Retention Requirements: Another issue for regulated data is its retention on both file and database servers and in backup files: • Regulation might set a maximum period for the retention of data. For example, if a company collects a customer’s address and credit card information to fulfill an order and the customer"},{"id":"c2-0371","module":21,"lesson":"21C Artificial Intelligence","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nAs well as ensuring secure handling of confidential and sensitive data, you need to consider methods for identifying and removing prohibited content and unlicensed software from company workstations.","context":"As well as ensuring secure handling of confidential and sensitive data, you need to consider methods for identifying and removing prohibited content and unlicensed software from company workstations.","options":["Prohibited Content and Licensing Issues","Data Integrity and Preservation","Backup Media Requirements","Low Level Format"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Prohibited Content and Licensing Issues: As well as ensuring secure handling of confidential and sensitive data, you need to consider methods for identifying and removing prohibited content and unlicensed software from company workstations."},{"id":"c2-0372","module":21,"lesson":"21A Data Backup & Recovery","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nEmployee workstations should only be used for work-related activity and data storage. In this context, prohibited content is any information that is not applicable to work. It can also specifically mean content that is obscene or illegally copied/pirated. The acceptable use","context":"Employee workstations should only be used for work-related activity and data storage. In this context, prohibited content is any information that is not applicable to work. It can also specifically mean content that is obscene or illegally copied/pirated. The acceptable use","options":["Backup Operations","Order of Volatility","Grandfather - Father - Son (GFS) Scheme","Prohibited Content"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Prohibited Content: Employee workstations should only be used for work-related activity and data storage. In this context, prohibited content is any information that is not applicable to work. It can also specifically mean content that is obscene or illegally copied/pirated. The acceptable use"},{"id":"c2-0373","module":21,"lesson":"21B Data Handling Best Practices","question":"What is being described here?\n\nProhibited content also extends to the unauthorized installation and use of software. When you install software, you must accept the license governing its use, often called the end-user license agreement. The terms of the license will vary according to the type of software, but the","context":"Prohibited content also extends to the unauthorized installation and use of software. When you install software, you must accept the license governing its use, often called the end-user license agreement. The terms of the license will vary according to the type of software, but the","options":["On Site versus Off Site Storage","Backup Methods","End-User License Agreements","A general order of volatility from most to least volatile is:"],"answer":2,"explanation":"End-User License Agreements: Prohibited content also extends to the unauthorized installation and use of software. When you install software, you must accept the license governing its use, often called the end-user license agreement. The terms of the license will vary according to the type of software, but the"},{"id":"c2-0374","module":21,"lesson":"21C Artificial Intelligence","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nSoftware is often activated using a product key, which will be a long string of characters and numbers. The product key will generate a different product ID, which is often used to obtain The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 21: Using Data Security","context":"Software is often activated using a product key, which will be a long string of characters and numbers. The product key will generate a different product ID, which is often used to obtain The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 21: Using Data Security","options":["Chain of Custody","3-2-1 Backup Rule","License Compliance Monitoring","Two main factors govern backup operations:"],"answer":2,"explanation":"License Compliance Monitoring: Software is often activated using a product key, which will be a long string of characters and numbers. The product key will generate a different product ID, which is often used to obtain The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 21: Using Data Security"},{"id":"c2-0375","module":21,"lesson":"21A Data Backup & Recovery","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nSoftware released under an open-source license generally makes it free to use, modify, and share and makes the program code used to design it available. The idea is that other programmers can investigate the program and make it more stable and useful. An open-source","context":"Software released under an open-source license generally makes it free to use, modify, and share and makes the program code used to design it available. The idea is that other programmers can investigate the program and make it more stable and useful. An open-source","options":["Backup Testing and Recovery Best Practices","Open-source Licenses","Data Destruction Methods","Backup Chains"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Open-source Licenses: Software released under an open-source license generally makes it free to use, modify, and share and makes the program code used to design it available. The idea is that other programmers can investigate the program and make it more stable and useful. An open-source"},{"id":"c2-0376","module":21,"lesson":"21B Data Handling Best Practices","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nDigital music and video are often subject to copy protection and digital rights management.","context":"Digital music and video are often subject to copy protection and digital rights management.","options":["Type Data Selection Backup Job Time Recovery Archive Attribute","Digital Rights Management","Recovery Options","Erasing / Wiping"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Digital Rights Management: Digital music and video are often subject to copy protection and digital rights management."},{"id":"c2-0377","module":21,"lesson":"21C Artificial Intelligence","question":"What is being described here?\n\nNon-disclosure agreements (NDA) are agreements designed to protect sensitive information. A NDA is a legally binding contract that obligates all parties to protect sensitive information that is shared between them. An NDA agreement can be either unilateral or mutual:","context":"Non-disclosure agreements (NDA) are agreements designed to protect sensitive information. A NDA is a legally binding contract that obligates all parties to protect sensitive information that is shared between them. An NDA agreement can be either unilateral or mutual:","options":["Regulated Data Classification","Low Level Format","Non-Disclosure Agreements","Requirement"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Non-Disclosure Agreements: Non-disclosure agreements (NDA) are agreements designed to protect sensitive information. A NDA is a legally binding contract that obligates all parties to protect sensitive information that is shared between them. An NDA agreement can be either unilateral or mutual:"},{"id":"c2-0378","module":21,"lesson":"21A Data Backup & Recovery","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nWhile performing technical support, you may have to report or respond to security incidents. A security incident could be one of a wide range of different scenarios, such as: • A computer or network infected with viruses, worms, or Trojans.","context":"While performing technical support, you may have to report or respond to security incidents. A security incident could be one of a wide range of different scenarios, such as: • A computer or network infected with viruses, worms, or Trojans.","options":["Backup Operations","Incident Response","Personally Identifiable Information","Synthetic Full Backup"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Incident Response: While performing technical support, you may have to report or respond to security incidents. A security incident could be one of a wide range of different scenarios, such as: • A computer or network infected with viruses, worms, or Trojans."},{"id":"c2-0379","module":21,"lesson":"21B Data Handling Best Practices","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nForensics is the science of collecting evidence from computer systems to a standard that will be accepted in a court of law. Like DNA or fingerprints, digital evidence is mostly latent. Latent means that the evidence cannot be seen with the naked eye; rather, it must be interpreted using","context":"Forensics is the science of collecting evidence from computer systems to a standard that will be accepted in a court of law. Like DNA or fingerprints, digital evidence is mostly latent. Latent means that the evidence cannot be seen with the naked eye; rather, it must be interpreted using","options":["Data Integrity and Preservation","Backup Media Requirements","Backup Methods","Personal Government-issued Information"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Data Integrity and Preservation: Forensics is the science of collecting evidence from computer systems to a standard that will be accepted in a court of law. Like DNA or fingerprints, digital evidence is mostly latent. Latent means that the evidence cannot be seen with the naked eye; rather, it must be interpreted using"},{"id":"c2-0380","module":21,"lesson":"21C Artificial Intelligence","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nWhen recovering evidence, it is important to follow the order of volatility. This is the order that data should be collected based on how long it is likely to remain available. The most volatile data should be collected first and then go down the list to the data that is least likely to","context":"When recovering evidence, it is important to follow the order of volatility. This is the order that data should be collected based on how long it is likely to remain available. The most volatile data should be collected first and then go down the list to the data that is least likely to","options":["Healthcare Data","Two main factors govern backup operations:","Order of Volatility","Grandfather - Father - Son (GFS) Scheme"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Order of Volatility: When recovering evidence, it is important to follow the order of volatility. This is the order that data should be collected based on how long it is likely to remain available. The most volatile data should be collected first and then go down the list to the data that is least likely to"},{"id":"c2-0381","module":21,"lesson":"21A Data Backup & Recovery","question":"What is being described here?\n\n1. CPU cache and registers - This data is extremely volatile and changes rapidly.","context":"1. CPU cache and registers - This data is extremely volatile and changes rapidly.","options":["A general order of volatility from most to least volatile is:","Credit Card Transactions","Backup Chains","On Site versus Off Site Storage"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A general order of volatility from most to least volatile is:: 1. CPU cache and registers - This data is extremely volatile and changes rapidly."},{"id":"c2-0382","module":21,"lesson":"21B Data Handling Best Practices","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nIt is vital that the evidence collected at the crime scene conforms to a valid timeline. Digital information is susceptible to tampering, so access to the evidence must be tightly controlled.","context":"It is vital that the evidence collected at the crime scene conforms to a valid timeline. Digital information is susceptible to tampering, so access to the evidence must be tightly controlled.","options":["3-2-1 Backup Rule","Data Handling Best Practices","Chain of Custody","Type Data Selection Backup Job Time Recovery Archive Attribute"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Chain of Custody: It is vital that the evidence collected at the crime scene conforms to a valid timeline. Digital information is susceptible to tampering, so access to the evidence must be tightly controlled."},{"id":"c2-0383","module":21,"lesson":"21C Artificial Intelligence","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nData destruction and disposal refer to either destroying or decommissioning data storage media, including hard disks, flash drives, tape media, and CDs/DVDs. The problem has become particularly prominent as organizations repurpose and recycle their old computers, either by","context":"Data destruction and disposal refer to either destroying or decommissioning data storage media, including hard disks, flash drives, tape media, and CDs/DVDs. The problem has become particularly prominent as organizations repurpose and recycle their old computers, either by","options":["Data Retention Requirements","Backup Testing and Recovery Best Practices","Requirement","Data Destruction Methods"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Data Destruction Methods: Data destruction and disposal refer to either destroying or decommissioning data storage media, including hard disks, flash drives, tape media, and CDs/DVDs. The problem has become particularly prominent as organizations repurpose and recycle their old computers, either by"},{"id":"c2-0384","module":21,"lesson":"21A Data Backup & Recovery","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nDisk erasing/wiping software ensures that old data is destroyed by writing to each location on a hard disk drive, either using zeroes or in a random pattern. This leaves the disk in a \"clean\" state ready to be passed to the new owner. This overwriting method is suitable for all but the","context":"Disk erasing/wiping software ensures that old data is destroyed by writing to each location on a hard disk drive, either using zeroes or in a random pattern. This leaves the disk in a \"clean\" state ready to be passed to the new owner. This overwriting method is suitable for all but the","options":["Recovery Options","Prohibited Content and Licensing Issues","Synthetic Full Backup","Erasing / Wiping"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Erasing / Wiping: Disk erasing/wiping software ensures that old data is destroyed by writing to each location on a hard disk drive, either using zeroes or in a random pattern. This leaves the disk in a \"clean\" state ready to be passed to the new owner. This overwriting method is suitable for all but the"},{"id":"c2-0385","module":21,"lesson":"21B Data Handling Best Practices","question":"What is being described here?\n\nMost disk vendors supply low-level formatting tools to reset a disk to its factory condition. Most of these tools will now incorporate some type of sanitize function. You must verify the specific capability of each disk model, but the following functions are typical:","context":"Most disk vendors supply low-level formatting tools to reset a disk to its factory condition. Most of these tools will now incorporate some type of sanitize function. You must verify the specific capability of each disk model, but the following functions are typical:","options":["Regulated Data Classification","Backup Media Requirements","Low Level Format","Prohibited Content"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Low Level Format: Most disk vendors supply low-level formatting tools to reset a disk to its factory condition. Most of these tools will now incorporate some type of sanitize function. You must verify the specific capability of each disk model, but the following functions are typical:"},{"id":"c2-0386","module":22,"lesson":"22A Change Management","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nChange management (CM) refers to policies and procedures that reduce the risk of configuration changes causing service downtime. Change management is closely related to configuration management.","context":"Change management (CM) refers to policies and procedures that reduce the risk of configuration changes causing service downtime. Change management is closely related to configuration management.","options":["Proper Component Handling","Other Safety Hazard Mitigations","Change Requests","Safety Equipment and Tools"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Change Requests: Change management (CM) refers to policies and procedures that reduce the risk of configuration changes causing service downtime. Change management is closely related to configuration management."},{"id":"c2-0387","module":22,"lesson":"22B Safety & Environment","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nEach change being requested should undergo a risk analysis. Risk analysis is a systematic approach to identify possible risks associated with implementing the change. Could the change cause a temporary outage of a critical system or service? Is there a risk of injury or death to the","context":"Each change being requested should undergo a risk analysis. Risk analysis is a systematic approach to identify possible risks associated with implementing the change. Could the change cause a temporary outage of a critical system or service? Is there a risk of injury or death to the","options":["Fuses","Trip Hazards","Electrostatic Discharge (ESD) wrist strap on ESD mat","Risk Analysis"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Risk Analysis: Each change being requested should undergo a risk analysis. Risk analysis is a systematic approach to identify possible risks associated with implementing the change. Could the change cause a temporary outage of a critical system or service? Is there a risk of injury or death to the"},{"id":"c2-0388","module":22,"lesson":"22C Scripting Basics","question":"What is being described here?\n\nWhen a change request has been drafted and submitted, it must go through an approval process.","context":"When a change request has been drafted and submitted, it must go through an approval process.","options":["Change Board Approvals","Lifting Techniques","Equipment Grounding","An example of an electrostatic discharge (ESD) workstation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Change Board Approvals: When a change request has been drafted and submitted, it must go through an approval process."},{"id":"c2-0389","module":22,"lesson":"22A Change Management","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nImplementation is when we have the approval to make a change and we have the authority to make the change. This process may require multiple steps to be followed to accomplish the change task.","context":"Implementation is when we have the approval to make a change and we have the authority to make the change. This process may require multiple steps to be followed to accomplish the change task.","options":["Proper Power Handling and Personal Safety","Environmental Impacts","Building Power Issues and Mitigations","Implementation and Acceptance"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Implementation and Acceptance: Implementation is when we have the approval to make a change and we have the authority to make the change. This process may require multiple steps to be followed to accomplish the change task."},{"id":"c2-0390","module":22,"lesson":"22B Safety & Environment","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nWhile troubleshooting or implementing changes to a system or service, it will be important to ensure you are working on the correct system or service. Accurate tracking of what physical assets your company or organization has is just as important as fixing the issues with those","context":"While troubleshooting or implementing changes to a system or service, it will be important to ensure you are working on the correct system or service. Accurate tracking of what physical assets your company or organization has is just as important as fixing the issues with those","options":["Dust Cleanup","Surge Suppressors","Electrical Fire Safety","Asset Management"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Asset Management: While troubleshooting or implementing changes to a system or service, it will be important to ensure you are working on the correct system or service. Accurate tracking of what physical assets your company or organization has is just as important as fixing the issues with those"},{"id":"c2-0391","module":22,"lesson":"22C Scripting Basics","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nEach asset record should include appropriate procurement documentation, such as the invoice and warranty/support contract (along with appropriate contact information). For software, it should record the licensing details with device/user allocations and limits.","context":"Each asset record should include appropriate procurement documentation, such as the invoice and warranty/support contract (along with appropriate contact information). For software, it should record the licensing details with device/user allocations and limits.","options":["Air filter mask","Warranty and Licensing","Battery Backups","Equipment Placement"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Warranty and Licensing: Each asset record should include appropriate procurement documentation, such as the invoice and warranty/support contract (along with appropriate contact information). For software, it should record the licensing details with device/user allocations and limits."},{"id":"c2-0392","module":22,"lesson":"22A Change Management","question":"What is being described here?\n\nWhen performing PC maintenance work, you may need to take into account compliance with government regulations. Regulations that typically affect PC maintenance or the installation of new equipment are: • Health and safety laws— Keeping the workplace free from hazards.","context":"When performing PC maintenance work, you may need to take into account compliance with government regulations. Regulations that typically affect PC maintenance or the installation of new equipment are: • Health and safety laws— Keeping the workplace free from hazards.","options":["Example of a UPS","Temperature, Humidity, and Ventilation Control","Other Safety Hazard Mitigations","Compliance with Regulations"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Compliance with Regulations: When performing PC maintenance work, you may need to take into account compliance with government regulations. Regulations that typically affect PC maintenance or the installation of new equipment are: • Health and safety laws— Keeping the workplace free from hazards."},{"id":"c2-0393","module":22,"lesson":"22B Safety & Environment","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nElectricity flows in a circuit. A circuit is made when conductors form a continuous path between the positive and negative terminals of a power source. An electrical circuit has the following properties: • Current is the amount of charge flowing through a conductor, measured in amps (A or I).","context":"Electricity flows in a circuit. A circuit is made when conductors form a continuous path between the positive and negative terminals of a power source. An electrical circuit has the following properties: • Current is the amount of charge flowing through a conductor, measured in amps (A or I).","options":["Electrical Safety","Trip Hazards","Materials Handling and Responsible Disposal","Electrostatic Discharge Mitigation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Electrical Safety: Electricity flows in a circuit. A circuit is made when conductors form a continuous path between the positive and negative terminals of a power source. An electrical circuit has the following properties: • Current is the amount of charge flowing through a conductor, measured in amps (A or I)."},{"id":"c2-0394","module":22,"lesson":"22C Scripting Basics","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nWhile working with electronic equipment, it would be wise to utilize insulated tools and gloves to protect yourself from harm. You may also want to test the component with a voltage tester before attempting to work on it. Ensuring there is no voltage present can protect you from","context":"While working with electronic equipment, it would be wise to utilize insulated tools and gloves to protect yourself from harm. You may also want to test the component with a voltage tester before attempting to work on it. Ensuring there is no voltage present can protect you from","options":["Proper Component Handling","Lifting Techniques","Material Safety Data Sheets","Safety Equipment and Tools"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Safety Equipment and Tools: While working with electronic equipment, it would be wise to utilize insulated tools and gloves to protect yourself from harm. You may also want to test the component with a voltage tester before attempting to work on it. Ensuring there is no voltage present can protect you from"},{"id":"c2-0395","module":22,"lesson":"22A Change Management","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nAn electrical device must be fitted with a fuse appropriate to its maximum current, such as 3A, 5A, or 13A. A fuse blows if there is a problem with the electrical supply, breaking the circuit to the power source. If the fuse fitted is rated too low, it will blow too easily; if the rating is too","context":"An electrical device must be fitted with a fuse appropriate to its maximum current, such as 3A, 5A, or 13A. A fuse blows if there is a problem with the electrical supply, breaking the circuit to the power source. If the fuse fitted is rated too low, it will blow too easily; if the rating is too","options":["Electrostatic Discharge (ESD) wrist strap on ESD mat","Fuses","Environmental Impacts","Proper Disposal"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Fuses: An electrical device must be fitted with a fuse appropriate to its maximum current, such as 3A, 5A, or 13A. A fuse blows if there is a problem with the electrical supply, breaking the circuit to the power source. If the fuse fitted is rated too low, it will blow too easily; if the rating is too"},{"id":"c2-0396","module":22,"lesson":"22B Safety & Environment","question":"What is being described here?\n\nElectrical equipment must be grounded. If there is a fault that causes metal parts in the equipment to become live in the circuit, the ground provides a path of least resistance for the electrical current to flow away harmlessly. Devices such as PCs and printers are connected to","context":"Electrical equipment must be grounded. If there is a fault that causes metal parts in the equipment to become live in the circuit, the ground provides a path of least resistance for the electrical current to flow away harmlessly. Devices such as PCs and printers are connected to","options":["An example of an electrostatic discharge (ESD) workstation","Equipment Grounding","Special care must be taken with respect to the following device types:","Dust Cleanup"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Equipment Grounding: Electrical equipment must be grounded. If there is a fault that causes metal parts in the equipment to become live in the circuit, the ground provides a path of least resistance for the electrical current to flow away harmlessly. Devices such as PCs and printers are connected to"},{"id":"c2-0397","module":22,"lesson":"22C Scripting Basics","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nWhenever you add or replace components within a PC or laptop, the power must be disconnected first. Remove the AC plug and also remove the battery if present. Hold down the power button on the device to ensure the circuits are drained of any residual power from the","context":"Whenever you add or replace components within a PC or laptop, the power must be disconnected first. Remove the AC plug and also remove the battery if present. Hold down the power button on the device to ensure the circuits are drained of any residual power from the","options":["Building Power Issues and Mitigations","Proper Power Handling and Personal Safety","4.8 Identify the basics of scripting","Air filter mask"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Proper Power Handling and Personal Safety: Whenever you add or replace components within a PC or laptop, the power must be disconnected first. Remove the AC plug and also remove the battery if present. Hold down the power button on the device to ensure the circuits are drained of any residual power from the"},{"id":"c2-0398","module":22,"lesson":"22A Change Management","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nFaulty electrical equipment can pose a fire risk. If the equipment allows more current to flow through a cable than the cable is rated for, the cable will heat up. This could ignite flammable material close to the cable. If an electrical wire does start a fire, it is important to use the","context":"Faulty electrical equipment can pose a fire risk. If the equipment allows more current to flow through a cable than the cable is rated for, the cable will heat up. This could ignite flammable material close to the cable. If an electrical wire does start a fire, it is important to use the","options":["Surge Suppressors","An example of a Linux shell script open in the Vim text editor","Temperature, Humidity, and Ventilation Control","Electrical Fire Safety"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Electrical Fire Safety: Faulty electrical equipment can pose a fire risk. If the equipment allows more current to flow through a cable than the cable is rated for, the cable will heat up. This could ignite flammable material close to the cable. If an electrical wire does start a fire, it is important to use the"},{"id":"c2-0399","module":22,"lesson":"22B Safety & Environment","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nEquipment placement or location is just as important as the equipment itself. Ensuring equipment is located in a secure location near a power source and out of public access ensures that only authorized personnel can access it. Servers and other networking equipment should","context":"Equipment placement or location is just as important as the equipment itself. Ensuring equipment is located in a secure location near a power source and out of public access ensures that only authorized personnel can access it. Servers and other networking equipment should","options":["Battery Backups","Electrostatic Discharge Mitigation","Equipment Placement","Change Requests"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Equipment Placement: Equipment placement or location is just as important as the equipment itself. Ensuring equipment is located in a secure location near a power source and out of public access ensures that only authorized personnel can access it. Servers and other networking equipment should"},{"id":"c2-0400","module":22,"lesson":"22C Scripting Basics","question":"What is being described here?\n\nIn addition to electrical hazards, there are other safety hazards that computer technicians must account for.","context":"In addition to electrical hazards, there are other safety hazards that computer technicians must account for.","options":["Example of a UPS","Risk Analysis","Proper Component Handling","Other Safety Hazard Mitigations"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Other Safety Hazard Mitigations: In addition to electrical hazards, there are other safety hazards that computer technicians must account for."},{"id":"c2-0401","module":22,"lesson":"22A Change Management","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nA trip hazard is caused by putting any object in pathways where people walk.","context":"A trip hazard is caused by putting any object in pathways where people walk.","options":["Materials Handling and Responsible Disposal","Trip Hazards","Change Board Approvals","Electrostatic Discharge (ESD) wrist strap on ESD mat"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Trip Hazards: A trip hazard is caused by putting any object in pathways where people walk."},{"id":"c2-0402","module":22,"lesson":"22B Safety & Environment","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nLifting a heavy object in the wrong way can damage your back or cause muscle strains and ligament damage. You may also drop the object and injure yourself or damage the object. When you need to lift or carry items, be aware of the maximum safe lifting weight as well as any","context":"Lifting a heavy object in the wrong way can damage your back or cause muscle strains and ligament damage. You may also drop the object and injure yourself or damage the object. When you need to lift or carry items, be aware of the maximum safe lifting weight as well as any","options":["Material Safety Data Sheets","An example of an electrostatic discharge (ESD) workstation","Lifting Techniques","Implementation and Acceptance"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Lifting Techniques: Lifting a heavy object in the wrong way can damage your back or cause muscle strains and ligament damage. You may also drop the object and injure yourself or damage the object. When you need to lift or carry items, be aware of the maximum safe lifting weight as well as any"},{"id":"c2-0403","module":22,"lesson":"22C Scripting Basics","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nThe location in which computer equipment is placed can affect its proper operation and lifespan. All electronic equipment should be kept away from extremes of temperature and damp or dusty conditions.","context":"The location in which computer equipment is placed can affect its proper operation and lifespan. All electronic equipment should be kept away from extremes of temperature and damp or dusty conditions.","options":["Environmental Impacts","Proper Disposal","Asset Management","Building Power Issues and Mitigations"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Environmental Impacts: The location in which computer equipment is placed can affect its proper operation and lifespan. All electronic equipment should be kept away from extremes of temperature and damp or dusty conditions."},{"id":"c2-0404","module":22,"lesson":"22A Change Management","question":"What is being described here?\n\nDust is drawn into the computer via ventilation holes. Over time, the dust can form a thick layer over components, heat sinks, fan blades, peripheral connection ports, and ventilation slots, preventing effective heat dissipation. It can clog up peripherals such as keyboards and mice.","context":"Dust is drawn into the computer via ventilation holes. Over time, the dust can form a thick layer over components, heat sinks, fan blades, peripheral connection ports, and ventilation slots, preventing effective heat dissipation. It can clog up peripherals such as keyboards and mice.","options":["Special care must be taken with respect to the following device types:","Surge Suppressors","Warranty and Licensing","Dust Cleanup"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Dust Cleanup: Dust is drawn into the computer via ventilation holes. Over time, the dust can form a thick layer over components, heat sinks, fan blades, peripheral connection ports, and ventilation slots, preventing effective heat dissipation. It can clog up peripherals such as keyboards and mice."},{"id":"c2-0405","module":22,"lesson":"22B Safety & Environment","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nImage © 123RF.com Note: Do not use compressed air blasters to clean up a toner spill or a laser printer within an office-type area. You will blow fine toner dust into the atmosphere and create a health hazard.","context":"Image © 123RF.com Note: Do not use compressed air blasters to clean up a toner spill or a laser printer within an office-type area. You will blow fine toner dust into the atmosphere and create a health hazard.","options":["4.8 Identify the basics of scripting","Battery Backups","Compliance with Regulations","Air filter mask"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Air filter mask: Image © 123RF.com Note: Do not use compressed air blasters to clean up a toner spill or a laser printer within an office-type area. You will blow fine toner dust into the atmosphere and create a health hazard."},{"id":"c2-0406","module":22,"lesson":"22C Scripting Basics","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nA computer that is too hot is likely to be unreliable. A computer must be ventilated so that its fans can draw relatively cool air across the motherboard and expel the warmed air from the rear vents. You must ensure that the room (ambient) temperature is not too high and that there is","context":"A computer that is too hot is likely to be unreliable. A computer must be ventilated so that its fans can draw relatively cool air across the motherboard and expel the warmed air from the rear vents. You must ensure that the room (ambient) temperature is not too high and that there is","options":["An example of a Linux shell script open in the Vim text editor","Electrical Safety","Temperature, Humidity, and Ventilation Control","Example of a UPS"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Temperature, Humidity, and Ventilation Control: A computer that is too hot is likely to be unreliable. A computer must be ventilated so that its fans can draw relatively cool air across the motherboard and expel the warmed air from the rear vents. You must ensure that the room (ambient) temperature is not too high and that there is"},{"id":"c2-0407","module":22,"lesson":"22A Change Management","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nStatic electricity is a high voltage, low current charge stored in an insulated body. Electrostatic discharge occurs when a path allows electrons to rush from a statically charged body to a component that has no charge. This can occur through touch or even over a small gap if the","context":"Static electricity is a high voltage, low current charge stored in an insulated body. Electrostatic discharge occurs when a path allows electrons to rush from a statically charged body to a component that has no charge. This can occur through touch or even over a small gap if the","options":["Change Requests","Safety Equipment and Tools","Materials Handling and Responsible Disposal","Electrostatic Discharge Mitigation"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Electrostatic Discharge Mitigation: Static electricity is a high voltage, low current charge stored in an insulated body. Electrostatic discharge occurs when a path allows electrons to rush from a statically charged body to a component that has no charge. This can occur through touch or even over a small gap if the"},{"id":"c2-0408","module":22,"lesson":"22B Safety & Environment","question":"What is being described here?\n\nProper component handling tools and techniques protect electronic components against ESD when you service a PC or mobile device: • If possible, work in an uncarpeted area. Ideally, use an ESD-safe floor or chair mat.","context":"Proper component handling tools and techniques protect electronic components against ESD when you service a PC or mobile device: • If possible, work in an uncarpeted area. Ideally, use an ESD-safe floor or chair mat.","options":["Proper Component Handling","Risk Analysis","Material Safety Data Sheets","Fuses"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Proper Component Handling: Proper component handling tools and techniques protect electronic components against ESD when you service a PC or mobile device: • If possible, work in an uncarpeted area. Ideally, use an ESD-safe floor or chair mat."},{"id":"c2-0409","module":22,"lesson":"22C Scripting Basics","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nThe Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 22: Implementing Operational Procedures Image by Audrius Merfeldas ©123RF.com.","context":"The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 22: Implementing Operational Procedures Image by Audrius Merfeldas ©123RF.com.","options":["Equipment Grounding","Change Board Approvals","Proper Disposal","Electrostatic Discharge (ESD) wrist strap on ESD mat"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Electrostatic Discharge (ESD) wrist strap on ESD mat: The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 22: Implementing Operational Procedures Image by Audrius Merfeldas ©123RF.com."},{"id":"c2-0410","module":22,"lesson":"22A Change Management","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nThe Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 22: Implementing Operational Procedures Image by Audrius Merfeldas ©123RF.com.","context":"The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 22: Implementing Operational Procedures Image by Audrius Merfeldas ©123RF.com.","options":["Special care must be taken with respect to the following device types:","Implementation and Acceptance","Proper Power Handling and Personal Safety","An example of an electrostatic discharge (ESD) workstation"],"answer":3,"explanation":"An example of an electrostatic discharge (ESD) workstation: The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 22: Implementing Operational Procedures Image by Audrius Merfeldas ©123RF.com."},{"id":"c2-0411","module":22,"lesson":"22B Safety & Environment","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nFaults in building power supply cause power problems such as surges, brownouts, and blackouts: • Surges— A surge is a brief increase in voltage, while a spike is an intense surge. A surge or spike can be caused by machinery and other high-power devices being turned on or off and","context":"Faults in building power supply cause power problems such as surges, brownouts, and blackouts: • Surges— A surge is a brief increase in voltage, while a spike is an intense surge. A surge or spike can be caused by machinery and other high-power devices being turned on or off and","options":["Asset Management","Electrical Fire Safety","Building Power Issues and Mitigations","4.8 Identify the basics of scripting"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Building Power Issues and Mitigations: Faults in building power supply cause power problems such as surges, brownouts, and blackouts: • Surges— A surge is a brief increase in voltage, while a spike is an intense surge. A surge or spike can be caused by machinery and other high-power devices being turned on or off and"},{"id":"c2-0412","module":22,"lesson":"22C Scripting Basics","question":"What is being described here?\n\nPassive protection devices can be used to filter out the effects of surges and spikes. The simplest surge suppressor devices come in the form of adapters, trailing sockets, or filter plugs, with the protection circuitry built into the unit. These devices offer low-cost protection to","context":"Passive protection devices can be used to filter out the effects of surges and spikes. The simplest surge suppressor devices come in the form of adapters, trailing sockets, or filter plugs, with the protection circuitry built into the unit. These devices offer low-cost protection to","options":["Equipment Placement","An example of a Linux shell script open in the Vim text editor","Surge Suppressors","Warranty and Licensing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Surge Suppressors: Passive protection devices can be used to filter out the effects of surges and spikes. The simplest surge suppressor devices come in the form of adapters, trailing sockets, or filter plugs, with the protection circuitry built into the unit. These devices offer low-cost protection to"},{"id":"c2-0413","module":22,"lesson":"22A Change Management","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nSudden power loss is likely to cause file corruption. If there is loss of power due to a brownout or blackout, system operation can be sustained for a few minutes by using battery backup.","context":"Sudden power loss is likely to cause file corruption. If there is loss of power due to a brownout or blackout, system operation can be sustained for a few minutes by using battery backup.","options":["Other Safety Hazard Mitigations","Battery Backups","Change Requests","Compliance with Regulations"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Battery Backups: Sudden power loss is likely to cause file corruption. If there is loss of power due to a brownout or blackout, system operation can be sustained for a few minutes by using battery backup."},{"id":"c2-0414","module":22,"lesson":"22B Safety & Environment","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\nImage by magraphics© 123RF.com. The key characteristics of a UPS are volt-amperes (VA) rating and runtime: • VA rating is the maximum load the UPS can sustain. To work out the minimum VA, sum the wattage of all the devices that will be attached to the UPS and multiply by 1.67 to account for","context":"Image by magraphics© 123RF.com. The key characteristics of a UPS are volt-amperes (VA) rating and runtime: • VA rating is the maximum load the UPS can sustain. To work out the minimum VA, sum the wattage of all the devices that will be attached to the UPS and multiply by 1.67 to account for","options":["Electrical Safety","Example of a UPS","Trip Hazards","Risk Analysis"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Example of a UPS: Image by magraphics© 123RF.com. The key characteristics of a UPS are volt-amperes (VA) rating and runtime: • VA rating is the maximum load the UPS can sustain. To work out the minimum VA, sum the wattage of all the devices that will be attached to the UPS and multiply by 1.67 to account for"},{"id":"c2-0415","module":22,"lesson":"22C Scripting Basics","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nSome of the components and consumables used with computer and printer systems can be hazardous to health and to the environment. You must comply with all relevant regulations when handling and disposing of these substances.","context":"Some of the components and consumables used with computer and printer systems can be hazardous to health and to the environment. You must comply with all relevant regulations when handling and disposing of these substances.","options":["Materials Handling and Responsible Disposal","Safety Equipment and Tools","Lifting Techniques","Change Board Approvals"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Materials Handling and Responsible Disposal: Some of the components and consumables used with computer and printer systems can be hazardous to health and to the environment. You must comply with all relevant regulations when handling and disposing of these substances."},{"id":"c2-0416","module":22,"lesson":"22A Change Management","question":"What is being described here?\n\nEmployers are obliged to assess the risk to their workforce from hazardous substances at work and to take steps to eliminate or control that risk. No work with hazardous substances should take place unless an assessment has been made. Employees are within their rights to refuse to","context":"Employers are obliged to assess the risk to their workforce from hazardous substances at work and to take steps to eliminate or control that risk. No work with hazardous substances should take place unless an assessment has been made. Employees are within their rights to refuse to","options":["Material Safety Data Sheets","Environmental Impacts","Fuses","Implementation and Acceptance"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Material Safety Data Sheets: Employers are obliged to assess the risk to their workforce from hazardous substances at work and to take steps to eliminate or control that risk. No work with hazardous substances should take place unless an assessment has been made. Employees are within their rights to refuse to"},{"id":"c2-0417","module":22,"lesson":"22B Safety & Environment","question":"Which item from this module matches the description?\n\nEven with procedures in place to properly maintain IT equipment, eventually, it will need to be decommissioned and either disposed of or recycled. IT equipment contains numerous components and materials that can cause environmental damage if they are disposed of","context":"Even with procedures in place to properly maintain IT equipment, eventually, it will need to be decommissioned and either disposed of or recycled. IT equipment contains numerous components and materials that can cause environmental damage if they are disposed of","options":["Equipment Grounding","Proper Disposal","Asset Management","Dust Cleanup"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Proper Disposal: Even with procedures in place to properly maintain IT equipment, eventually, it will need to be decommissioned and either disposed of or recycled. IT equipment contains numerous components and materials that can cause environmental damage if they are disposed of"},{"id":"c2-0418","module":22,"lesson":"22C Scripting Basics","question":"Which Core 2 term is described by the following?\n\n• Battery disposal— Swollen or leaking batteries from laptop computers or cell phones and tablets must be handled very carefully and stored within appropriate containers. Use gloves and safety goggles to minimize any risk of burns from corrosive material. Batteries must be","context":"• Battery disposal— Swollen or leaking batteries from laptop computers or cell phones and tablets must be handled very carefully and stored within appropriate containers. Use gloves and safety goggles to minimize any risk of burns from corrosive material. Batteries must be","options":["Warranty and Licensing","Proper Power Handling and Personal Safety","Special care must be taken with respect to the following device types:","Air filter mask"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Special care must be taken with respect to the following device types:: • Battery disposal— Swollen or leaking batteries from laptop computers or cell phones and tablets must be handled very carefully and stored within appropriate containers. Use gloves and safety goggles to minimize any risk of burns from corrosive material. Batteries must be"},{"id":"c2-0419","module":22,"lesson":"22A Change Management","question":"Which concept matches this description?\n\nLearning Outcomes As you study this lesson, answer the following questions: • What is a script?","context":"Learning Outcomes As you study this lesson, answer the following questions: • What is a script?","options":["Temperature, Humidity, and Ventilation Control","Electrical Fire Safety","Compliance with Regulations","4.8 Identify the basics of scripting"],"answer":3,"explanation":"4.8 Identify the basics of scripting: Learning Outcomes As you study this lesson, answer the following questions: • What is a script?"},{"id":"c2-0420","module":22,"lesson":"22B Safety & Environment","question":"What is being described here?\n\nRemember that in Linux, the script file must have the execute permission set to run. Execute can be set as a permission for the user, group, or world (everyone). If a PATH variable to the script has not been configured, execute it from the working directory by preceding the filename","context":"Remember that in Linux, the script file must have the execute permission set to run. Execute can be set as a permission for the user, group, or world (everyone). If a PATH variable to the script has not been configured, execute it from the working directory by preceding the filename","options":["An example of a Linux shell script open in the Vim text editor","Electrostatic Discharge Mitigation","Electrical Safety","Equipment Placement"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An example of a Linux shell script open in the Vim text editor: Remember that in Linux, the script file must have the execute permission set to run. Execute can be set as a permission for the user, group, or world (everyone). If a PATH variable to the script has not been configured, execute it from the working directory by preceding the filename"}],"flashcards":{"11":[{"term":"Standard Operating Procedure","def":"Employees must understand how to use computers and networked services securely and safely and be aware of their responsibilities. To support this, the organization needs to create written policies and procedures to help staff understand and fulfill their tasks and follow best practices:"},{"term":"Typical examples of SOPs are as follows:","def":"• Procedures for custom installation of software packages, such as verifying system requirements, validating download/installation source, confirming license validity, adding the software to change control/monitoring processes, and developing support/training documentation"},{"term":"Service Level Agreements","def":"Service level agreements (SLAs) define the level of service requirements from an internal department or external, third-party vendor. Examples of services that most likely have an SLA in place include: • Internal departments of the company that are providing resources to one another such as"},{"term":"Incident Reporting and Ticketing Systems","def":"A ticketing system manages requests, incidents, and problems. Ticketing systems can be used to support both internal end-users and external customers."},{"term":"The general process of ticket management is as follows:","def":"1. A user contacts the help desk, perhaps by phone or email, or directly via the ticketing system."},{"term":"Categories and Severity","def":"Categories Categories and subcategories group related tickets together. This is useful for assigning tickets to the relevant support section or technician and for reporting and analysis."},{"term":"Categories","def":"Categories and subcategories group related tickets together. This is useful for assigning tickets to the relevant support section or technician and for reporting and analysis."},{"term":"Ticket Management","def":"After opening an incident or problem ticket, the troubleshooting process is applied until the issue is resolved. At each stage, the system must track the ownership of the ticket (who is dealing with it) and its status (what has been done)."},{"term":"Escalation Levels","def":"Escalation occurs when an agent cannot resolve the ticket. Some of the many reasons for escalation include: • The incident is related to a problem and requires analysis by senior technicians or by a third-party/warranty support service."},{"term":"Support Documentation and Knowledge Base Articles","def":"It is also useful to link an inventory record to appropriate troubleshooting and support sources."},{"term":"Clear Written Communication","def":"Free-form text fields allow ticket requesters and agents to add descriptive information. There are normally three fields to reflect the ticket life cycle: • Issue description records the initial request with any detail that could easily be collected at the time."},{"term":"Knowledge Base","def":"A knowledge base is a self-serve central repository for information. IT service organizations will normally house troubleshooting articles that end users can refer to when attempting to self-correct or diagnose an issue. Frequently asked questions (FAQs) may also be provided to"},{"term":"Policy Documentation","def":"An acceptable use policy (AUP) sets out what someone is allowed to use a particular service or resource for. Such a policy might be used in different contexts. For example, an AUP could be enforced by a business to govern how employees use equipment and services such as"},{"term":"Professional Support Processes","def":"From the point of first contact, the support process must reassure customers that their inquiry will be handled efficiently. If the customer has already encountered a problem with a product, finding that the support process is also faulty will double their poor impression of your"},{"term":"Professional Documentation","def":"Support contact information and hours of operation should be well-advertised so that the customer knows exactly how to open a ticket. The service should have proper documentation so the customer knows what to expect regarding supported items, how long incidents may take"},{"term":"Set Expectations and Timeline","def":"On receiving the request (whether it is a call, email, or face-to-face contact), acknowledge the request and set expectations. For example, repeat the request back to the customer, then state the next steps and establish a timeline. For example, \"I have assigned this problem to"},{"term":"Meet Expectations and Timeline","def":"If possible, the request should be resolved in one call. If this is not possible, the call should be dealt with as quickly as possible and escalated to a senior support team if a solution cannot be found promptly. What is important is that you drive problem acceptance and resolution, either"},{"term":"Repair and Replace Options","def":"If there is a product issue that cannot be solved remotely, you might offer to repair or replace the product: • Repair-—The customer will need clear instructions about how to pack and return the item to a repair center, along with a ticket-tracking number and returned-merchandise authorization"},{"term":"Follow Up","def":"If you have resolved the ticket and tested that the system is operating normally again, you should give the customer a general indication of what caused the issue and what you did to fix it, along with assurance that the problem is now fixed and unlikely to reoccur. Upon leaving"},{"term":"Professional Support Delivery","def":"Respect means that you treat others (and their property) as you would like to be treated."},{"term":"Be On Time","def":"Ensure that you are on time for each in-person appointment or contact call. If it becomes obvious that you are not going to be on time, inform the customer as soon as possible. Be accountable for your actions, both before you arrive on site and while on site. This means being"},{"term":"Avoid Distractions","def":"A distraction is anything that interrupts you from the task of resolving the ticket. Other than a genuinely critical incident taking priority, do not allow interruptions when you are working at a customer's site. Do not take calls from colleagues unless they are work-related and urgent."},{"term":"Deal Appropriately with Confidential and Private Materials","def":"You must also demonstrate respect for the customer's property, including any confidential or private data they might have stored on a PC or smartphone, or printed as a document: • Do not open data files, email applications, contact managers, web pages that are signed into"},{"term":"Professional Appearance","def":"There are many things that contribute to the art of presentation. Your appearance and attire, the words you use, and respecting cultural sensitivities are particularly important."},{"term":"Using Proper Language","def":"When you greet someone, you should be conscious of making a good first impression. When you arrive on site, make eye contact, greet the customer, and introduce yourself and your company. When you answer the phone, introduce yourself and your department and offer"},{"term":"Cultural Sensitivity","def":"Cultural sensitivity means being aware of customs and habits used by other people. It is easy to associate culture simply with national elements, such as the difference between the way Americans and Japanese people greet one another. However, within each nation there are many"},{"term":"Professional Communications","def":"You must listen carefully to what is being said to you; it will give you clues to the customer's technical level, enabling you to pace and adapt your replies accordingly."},{"term":"Active Listening","def":"Active listening is the skill of listening to an individual with your full attention without arguing with, commenting on, or misinterpreting what they have said. With active listening, you make a conscious effort to keep your attention focused on what the other person is saying, as"},{"term":"Clarifying and Questioning Techniques","def":"There will inevitably be a need to establish some technical facts with the customer. This means directing the customer to answer your questions. There are two broad types of questioning: • Open-ended—A question that invites the other person to compose a response. For example,"},{"term":"Difficult Situations","def":"A difficult situation occurs when either you or the customer becomes, or risks becoming, angry or upset. There are several techniques that you can use to defuse this type of tension."},{"term":"Maintain a Positive Attitude","def":"Understand that an angry customer is usually frustrated that things are not working properly, or feels let down. Perhaps a technician arrived late and the customer is already irritated. Or perhaps the customer has spent a large amount of money and is now anxious that it has been"},{"term":"Dealing with Difficult Customers","def":"It is never easy to talk to someone who is unreasonable, abusive, or shouting, but it is important to be able to deal with these situations professionally."},{"term":"Identify early signs that a customer is becoming angry","def":"Image by Wang Tom © 123RF.com. Do Not Post Experiences on Social Media Everyone has bad days when they feel the need to get some difficult situation off their chest."},{"term":"Do Not Post Experiences on Social Media","def":"Everyone has bad days when they feel the need to get some difficult situation off their chest."},{"term":"1.1 Explain common operating system (OS) types and their purposes","def":"Learning Outcomes As you study this lesson, please answer the following questions: • What are the four main operating systems found on computer systems?"}],"12":[{"term":"Windows Interfaces","def":"An OS is made up of kernel files and device drivers to interface with the hardware plus programs to provide a user interface and configuration tools. The earliest operating systems for PCs, such as Microsoft's Disk Operating System (DOS), used a command-line user interface"},{"term":"Windows 10 Desktop","def":"One of the main functions of an OS is to provide an interface (or shell) for the user to configure and operate the computer hardware and software. Windows has several interface components designed both for general use and for more technical configuration and troubleshooting."},{"term":"Windows 11 Desktop","def":"Windows 11 refreshes the desktop style by introducing a center-aligned taskbar, better spacing for touch control, and rounded corners. It also makes the multiple desktops feature more accessible. Multiple desktops allow the user to set up different workspaces, such as one"},{"term":"Windows Settings and Control Panel","def":"The Windows Settings app and Control Panel are the two main interfaces for administering Windows. Administering an OS means configuring options, setting up user accounts, and adding and removing devices and software. All Windows configuration data is ultimately held in"},{"term":"Windows Settings","def":"Windows Settings is a touch-enabled interface for managing Windows. The Settings app is the preferred administrative interface. Configuration option \"pages\" are divided between a few main headings."},{"term":"Control Panel","def":"Most of the standard Windows 10 and Windows 11 configuration settings can be located within Windows Settings, but not all of them. Some options are still configured via the legacy Control Panel interface."},{"term":"Tools Work Folders","def":"Accounts Settings A user account controls access to the computer. Each account can be assigned rights or privileges to make OS configuration changes. Accounts can also be assigned permissions on files, folders, and printers."},{"term":"Accounts Settings","def":"A user account controls access to the computer. Each account can be assigned rights or privileges to make OS configuration changes. Accounts can also be assigned permissions on files, folders, and printers."},{"term":"Windows Account Settings","def":"A Windows account can either be configured as a local-only account or linked to a Microsoft Account. A local account can be used to sign in on a single computer only. A Microsoft account gives access to Microsoft's cloud services and allows sign-in and syncs desktop settings and"},{"term":"User Accounts Control Panel Applet","def":"The User Accounts Applet in Control Panel is the legacy interface. Many of its functions are obfuscated or hidden and may not readily be used to add new accounts. It does provide options for adjusting the account name and changing the account privilege level between the"},{"term":"Privacy and Security Settings","def":"Privacy and Security Settings govern what usage data Windows is permitted to collect and what device functions are enabled and for which apps. The security settings allow a user to change antivirus, browser, and firewall settings. There are multiple settings toggles to determine what"},{"term":"Desktop Settings","def":"The desktop can be configured to use local settings and personalized to adjust its appearance."},{"term":"Time and Language Settings","def":"The Time and Language settings pages are used for two main purposes: • Set the correct date/time and time zone. Keeping the PC synchronized to an accurate time source is important for processes such as authentication and backup."},{"term":"The Time and Language settings pages are used for two main purposes:","def":"• Set the correct date/time and time zone. Keeping the PC synchronized to an accurate time source is important for processes such as authentication and backup."},{"term":"Personalization Settings","def":"The Personalization Settings allow you to select and customize themes, which set the appearance of the desktop environment. Personalization and theme settings include the desktop wallpaper, screen saver, color scheme, font, and properties for the Start menu and"},{"term":"File Explorer","def":"File management is a critical part of using a computer. As a computer support professional, you will often have to assist users with locating files. In Windows, file management is performed using the File Explorer app. File Explorer enables you to open, copy, move, rename, view, and"},{"term":"System Objects","def":"In Windows, access to data files is typically mediated by system objects. These are shown in the left-hand navigation pane in File Explorer. Some of the main system objects are: • User account- Contains personal data folders belonging to the signed-in account profile. For"},{"term":"Drives and Folders","def":"While the system objects represent logical storage areas, the actual data files are written to disk drives. Within the This PC object, drives are referred to by letters and optional labels. A \"drive\" can be a single physical disk or a partition on a disk, a shared network folder mapped to a drive"},{"term":"File Explorer Options","def":"File Explorer has configurable options for view settings and file search."},{"term":"Indexing Options","def":"You can configure file search behavior on the Search tab of the File Explorer Options dialog."},{"term":"System Settings","def":"The System Settings page in the Settings app presents options for configuring input and output devices, power, remote desktop, notifications, and clipboard (data copying). There is also an About page listing key hardware and OS version information."},{"term":"Windows Update","def":"Windows Update hosts critical updates and security patches plus optional software and hardware device driver updates."},{"term":"Windows Security","def":"The Windows Security page contains shortcuts to the management pages for the built-in Windows Defender virus/threat protection and firewall product."},{"term":"Activation","def":"Microsoft Product Activation is an anti-piracy technology that verifies that software products are legitimately purchased. You must activate Windows within a given number of days after installation. After the grace period, certain features will be disabled until the system is activated"},{"term":"Device Settings","def":"Most Windows-compatible hardware devices use Plug and Play. This means that Windows automatically detects when a new device is connected, locates drivers for it, and installs and configures it with minimal user input. In some cases, you may need to install the hardware"},{"term":"Display and Sound Settings","def":"The principal Display configuration settings are: • Scale—A large high-resolution screen can use quite small font sizes for the user interface."},{"term":"The principal Display configuration settings are:","def":"• Scale—A large high-resolution screen can use quite small font sizes for the user interface."},{"term":"Power Options","def":"Power management allows Windows to selectively reduce or turn off the power supplied to hardware components. The computer can be configured to enter a power-saving mode automatically; for example, if there is no use of an input device for a set period. This is"},{"term":"Apps, Programs, and Features","def":"Windows supports several types of installable software: The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 12: Configuring Windows • Windows Features are components of the operating system that can be enabled or disabled."},{"term":"Windows supports several types of installable software:","def":"The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 12: Configuring Windows • Windows Features are components of the operating system that can be enabled or disabled."},{"term":"Apps Settings","def":"In the Settings app, the Apps Settings group is used to view and remove installed apps and Windows Features. You can also configure which app should act as the default for opening, editing, and printing particular file types and manage which apps run at startup."},{"term":"Programs and Features","def":"The Programs and Features Control Panel applet is the legacy software management interface."},{"term":"Mail","def":"The Mail Applet in Control Panel is added if the Microsoft Outlook client email application is installed on the computer. It can be used to add email accounts/profiles and manage the .OST and .PST data files used to cache and archive messages. Detailed configuration of the email"},{"term":"Gaming","def":"The Gaming settings page is used to toggle game mode on and off. Game mode suspends Windows Update and dedicates resources to supporting the 3-D performance and frame rate of the active game app rather than other software or background services."},{"term":"Network Settings","def":"A Windows host can be configured with one or more types of network adapters. Adapter types include Ethernet, Wi-Fi, cellular radio, and virtual private network (VPN). Each adapter must be configured with Internet Protocol (IP) address information. Each network that an adapter is used"}],"13":[{"term":"Device Manager","def":"Device Manager (devmgmt.msc) lets you view and edit installed hardware properties, change configuration settings, update drivers, and remove or disable devices."},{"term":"Updating and Troubleshooting Devices","def":"Windows may identify a device's type and function but fail to find a driver, resulting in an \"Unknown Device\" or a \"generic\" type with a yellow exclamation mark in Device Manager. If the device never worked, ensure compatibility of the device and driver with the OS. Manufacturers"},{"term":"Removing, Uninstalling, and Disabling Devices","def":"For Plug and Play, hot-swappable devices, you can remove them without uninstalling. Before removing a storage device, close any active applications, click the Safely Remove Hardware icon in the taskbar, and select the option to stop or eject the device."},{"term":"Disk Management Console","def":"The disk subsystem stores all data generated by the operating system and applications. As the primary store of so much data, ensuring its reliability and performance is a critical management task."},{"term":"Disk Maintenance Tools","def":"Of all the computer's subsystems, disk drives, and the file system probably require the most attention to keep in optimum working order. File storage is subject to three main problems: • Fragmentation: On a hard disk, files ideally occupy contiguous clusters. Over time, they"},{"term":"Disk Defragmenter","def":"The Defragment and Optimize Drives tool enhances HDD and SSD performance: • HDDs: On an HDD, defragmenting reorganizes file data into contiguous clusters, reducing the time the controller needs to seek across the disk to read a file."},{"term":"Disk Clean-up","def":"The Disk Clean-up tool identifies files safe for deletion to free up space, including those in the Recycle Bin and temporary files. Running it in administrator mode with the Clean up system files option reclaims space from caches like Windows Update and Defender."},{"term":"Task Scheduler","def":"The Task Scheduler automates commands and scripts, with many Windows processes having predefined schedules. Tasks can be set to run once at a future date/time or on a recurring schedule. They can involve simple applications (including switches, if necessary), batch files, or"},{"term":"Local Users and Groups Console","def":"The Local Users and Groups console offers an advanced interface for managing user accounts, including creating, modifying, disabling, deleting, and resetting passwords."},{"term":"Certificate Manager","def":"A digital certificate verifies the identity of a user, computer, or service, with validity guaranteed by the issuing certification authority (CA). The certificate manager console displays installed certificates and allows for requesting and importing new ones."},{"term":"Key subfolders include:","def":"• Personal Folder: Stores certificates issued to the user account, used for network authentication, data encryption, and digital signatures."},{"term":"Group Policy Editor","def":"GUI tools like Settings and Control Panel modify user profiles and system configurations stored in the registry, but many registry settings aren't accessible through these tools. The Group Policy Editor offers a robust way to configure these Windows settings without directly editing"},{"term":"Registry Editor","def":"The Windows registry is a remotely accessible database for storing configuration information for the OS, devices, and applications. Use the Registry Editor (regedit.msc) to view or edit the registry."},{"term":"Registry Keys","def":"The Registry is organized into five root keys containing computer and user databases: The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 13: Managing Windows • HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE (HKLM): Manages system-wide settings."},{"term":"Editing the Registry","def":"Root keys contain subkeys and data items called value entries, similar to folders and files. A value entry includes the name, data type (such as string or binary), and the value itself. Use the Find tool to search for keys or values."},{"term":"Custom Microsoft Management Consoles","def":"A Microsoft Management Console (MMC) is a container for snap-ins like Device Manager, Disk Management, Group Policy Editor, and Certificate Manager. Use the mmc command to customize and create a console with your chosen snap-ins. Save the console as an MSC file in the"},{"term":"Command Prompt","def":"You can execute any command from the Run dialog, but for a series of commands or viewing output, use the command shell. The command prompt processes legacy commands from early Windows versions."},{"term":"Administrative Command Prompt","def":"Some commands require elevated privileges. If a command needs elevation, you'll see \"The requested operation requires elevation.\" To run as administrator, right-click the Command Prompt shortcut, select \"Run as administrator,\" and confirm the UAC prompt. Alternatively,"},{"term":"Command Syntax","def":"Enter commands at the prompt (>) using the command name, switches, and arguments with proper syntax. Press ENTER to execute the command. Required and optional arguments and switches are listed in the command syntax. Switches are usually preceded by a forward slash. If"},{"term":"Getting Help","def":"The command prompt includes a basic help system. Type help and then press ENTER at the command prompt to see available commands or type help Command for syntax and switches used for the command. Use the /? switch for command-specific help, e.g., netstat /? displays"},{"term":"Navigation Commands","def":"The string before the > in the command prompt indicates the current working directory."},{"term":"Listing Files and Directories","def":"Use the dir command to list files and subdirectories in the current directory or a specified path."},{"term":"Changing the Current Directory","def":"Use the cd command to change the working directory. Enter the full path to switch directories, such as cd C:\\Users\\David ."},{"term":"Here are some shortcuts:","def":"• To move from C:\\Users\\David to C:\\Users\\David\\Documents, type: cd Documents • To move up to the parent directory from C:\\Users\\David\\Documents, type: cd .."},{"term":"Changing the Current Drive","def":"The active, or in focus, drive is managed separately from the directory. To switch drives, enter the drive letter followed by a colon and press ENTER. For example, typing D: switches to the D drive, and the prompt will update to D:\\ , indicating that D is now the default drive."},{"term":"File Management Commands","def":"The move command and copy command allow you to transfer files within a single directory."},{"term":"Copying Directory Structures","def":"The robocopy command, or \"robust copy,\" is another file copy utility. Microsoft recommends using robocopy over xcopy for better handling of long file names and NTFS attributes."},{"term":"The command includes various switches for enhanced functionality:","def":"• /xf: Excludes files that match the specified names or paths."},{"term":"Creating a Directory","def":"To create a directory, use the md or mkdir command. For example, type md Data to create a directory named Data in the current directory. To create a directory named Docs within a directory called Data on the A drive, while the current path is C:, type md A:\\Data\\Docs ."},{"term":"Removing a Directory","def":"To delete an empty directory, use rd Directory or rmdir Directory. If the directory contains files or subdirectories, use the /s switch to remove them. The /q switch can be used to suppress confirmation messages for quiet mode."},{"term":"Disk Management Commands","def":"The Disk Management snap-in is easy to use, but there are some circumstances where you may need to manage volumes at a command prompt."},{"term":"The diskpart Command","def":"The diskpart command serves as the command-line interface for the Disk Management tool."},{"term":"The format Command","def":"The format command creates a new file system on a drive, deleting all existing data. The basic command is format drive navigation input X: /fs:SYS, where X is the drive letter and SYS is the file system type (e.g., NTFS, FAT32, EXFAT). By default, it scans for bad sectors, which can be"},{"term":"The chkdsk Command","def":"The chkdsk command scans the file system and disk sectors for faults and attempts to repair detected problems. A version called autochk runs automatically if file system errors are detected at boot."},{"term":"There are three ways to use chkdsk :","def":"• chkdsk X : (where X is the drive letter but no switch is used) runs in read-only mode, reporting any errors that need repair."}],"14":[{"term":"Troubleshoot IP Configuration","def":"Windows can report several types of error states for a local network adapter. If the connection is reported as unplugged or disconnected, you need to check the cable or wireless network configuration. Two other states are reported if the link is available, but IP is not correctly"},{"term":"Network Reset","def":"If there are persistent network problems with either a client or a server, one \"stock\" response is to try restarting the computer hardware. You can also try restarting just the application service."},{"term":"Troubleshoot Local Network Connectivity","def":"If the link and IP configuration both seem to be correct, the problem may not lie with the local machine but somewhere in the overall network topology. You can test connections to servers such as file shares, printers, or email by trying to use them. One drawback of this method is that"},{"term":"Troubleshoot Remote Network Connectivity","def":"When a packet is forwarded to a remote network, each router in the path to the network counts as one hop. The path taken by a packet can be used to diagnose routing issues. The tracert command (pronounced trace route) line utility is used to trace the path a packet of information"},{"term":"Troubleshoot Name Resolution","def":"If you cannot identify a problem with basic connectivity, you should start to suspect a problem at a higher layer of processing. There are three main additional \"layers\" where network services fail: • Security- A firewall or other security software or hardware might be blocking the connection"},{"term":"Troubleshoot Network Ports","def":"The netstat command can be used to investigate open ports and connections on the local host."},{"term":"Remote Desktop Tools","def":"With remote desktop, the target PC runs a graphical terminal server to accept connections from clients. This allows a user to work at the desktop of a different computer over the network."},{"term":"Remote Desktop Protocol","def":"Windows uses the Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP) to implement terminal server and client functionality. To connect to a server via Remote Desktop, open the Remote Desktop Connection shortcut or run mstsc.exe . Enter the server's IP address or fully qualified domain name"},{"term":"Virtual Network Computing","def":"There are alternatives to using RDP for remote access. For example, in macOS, you can use the Screen Sharing feature for remote desktop functionality. Screen Sharing is based on the Virtual Network Computing (VNC) protocol. You can use any VNC client to connect to a Screen Sharing"},{"term":"RDP Server and Security Settings","def":"A Remote Desktop server is not enabled by default. To change remote access settings, open the Remote Desktop page in the Settings app."},{"term":"Microsoft Remote Assistance","def":"Remote Assistance allows a user to ask for help from a technician or co-worker via an invitation file protected by a passcode. The helper can open the file to connect over RDP and join the session with the user. There is a chat feature, and the helper can request control of the desktop."},{"term":"Secure Shell","def":"Secure shell is also a remote access protocol, but it connects to a command interpreter rather than a desktop window manager. SSH uses TCP port 22 (by default). SSH uses encryption to protect each session. There are numerous commercial and open-source SSH products available"},{"term":"Remote Monitoring and Desktop Management Tools","def":"Network visibility refers to the challenge of ensuring that every host communicating on the network is authorized to be there and is running in a secure configuration. It is impractical for a technician to regularly locate and visit each device, so visibility depends on remote monitoring"},{"term":"Simple Protocol for Independent Computing Environments (SPICE)","def":"The Simple Protocol for Independent Computing Environments provides a remote display system to monitor and interact with virtual machine environments from across the Internet."},{"term":"Other Remote Access Tools","def":"Enterprise monitoring suites are designed for environments with large numbers of desktops, and the cost can be prohibitive when managing just a few machines. Other protocols and software tools are available for accepting incoming connections to non-Windows devices and"},{"term":"Screen-sharing Software","def":"There are many third-party alternatives to the sort of screen sharing and remote-control functionality implemented by MSRA/Quick Assist. Examples include TeamViewer and LogMeIn."},{"term":"Video-conferencing Software","def":"Most video conferencing or web-conferencing software, such as Microsoft Teams or Zoom, includes a screen-share client, and some also allow participants to be granted control of the share. The share can be configured as a single window or the whole desktop. The share"},{"term":"File Transfer Software","def":"Setting up a network file share can be relatively complex. You need to select a file-sharing protocol that all the connecting hosts can use and that allows configuring permissions on the share and provisioning user accounts that both the server and client recognize. Consequently,"},{"term":"OS vendors have developed other types of file transfer software:","def":"• AirDrop—Supported by Apple iOS and macOS, this uses Bluetooth to establish a Wi-Fi Direct connection between the devices for the duration of the file transfer. The connection is secured by the Bluetooth pairing mechanism and Wi-Fi encryption."},{"term":"Virtual Private Networks","def":"Where remote desktop or SSH establishes a connection to a single host over the network, a virtual private network (VPN) establishes a tunneled link that joins your local computer to a remote network. The VPN could be used as an additional layer of security. For example, you"},{"term":"System Information","def":"The System Information tool (msinfo32.exe) generates a comprehensive report on the system's hardware and software components. It provides an inventory of system resources, firmware and OS versions, driver file locations, environment variables, and network status."},{"term":"Event Viewer","def":"When Windows detects a problem, it generates an error message to aid troubleshooting. You can look up these messages using the Microsoft Knowledge Base (support.microsoft.com) or third-party support sites."},{"term":"Default Log Files","def":"The Windows Logs folder contains four main log files: • System Log: Records events affecting the core OS, such as service load failures, hardware conflicts, driver load failures, and network issues."},{"term":"The Windows Logs folder contains four main log files:","def":"• System Log: Records events affecting the core OS, such as service load failures, hardware conflicts, driver load failures, and network issues."},{"term":"Event Sources and Severity Levels","def":"Each event is generated by a source application and assigned an ID and severity level: The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 14: Supporting Windows • Critical: Highest priority issues, often indicating a halted or unresponsive process."},{"term":"Task Manager Process Monitoring","def":"Task Manager monitors your PC's key resources. Open it by pressing CTRL+SHIFT+ESC, right- clicking the taskbar or Start, or pressing CTRL+ALT+DEL and selecting Task Manager."},{"term":"Task Manager Performance Monitoring","def":"The Performance tab offers detailed information about the CPU, memory, disk, network, and graphics processing unit (GPU) subsystems. The App History tab displays usage information for Windows Store apps."},{"term":"Disk Monitoring","def":"The Disk pages display type, capacity, and statistics for active time, response time, and read/ write speeds."},{"term":"Network Monitoring","def":"The Ethernet or Wi-Fi tab displays send and receive throughput for the active network adapter, along with the IP address and MAC address. For active wireless adapters, it also shows the SSID, connection type (802.11 standard), and signal strength."},{"term":"CPU and GPU Monitoring","def":"The CPU page shows the number of cores and logical processors (HyperThreading), whether the system is multisocket, and whether virtualization is enabled. The statistics show overall utilization, system uptime, and a count of the number of processes, threads, and handles."},{"term":"Memory Monitoring","def":"The Memory page reports which slots have modules installed and the speed. The usage statistics are broken down as follows: • In use refers to system (RAM) usage only."},{"term":"Disk and Network Monitoring","def":"Disk Monitoring The Disk pages report the type and capacity plus statistics for active time, response time, and read/write speeds."},{"term":"Task Manager User Monitoring","def":"The Users tab in Task Manager lets you: • View all logged-in users."},{"term":"The Users tab in Task Manager lets you:","def":"• View all logged-in users. • Send messages to users."},{"term":"Startup Processes and Services Console","def":"The Startup tab allows you to disable programs set to run at startup from the Startup folder or registry. Right-click the headers to view the startup type and each item's impact on boot times."}],"15":[{"term":"Logical Security Controls","def":"A security control is a safeguard or prevention method to avoid, counteract, or minimize risks relating to personal or company property. For example, a firewall is a type of security control because it controls network communications by allowing only traffic that has specifically been"},{"term":"Confidentiality, Integrity, and Availability:","def":"• Confidentiality ensures that sensitive data is only accessible by authorized users."},{"term":"Access Control Lists","def":"A permission is a security setting that determines the level of access an account has to a particular resource. A permission is usually implemented as an access control list attached to each resource. Within an ACL, each access control entry (ACE) identifies a subject and the"},{"term":"Implicit Deny","def":"ACL security is typically founded on the principle of implicit deny. implicit deny means that unless there is a rule specifying that access should be granted, any request for access is denied. This principle can be seen clearly in firewall policies. A firewall filters access requests"},{"term":"Principle of Least Privilege","def":"A complementary principle to implicit deny is that of least privilege. This means that a user should be granted the minimum possible access necessary to perform the job. This can be complex to apply in practice, however. Designing a permissions system that respects the"},{"term":"Information Security","def":"Information security is assured by developing security policies and controls. Making a system more secure is also referred to as hardening it. Different security policies should cover every The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 15: Securing Windows"},{"term":"Relationship between vulnerability, threat, and risk","def":"To assist with workstation and network security assessments, you need to understand the types of threats that an organization is exposed to and how vulnerabilities can be exploited to launch attacks."},{"term":"Hashing and Encryption Concepts","def":"Many logical security controls depend to some extent on the use of encryption technologies."},{"term":"Symmetric Encryption","def":"A symmetric encryption cipher uses a single secret key to both encrypt and decrypt data. The secret key is so-called because it must be kept secret. If the key is lost or stolen, the security is breached. Consequently, the main problem with symmetric encryption is secure distribution"},{"term":"Asymmetric Encryption","def":"An asymmetric encryption cipher uses a key pair. A key pair is a private key and a public key that are mathematically linked. For any given message, either key can perform either the encrypt or decrypt operation but not both. Only the paired key can reverse the operation. For"},{"term":"Cryptographic Hashes","def":"A hash is a short representation of data. A hash function takes any amount of data as input and produces a fixed-length value as output. A cryptographic hash performs this process as a one- way function that makes it impossible to recover the original value from the hash. Cryptographic"},{"term":"Digital Signatures and Key Exchange","def":"Cryptographic hashes and encryption ciphers have different roles in achieving the information security goals of confidentiality, integrity, and availability. Often two or more of these three different types are used together in the same product or technology."},{"term":"Digital Signatures","def":"A digital signature proves that a message or digital certificate has not been altered or spoofed."},{"term":"Key Exchange","def":"Key exchange allows two hosts to know the same symmetric encryption key without any other host finding out what it is. A symmetric cipher is much faster than an asymmetric one, so it is often used to protect the actual data exchange in a session. Asymmetric encryption only"},{"term":"User and Group Accounts","def":"A user account is the principal means of controlling access to computer and network resources and assigning rights or privileges. In Windows, a user can be set up with a local account or a Microsoft account: • A local account is defined on that computer only. For example, PC1\\David is the"},{"term":"Security Groups","def":"A security group is a collection of user accounts. Security groups are used when assigning permissions and rights, as it is more efficient to assign permissions to a group than to assign them individually to each user. You can set up a number of custom groups with least privilege"},{"term":"Local Users and Groups","def":"The Local Users and Groups management console provides an interface for managing both user and group accounts. Use the shortcut menus and object Properties dialogs to create, disable, and delete accounts, change account properties, reset user passwords, create custom"},{"term":"User Account Control","def":"Just-in-Time (JIT) access is a security practice in which users are granted access to resources only when needed and for only as long as it takes to complete the needed task."},{"term":"Authentication Methods","def":"In an access control system, accounts are configured with permissions to access resources and (for privileged accounts) rights to change the system configuration. To access an account, the user must authenticate by supplying the correct credentials, proving that they are the valid"},{"term":"Multifactor Authentication","def":"Using a single factor makes authentication less reliable. A password could be shared, a device token could be stolen, or a facial recognition system could be spoofed using a photograph."},{"term":"An invalid multifactor authentication example would be:","def":"• User submits a username/password (knowledge) and a PIN (knowledge) to sign in."},{"term":"2-Step Verification","def":"2-step verification is a means of using a soft token to check that a sign-in request is authentic."},{"term":"It works on the following lines:","def":"1. The user registers a trusted contact method with the app. This could be an email account or phone number, for instance."},{"term":"One-Time Passwords","def":"The soft tokens may also be referred to as a one-time password (OTP). The OTP is only valid for a single login session and a new unique passcode is generated for each login attempt. OTPs add an additional level of security because even if the user's password is compromised, the"},{"term":"There are a few types of one-time passwords including:","def":"The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 15: Securing Windows • Time-based OTP (TOTP) - This OTP is only valid for a set amount of time, such as 1 minute, before it expires. If the OTP is not entered within the specified timeframe, a new OTP will"},{"term":"Hard Token Authentication","def":"A hard token works in the same sort of way as an authenticator app but is implemented as firmware in a smart card or USB thumb drive rather than running on a smartphone. The hard token is first registered with the service or network. When the user needs to authenticate,"},{"term":"Windows authentication involves a complex architecture of","def":"components (docs.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/security/windows-authentication/ credentials-processes-in-windows-authentication), but the following three scenarios are typical: • Windows local sign-in - The Local Security Authority (LSA) compares the submitted"},{"term":"Username and Password","def":"A username and password credential is configured by creating the user account and choosing a password. The user can change the password by pressing CTRL+ALT+DELETE or using account settings. An administrator can also reset the password using Local Users and Groups."},{"term":"Fingerprint Windows Hello PIN Security Key Password Picture Password","def":"Note: A PIN must be configured to set up Windows Hello. The PIN acts as a backup mechanism in case other methods become unavailable. For example, a camera may fail to work and make facial recognition impossible, or a hardware token might be lost or temporarily unavailable."},{"term":"Single Sign-On","def":"Single sign-on means that a user authenticates once to a device or network to gain access to multiple applications or services. The Kerberos authentication and authorization model for Active Directory domain networks implements SSO. A user who has authenticated with"},{"term":"Security Assertions Markup Language (SAML)","def":"Security Assertions Markup Language (SAML) is a special type of SSO. With SAML, an Identity Provider (IdP) is used to pass user credentials to a service provider (SP). This process works as below: • The user attempts to login to the service provider, such as Google Workspace."},{"term":"Windows Domains and Active Directory","def":"A local account is only recognized by the local machine and cannot be used to access other computers. For example, if the user David needs access to multiple computers in a workgroup environment, a separate local account must be configured on each computer ( PC1\\David,"},{"term":"Domain Controllers","def":"To create a domain, you need at least one Windows Server computer configured as a domain controller (DC). A DC stores a database of network information called active directory. This database stores user, group, and computer objects."},{"term":"Member Servers","def":"A member server is any server-based system that has been joined to the domain but does not maintain a copy of the Active Directory database. A member server provides file and print and application server services, such as Exchange for email or SQL Server for database or line-of-"},{"term":"Organizational Units","def":"An organizational unit is a way of dividing a domain up into different administrative realms."}],"16":[{"term":"Windows Home","def":"Designed for domestic consumers and SOHO use; it cannot join a Windows domain network."},{"term":"Windows Pro","def":"Designed for small and medium-sized businesses and supports features such as Group Policy, BitLocker, and Remote Desktop host."},{"term":"Windows Enterprise","def":"Tailored for large organizations with advanced security, deployment, and management features and is available through volume licensing."},{"term":"N versions","def":"Windows editions created to comply with EU regulations by excluding certain pre-installed multimedia applications."},{"term":"OEM license","def":"A Windows Home license pre-installed on a PC and valid only for that device."},{"term":"Retail license","def":"A Windows Home license that can be transferred between computers but can be active on only one device at a time."},{"term":"Workgroup","def":"A network where devices share resources but are managed independently."},{"term":"Domain","def":"A network where devices connect to a centralized Domain Controller for management and security policies."},{"term":"Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc)","def":"A tool for configuring and enforcing OS and application settings across devices."},{"term":"BitLocker","def":"A Windows feature that provides disk encryption to protect data."},{"term":"Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP)","def":"A remote connection protocol supported as a host feature by Windows editions other than Home."},{"term":"In-place upgrade","def":"An upgrade that preserves applications, settings, and data when they are compatible."},{"term":"Clean install","def":"An installation path that does not preserve the existing OS installation in place."},{"term":"Feature update","def":"A Windows update that introduces changes to features and functionality."},{"term":"32-bit Windows","def":"A Windows architecture with a 4 GB RAM limit for 32-bit editions."},{"term":"64-bit Windows","def":"A Windows architecture that can support more RAM than 32-bit editions, with limits varying by license."},{"term":"Windows 11","def":"A Windows client OS available in Home, Pro, and Enterprise editions in the guide."},{"term":"Pro for Workstations","def":"A Windows edition designed for advanced users and high-performance hardware."},{"term":"Enterprise volume licensing","def":"The licensing model through which Windows Enterprise and Education are available."},{"term":"TPM","def":"A hardware security requirement referenced for Windows installation and upgrade considerations."},{"term":"1.3 Compare and contrast basic features of Microsoft Windows editions","def":"Learning Outcomes As you study this lesson, answer the following questions: • What are the key differences between Windows Home and Windows Pro editions, and which edition supports domain network integration?"},{"term":"Windows Versions","def":"Windows 10 and Windows 11, available in Home, Pro, and Enterprise editions, represent the currently supported versions of the Windows client OS. These editions target different market sectors, such as home users and corporate environments. Additionally, both versions have"},{"term":"32-bit Versus 64-bit","def":"Each version and edition of Windows 10 was initially available in both 32-bit and 64-bit formats."},{"term":"Desktop Styles","def":"The Windows user interface (UI) revolves around the desktop, Start menu, taskbar, and notification area. While these elements remain consistent, Windows versions, editions (such as Home, Pro, Pro for Workstations, and Enterprise), and feature updates often bring changes. For"},{"term":"Windows Home Edition","def":"Designed for domestic consumers and small office/home office (SOHO) use, Windows Home lacks unique features and has fewer capabilities than other editions, notably unable to join a Windows domain network."},{"term":"Windows Home Licensing","def":"Windows Home offers two licensing models: 1. Original Equipment Manufacturer (OEM) License: This license comes pre-installed on a PC or laptop and is valid only for that device. The computer vendor provides support. Most new devices can upgrade to Windows 11."},{"term":"Windows Home offers two licensing models:","def":"1. Original Equipment Manufacturer (OEM) License: This license comes pre-installed on a PC or laptop and is valid only for that device. The computer vendor provides support. Most new devices can upgrade to Windows 11."},{"term":"Windows Home System Limitations","def":"Windows Home supports multicore processing (up to 64 cores) and HyperThreading but does not support multiple CPUs. The 64-bit edition is limited to 128 GB of RAM."},{"term":"Windows Editions for Work and Education","def":"• Windows Pro: Designed for small and medium-sized businesses, available through OEM or retail licensing. It offers features like Group Policy, BitLocker, and Remote Desktop host for enhanced network administration. The Pro for Workstations edition supports more advanced"},{"term":"Domain Network Support","def":"The primary distinction between Pro, Enterprise, and Education editions versus Windows Home is domain network support. These editions can join a domain, allowing centralized management of computers, user accounts, and policies via a Domain Controller (DC) server. This is essential"},{"term":"Workgroup vs. Domain Networks","def":"• Workgroup: Devices share files and resources but are managed independently."},{"term":"Notable Features of Windows Pro, Enterprise, and Education Editions","def":"• Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc): Configures and enforces OS and application settings across devices, ensuring consistent configurations. Not available in Windows Home."},{"term":"Windows Pro and Enterprise Editions:","def":"• Windows Pro: Available as an OEM, retail/full packaged product (FPP), or through volume licensing, which offers discounts for bulk purchases and allows custom installation images for quick deployment. Windows Pro for Workstations includes all Pro features but supports"},{"term":"Windows Upgrade Paths and Feature Updates","def":"An in-place upgrade allows you to upgrade to a new Windows version while preserving applications, settings, and data, provided they are compatible."},{"term":"Edition Considerations:","def":"• You can upgrade within the same or higher edition (e.g., Windows 10 Home to Windows 11 Home or Pro), but you cannot upgrade from Home to Enterprise directly."}],"17":[{"term":"Shells, Terminals, and Consoles","def":"So far in this course, you worked mostly with the Microsoft Windows operating system. A CompTIA A+ technician should be capable of supporting diverse OS environments. The various operating systems you might encounter use different interfaces and command syntax, but the"},{"term":"Bootloaders","def":"Before the operating system loads, a bootloader initializes the system. It is responsible for loading the kernel into memory and starting the operating system. Bootloaders support multi-boot configurations, allowing multiple operating systems on the same device. Common"},{"term":"Shells and Terminals","def":"A shell provides a command-line environment for users to interact with the OS and applications."},{"term":"Desktop Environments","def":"Linux distros intended for client PCs usually start with a graphical desktop environment. This environment is powered by Xorg, an open-source implementation of the X Window System."},{"term":"Console Switching","def":"In systems with a graphical environment, the X server runs on a virtual tty console, typically tty1. Users can switch between consoles using CTRL+ALT+Fx keys, with each console supporting different login prompts and shells."},{"term":"Linux commands follow a standard format:","def":"• Command: The first \"word\" is the command, which can be a full or relative path to an executable, or simply the name of an executable located in a directory specified by the PATH environment variable. The command is recognized up to the first space character."},{"term":"Case Sensitivity","def":"In Linux, commands, parameters, and file and directory names are all case-sensitive. For instance, ls -l file.data and ls -L File.data will yield different results. Typing a command name with incorrect capitalization will result in an error message."},{"term":"Help System","def":"To view a Linux command's function and syntax, use the --help option. Since the help output can be lengthy, it's common to pipe it to the more command for viewing one page at a time, e.g.,ls --help | more . Alternatively, use the man command to access detailed manual pages for"},{"term":"Text Editors","def":"Most Linux files are in plain text format and can be easily edited. There are many text editors available. For those familiar with Windows, the nano editor is a simple option. To open or create a file, use nano filepath , or nano -l filepath to display line numbers. Navigate with the cursor"},{"term":"Navigation Commands","def":"In Linux, everything is represented as a file within a unified file system. The first fixed disk is typically /dev/sda , while additional devices, like a USB drive, appear as /dev/sdb ."},{"term":"Linux","def":"Key commands for navigating the Linux file system include pwd,cd,ls, and cat."},{"term":"Search Commands","def":"Linux supports very fast and accurate file system informational search commands."},{"term":"Metacharacters and Escaping","def":"In Linux, escaping means using a special character (usually a backslash \\) to indicate that the following character should be treated as a literal rather than interpreted in its usual, special way. This is necessary when dealing with metacharacters, which have specific meanings in the"},{"term":"There are three ways to escape strings:","def":"• Backslash (\\): Escapes the next character only. For example,\\* treats * as a literal, and \\\\ treats \\ as a literal."},{"term":"Filesystem Management","def":"Filesystem management involves organizing, maintaining, and accessing data stored on disk drives. It includes tasks such as mounting filesystems, checking and repairing them, and configuring their behavior."},{"term":"Key filesystem management tools include:","def":"• Mounting: To access a filesystem, it must be mounted, which means attaching it to a directory in the existing filesystem hierarchy. The mount command is used for this purpose."},{"term":"File Management Commands","def":"File management commands are used to move, copy, and delete data."},{"term":"User Account Management","def":"In Linux, the root account, or superuser, has full administrative privileges and can perform any action on the system. It should be used only when absolutely necessary. During setup, most Linux distributions prompt you to create a regular user account for daily tasks. Instead of"},{"term":"User Management Commands","def":"User settings are stored in the /etc/passwd file, while group settings are in the /etc/group file."},{"term":"Group Management Commands","def":"Each user account can be assigned to groups to manage file permissions. Use the groupadd, groupmod, and groupdel commands to manage group memberships. A user can belong to multiple groups but has only one effective group ID at a time, listed in /etc/passwd. The"},{"term":"File Permissions Commands","def":"Each file in Linux has a set of permissions that determine user access levels. The permissions system includes three rights: • Read (r): Allows viewing the contents of a file or directory."},{"term":"For example, using ls -l for a long directory listing:","def":"• drwxr-xr-x 2 bobby admins Desktop : The owner (bobby) has full (rwx) permissions, while the group (admins) and others have read and execute (r-x) permissions."},{"term":"For example, numeric permission 0754 translates to:","def":"The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 17: Supporting Other OS • 7: Owner has all rights (4+2+1) • 5: Group has read and execute (4+0+1) • 4: Others have read (4+0+0) The leading zero indicates octal format but can often be omitted. Another common combination"},{"term":"Modifying permissions using the chmod command","def":"chown Command The chown command allows the superuser or sudoers to change the owner of a file or directory. Regular users cannot use chown , even if they own the file, but they can change the group using the chgrp command."},{"term":"Examples:","def":"1. Change the owner of a file: chown username file.txt 2. Change both the owner and group: chown username:groupname file.txt 3. Change ownership recursively for a directory and its contents: chown -R username:groupname /path/to/directory 4. Change only the group (using :): chown :groupname file.txt"},{"term":"2. Change both the owner and group: chown username:groupname file.txt","def":"3. Change ownership recursively for a directory and its contents: chown -R username:groupname /path/to/directory 4. Change only the group (using :): chown :groupname file.txt The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 17: Supporting Other OS"},{"term":"Distributions and Repositories","def":"A distribution includes precompiled software packages deemed appropriate by the vendor or sponsor. These packages, along with updates, are posted to software repositories. Vendors often maintain multiple repositories, such as stable, beta, and unsupported packages."},{"term":"Listing package manager sources in Ubuntu Linux","def":"Package managers must be configured with the web addresses of desired repositories, typically done automatically during setup. They handle installing, uninstalling, and updating software. Package integrity is verified using cryptographic hashes or signatures, such as MD5,"},{"term":"Process Monitoring Commands","def":"Every process in Linux is assigned a unique process ID (PID) upon starting, allowing the system and users to identify it. This PID is a non-negative integer that increments with each new process. PID 1 is reserved for the initial daemon, the first process to start, serving as the parent"},{"term":"Listing the state of running processes","def":"Various keystrokes execute process management actions, including: • ENTER: Refresh the status of all processes."},{"term":"Various keystrokes execute process management actions, including:","def":"• ENTER: Refresh the status of all processes. • SHIFT+N: Sort processes in decreasing PID order."},{"term":"Network Management Commands","def":"In Linux, Ethernet interfaces were traditionally named eth0, eth1, etc., but modern distributions often use names like enp0s3 or ens33, based on the system's hardware topology."},{"term":"Example usage:","def":"ip addr show # Display all network interfaces and their IP addresses ip link set enp0s3 up # Bring up the network interface enp0s3 ip route show # Display the routing table /etc/hosts The/etc/hosts file is a simple text file that maps hostnames to IP addresses. It is used for local"},{"term":"Example entry in /etc/hosts:","def":"127.0.0.1 localhost 192.168.1.10 myserver.local /etc/resolv.conf The /etc/resolv.conf file contains information that defines how DNS (Domain Name System) resolution is handled. It specifies the DNS servers that the system should query to resolve domain names into IP addresses."},{"term":"127.0.0.1 localhost","def":"192.168.1.10 myserver.local /etc/resolv.conf The /etc/resolv.conf file contains information that defines how DNS (Domain Name System) resolution is handled. It specifies the DNS servers that the system should query to resolve domain names into IP addresses."}],"18":[{"term":"Vulnerabilities","def":"A vulnerability is some fault or weakness in a system that could be exploited by a threat actor. Vulnerabilities can arise due to a very wide range of causes. Some of these causes include improperly configured or installed hardware or software, delays in applying and"},{"term":"Non-compliant Systems","def":"A configuration baseline is a set of recommendations for deploying a computer in a hardened configuration to minimize the risk that there could be vulnerabilities. There are baselines for different operating systems and different server and client roles. For example, a web server"},{"term":"Unprotected Systems","def":"A baseline will recommend specific technical security controls to ensure a secure configuration."},{"term":"Software and Zero-day Vulnerabilities","def":"A software vulnerability is a fault in design or in code that can cause an application security system to be circumvented or that will cause the application to crash. The most serious vulnerabilities allow the attacker to execute arbitrary code on the system, which could allow"},{"term":"Unpatched and End of Life Operating Systems","def":"While zero-day exploits can be extremely destructive, they are relatively rare events. A greater threat is the large number of unpatched or legacy systems in use."},{"term":"Bring Your Own Device Vulnerabilities","def":"Bring your own device is a provisioning model that allows employees to use personal mobile devices to access corporate systems and data. In this scenario, it is very difficult for the security team to identify secure configuration baselines for each type of device and mobile"},{"term":"Social Engineering","def":"Threat actors can use a diverse range of techniques to compromise a security system. A prerequisite of many types of attacks is to obtain information about the network and its security controls. Social engineering—or hacking the human—refers to techniques that persuade or"},{"term":"Impersonation","def":"Impersonation means that the social engineer develops a pretext scenario to allow himself or herself an opportunity to interact with an employee. A classic impersonation pretext is for the threat actor to phone into a department pretending to be calling from IT support, claim"},{"term":"Dumpster Diving","def":"To make a pretext seem genuine, the threat actor must obtain privileged information about the organization or an individual. For example, an impersonation pretext is much more effective if the attacker knows the user's name. As most companies are set up toward customer"},{"term":"Shoulder Surfing","def":"A shoulder surfing attack means that the threat actor learns a password or PIN (or other secure information) by watching the user type it. Despite the name, the attacker may not have to be in proximity to the target—they could use high-powered binoculars or CCTV to directly observe"},{"term":"Tailgating and Piggybacking","def":"Tailgaiting is a means of entering a secure area without authorization by following closely behind the person who has been allowed to open the door or checkpoint. Piggybacking is a similar situation but means that the attacker enters a secure area with an employee's"},{"term":"Phishing and Evil Twins","def":"Phishing uses social engineering techniques to make spoofed electronic communications seem authentic to the victim. A phishing message might try to convince the user to perform some action, such as installing malware disguised as an antivirus program or allowing a threat"},{"term":"Some phishing variants are referred to by specific names:","def":"• Spear-phishing occurs when the attacker has some information that makes the target more likely to be fooled by the attack. The threat actor might know the name of a document that the target is editing, for instance, and send a malicious copy, or the phishing email might"},{"term":"Threat Types","def":"Historically, cybersecurity techniques were highly dependent on the identification of \"static\" known threats, such as computer viruses. This type of threat leaves a programming code signature in the file that it infects that is relatively straightforward to identify with automated"},{"term":"External versus Internal Threats","def":"An external threat actor is one who has no account or authorized access to the target system."},{"term":"Footprinting Threats","def":"Footprinting is an information-gathering threat in which the attacker attempts to learn about the configuration of the network and security systems. A threat actor will perform reconnaissance and research about the target, gathering publicly available information, scanning network ports"},{"term":"Spoofing Threats","def":"A spoofing threat is any type of attack where the threat actor can masquerade as a trusted user or computer. Spoofing can mean cloning a valid MAC or IP address, using a false digital certificate, creating an email message that imitates a legitimate one, or performing social"},{"term":"On-path Attacks","def":"An is a specific type of spoofing where the threat actor can covertly intercept traffic between two hosts or networks. This allows the threat actor to read and possibly modify the packets. An on-path attack is often designed to try to recover password hashes. An evil twin is one example"},{"term":"Denial of Service Attack","def":"A denial of service attack attack causes a service at a given host to fail or to become unavailable to legitimate users. Typically, a DoS attack tries to overload a service by bombarding it with spoofed requests. It is also possible for DoS attacks to exploit design failures"},{"term":"Distributed DoS Attacks and Botnets","def":"Network-based DoS attacks are normally accomplished by flooding the server with bogus requests. They rely on the attacker having access to greater bandwidth than the target or on the target being required to devote more resources to each connection than the attacker. This"},{"term":"Password Attacks","def":"On-path and malware attacks can be difficult to perpetrate. Many network intrusions occur because a threat actor simply obtains credentials to access the network. Also, when threat actors gain some sort of access via an on-path or malware attack, they are likely to attempt to"},{"term":"Hashcat password cracking utility","def":"Cross-site Scripting Attacks Many network services are now deployed as web applications. The HTTP/HTTPS web protocol is based on servers responding to client requests. A developer can extend the basic protocol with software code and information stored in databases to implement a dynamic web app,"},{"term":"Cross-site Scripting Attacks","def":"Many network services are now deployed as web applications. The HTTP/HTTPS web protocol is based on servers responding to client requests. A developer can extend the basic protocol with software code and information stored in databases to implement a dynamic web app,"},{"term":"SQL Injection Attacks","def":"A web application is likely to use Structured Query Language (SQL) to read and write information from a database. SQL statements perform operations such as selecting data (SELECT), inserting data (INSERT), deleting data (DELETE), and updating data (UPDATE). In"},{"term":"Wi-Fi Protected Access","def":"Wireless LANs require careful configuration to make the connection and transmissions over the link secure. The main problem with wireless is that because it is unguided, there is no way to prevent anything within range from listening to the signals. If the wireless traffic is unencrypted,"},{"term":"Temporal Key Integrity Protocol","def":"The first version of Wi-Fi Protected Access was designed to fix critical vulnerabilities in the earlier wired equivalent privacy (WEP) standard. Like WEP, version 1 of WPA uses the RC4 symmetric cipher to encrypt traffic but adds a mechanism called the Temporal Key Integrity"},{"term":"Advanced Encryption Standard (AES)","def":"Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) is an encryption method that uses symmetric keys and block ciphers to encrypt data. This means that the data is divided into blocks of 128-bits and each block is encrypted independently."},{"term":"WPA2","def":"Neither WEP nor the original WPA version is considered secure enough for continued use."},{"term":"Wi-Fi Authentication Methods","def":"Wi-Fi authentication comes in three types: open, personal, and enterprise. Within the personal authentication category, there are two methods: WPA2 pre-shared key (PSK) authentication and WPA3 Simultaneous Authentication of Equals."},{"term":"WPA2 Pre-Shared Key Authentication","def":"In WPA2, pre-shared key authentication uses a passphrase to generate the key that is used to encrypt communications. It is also referred to as group authentication because a group of users shares the same passphrase. When the access point is set to WPA2-PSK mode, the"},{"term":"WPA3 Personal Authentication","def":"While WPA3 still uses passphrase-based group authentication of stations in personal mode, it changes the method by which this secret is used to agree session keys. In WPA3, the simultaneous authentication of equals (SAE) protocol replaces the PSK mechanism."},{"term":"Enterprise Authentication Protocols","def":"The main problems with personal modes of authentication are that the distribution of the passphrase cannot be secured properly and that the access point administrator may choose an insecure passphrase. Personal authentication also fails to provide accounting because all"},{"term":"Enterprise authentication uses the following general workflow:","def":"1. When a wireless station (a supplicant) requests an association, the AP enables the channel for EAPoW traffic only."},{"term":"RADIUS, TACACS+, and Kerberos","def":"Enterprise authentication uses an AAA server and network directory. These components can be implemented by several different protocols."},{"term":"RADIUS","def":"Remote Authentication Dial-in User Service is one way of implementing the AAA server when configuring enterprise authentication. The wireless access point is configured as a client of the RADIUS server. Rather than storing and validating user credentials directly, it forwards this"}],"19":[{"term":"Password Best Practices","def":"One of the first pillars of workstation security is ensuring that only authorized users can operate the computers connected to the network. Effective user security depends on strong credential management, effective account policies, and best practice end-user behavior."},{"term":"Password Rules","def":"The following rules are easy for users to apply and make passwords more difficult to crack: • Make the password sufficiently long—12+ character length is suitable for an ordinary user account. Administrative accounts should have longer passwords."},{"term":"Restrict User Permissions","def":"Account management policies are used to determine what rights and privileges each employee should be assigned. These policies should be guided by the principle of least privilege."},{"term":"Change Default Administrator Account and Password","def":"The root or superuser in Linux or the Administrator user account in Windows is the default system owner. These default accounts have no practical limitations and consequently are the ultimate target for threat actors. In many cases, these default accounts are disabled during the"},{"term":"Disable Guest Account","def":"A guest account allows unauthenticated access to the computer and may provide some sort of network access too. In current versions of Windows, the Guest account is disabled by default and cannot be used to sign-in. It is only enabled to facilitate password-less file sharing in a"},{"term":"Account Policies","def":"Account policies supplement best practice behavior by enforcing requirements as controls imposed by the OS. On a standalone workstation, password and account policies can be configured via the Local Security Policy snap-in ( secpol.msc) or the Group Policy Editor"},{"term":"Unused Services","def":"Services are used by the operating system and hardware to increase the functionality of the computer. Some services you may be familiar with may include update services, file and print services, and even network services like DHCP and DNS."},{"term":"Execution Control","def":"Authentication and authorization policies give subjects the right to sign on to a computer and network and (potentially) to make changes to the system configuration. This places a certain amount of trust in the user to exercise those rights responsibly. Users can act maliciously,"},{"term":"Trusted/Untrusted Software Sources","def":"To prevent the spread of malware such as Trojans, it is necessary to restrict the ability of users to run unapproved program code, especially code that can modify the OS, such as an application installer. Windows uses the system of Administrator and Standard user accounts,"},{"term":"AutoRun and AutoPlay","def":"One of the problems with legacy versions of Windows is that when an optical disc is inserted or a USB drive is attached, Windows would automatically run commands defined in an autorun.inf file stored in the root of the drive. A typical autorun.inf would define an icon for a disk and"},{"term":"Windows Defender Antivirus","def":"Even with UAC and execution control, there are still plenty of ways for malware to install onto a PC. A program might use particularly effective social engineering techniques to persuade the user to bypass the normal checks. The malware might exploit a vulnerability to execute without"},{"term":"Windows Defender Antivirus Updated Definitions","def":"It is particularly important that antivirus software be updated regularly. Two types of updates are generally necessary: • Definitions are information about new viruses or malware. These updates may be made available daily or even hourly."},{"term":"Activating and Deactivating Windows Defender Antivirus","def":"The nature of malware means that there should be no simple means of deactivating an antivirus product, or the malware could easily circumvent it. Defender Antivirus can be disabled temporarily by toggling the Real-time protection button. It will re-activate itself after a short"},{"term":"Windows Defender Firewall","def":"Where the antivirus product protects against threats in the file system, Windows Defender Firewall implements a personal/host firewall to filter inbound and outbound network traffic. The basic Settings app interface allows you to activate or deactivate the firewall for a given network"},{"term":"Encrypting File System","def":"When data is hosted on a file system, it can be protected by the operating system's security model. Each file or folder can be configured with an access control list (ACL), describing the permissions that principals have on the file. These permissions are enforced only when the OS"},{"term":"Windows BitLocker and BitLocker To Go","def":"An alternative to file encryption is to use a full disk encryption (FDE) product. The Windows BitLocker disk encryption product is available with all editions of Windows except for the Home edition."},{"term":"Browser Selection and Installation","def":"Microsoft’s Internet Explorer (IE) used to be dominant in the browser market, but alternatives such as Google's Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, and Opera have replaced it. IE itself is no longer supported. Edge, Microsoft’s replacement browser, now uses the same underlying Chromium"},{"term":"Trusted Sources","def":"As the browser is a security-critical type of software, it is particularly important to use a trusted source, such as an app store. If installed as a desktop application, care should be taken to The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 19: Managing Security Settings"},{"term":"Untrusted Sources","def":"Using a browser from an untrusted source where the publisher of the installer cannot be verified through a digital signature or hash is a security risk and likely to expose the user to unwanted adverts, search engines, and even spyware and redirection attacks. Some PC"},{"term":"Browser Settings","def":"Each browser maintains its own settings that are accessed via its Meatball (…) or Hamburger (⇨) menu button. Alternatively, you can open the internal URL, such as chrome://settings, edge://settings, or about:preferences (Firefox). The settings configure options such as"},{"term":"Sign-in and Browser Data Synchronization","def":"A browser sign-in allows the user to synchronize settings between instances of the browser software on different devices. As well as the browser settings, items that can be synced include bookmarks, history, saved autofill entries, and passwords. Settings and data for specific"},{"term":"Password Manager","def":"A typical user might be faced with having to remember dozens of sign-ins for different services and resort to using the same password for each. This is insecure because just one site breach could result in the compromise of all the user's digital identities. Each major browser now"},{"term":"Browser Extensions and Plug-ins","def":"A browser add-on is some type of code that adds to the basic functionality of the software."},{"term":"Add-ons come in several different types:","def":"• Browser extensions add or change a browser feature via its application programming interface (API). For example, an extension might install a toolbar or change menu options."},{"term":"Browser Patching","def":"Maintaining a system that is up to date helps to ensure it remains protected against new threats."},{"term":"Secure Connections and Valid Certificates","def":"The web uses Transport Layer Security (TLS) and digital certificate to implement a secure connection. A secure connection validates the identity of the host running a site and encrypts communications to protect against snooping. The identity of a web server computer for a given"},{"term":"Browser Privacy Settings","def":"The marketing value of online advertising has created an entire industry focused on creating profiles of individual search and browsing habits. The main function of privacy controls is to govern sites' use of these tracking tools, such as cookies. A cookie is a text file used to store"},{"term":"Malware Vectors","def":"Malware is usually simply defined as software that does something bad, from the perspective of the system owner. The more detailed classification of different malware types helps to identify the likely source and impact of a security incident. Some malware classifications focus on the"},{"term":"Malware Payloads","def":"Classifying malware by payload is a way of identifying what type of actions the code performs other than simply replicating or persisting on a host."},{"term":"Backdoors","def":"Modern malware is usually designed to implement some type of backdoor, also referred to as a remote access trojan. Once the malware is installed, it allows the threat actor to access the PC, upload/exfiltrate data files, and install additional malware tools. This could allow the attacker to"},{"term":"Adware","def":"Adware is a specialized malware used to display unwanted and unsolicited advertisements on your workstation or device. This can be accomplished by storing cookies in your browser or redirecting connection requests to an advertisement server. These Potentially Unwanted"},{"term":"Spyware and Keyloggers","def":"Spyware is malware that can perform browser reconfigurations, such as allowing tracking cookies, changing default search providers, opening arbitrary pages at startup, adding bookmarks, and so on. Spyware might also be able to monitor local application activity, take"},{"term":"Rootkits","def":"In Windows, malware can only be manually installed with local administrator privileges. This means the user must be confident enough in the installer package to enter the credentials or accept the User Account Control (UAC) prompt. Additionally, Windows tries to protect the"},{"term":"Ransomware and Cryptominers","def":"Ransomware is a type of malware that tries to extort money from the victim. One class of ransomware will display threatening messages, such as requiring Windows to be reactivated or suggesting that the computer has been locked by the police because it was used to view child"},{"term":"Troubleshoot PC Security Symptoms","def":"The multiple classifications for malware vectors and payloads mean that there can be very many different symptoms of security issues. In very general terms, any sort of activity or configuration change that was not initiated by the user is a good reason to suspect malware"}],"20":[{"term":"Screen lock","def":"A mobile security mechanism that activates when a device is idle or the power button is pressed."},{"term":"PIN","def":"A numeric credential that can be used for mobile screen-lock authentication."},{"term":"Fingerprint authentication","def":"A biometric method using a fingerprint sensor for device authentication."},{"term":"Facial recognition","def":"A biometric method using a 3-D image of the user’s face for authentication."},{"term":"Pattern lock","def":"A screen-lock method based on swiping a join-the-dots pattern."},{"term":"Mobile security software","def":"Software that can protect mobile devices against malware, phishing, and software exploits."},{"term":"Mobile OS patching","def":"Keeping the mobile operating system updated with patches and new OS versions."},{"term":"Mobile antivirus/anti-malware","def":"Security software that can filter content, block known phishing sites, and monitor app permissions and usage."},{"term":"Mobile firewall app","def":"An app that can monitor app activity and prevent connections to specific ports or IP addresses."},{"term":"BYOD","def":"A deployment model where employees use their own devices subject to company requirements and oversight."},{"term":"COBO","def":"A deployment model where the company owns the device and it is used solely for business purposes."},{"term":"COPE","def":"A deployment model where the company owns the device but employees can use it for personal activities under policy."},{"term":"CYOD","def":"A deployment model where employees choose from a list of approved devices."},{"term":"Mobile Device Management (MDM)","def":"Software designed to apply security policies to mobile devices in an enterprise."},{"term":"Configuration profiles","def":"MDM profiles that define and enforce security requirements and device settings."},{"term":"2FA","def":"Authentication requiring two different kinds of credentials."},{"term":"Mobile device encryption","def":"A security mechanism used to protect data on smartphones and tablets if a device is lost or stolen."},{"term":"Screen Locks","def":"If threat actors gain access to smartphones or tablets, they can retrieve vast amounts of information to facilitate further attacks. This includes confidential data, cached passwords for email, VPNs, and websites, as well as contacts and message histories (SMS, email, and IM),"},{"term":"Patching/OS Updates","def":"Keeping a mobile OS and its apps updated with patches and new OS versions is crucial, similar to desktop computers."},{"term":"Antivirus/Anti-malware Apps","def":"Modern smartphones are vulnerable to software exploits and malware, especially if apps are installed from untrusted sources. The evolving nature of mobile OS threats makes it challenging to maintain pattern databases or use heuristics effectively."},{"term":"Firewall Apps","def":"Firewall apps for mobile devices monitor app activity and prevent connections to specific ports or IP addresses. Firewalls need higher permission levels (root) to control other apps, making installation challenging. \"No-root\" firewalls create a local virtual private network (VPN) interface"},{"term":"Enterprise Mobility Management","def":"Mobile devices have become essential for email, daily management tasks, and accessing business processes and cloud applications. A mobile device deployment model includes the policies and procedures that define how employees receive mobile devices and applications:"},{"term":"Mobile Device Management","def":"Mobile device management (MDM) is a class of software designed to apply security policies to the use of mobile devices in the enterprise. This software can be used to manage enterprise- owned devices as well as bring your own device (BYOD) user-owned smartphones."},{"term":"Two-factor Authentication","def":"Most smartphones and tablets are single-user devices. Access control can be implemented by configuring a screen lock that can only be bypassed using the correct password, personal identification number (PIN), or swipe pattern. Many devices now support biometric"},{"term":"Mobile Data Security","def":"If a mobile device is lost or stolen, mechanisms exist to recover it and prevent misuse or data loss."},{"term":"Device Encryption","def":"All but the earliest versions of mobile device OSs for smartphones and tablets provide some type of default encryption."},{"term":"Remote Backup Applications","def":"Most mobile devices are linked to vendor cloud services (iCloud for iOS, Google Sync for Android, OneDrive for Microsoft) for automatic remote backup of data, apps, and settings."},{"term":"Locator Apps and Remote Wipe","def":"Most smartphones and many tablets are equipped with GPS receivers, which determine a device's position using satellite information. GPS requires line-of-sight and does not work indoors. High-accuracy location services use GPS along with Wi-Fi access points and Bluetooth"},{"term":"Mobile Device Troubleshooting Tools","def":"When troubleshooting mobile devices, the Settings app is frequently used. Its layout differs between iOS and Android and can vary with different versions."},{"term":"Reboot","def":"Rebooting a mobile device can often resolve temporary performance or stability issues. Unlike leaving the device in sleep mode, powering it off closes all applications and clears RAM without affecting stored data and settings. This soft reset is effective for restoring unresponsive or"},{"term":"Factory Reset","def":"A factory reset erases all user data, apps, and settings from a device. Afterward, the device must be manually set up with a new user account and apps or restored from a backup. Ensure the device is fully charged or connected to a power source before performing a reset."},{"term":"Battery Life Issues","def":"Battery life issues on mobile devices could come from a variety of factors. Background app activity can significantly drain the battery, so check which apps consume the most power and disable unnecessary background processes. Adjust screen brightness and timeout settings to"},{"term":"Troubleshoot Device and OS Issues","def":"If rebooting doesn't resolve the issue, follow these troubleshooting steps. If problems persist, consider a factory reset."},{"term":"OS Fails to Update","def":"An OS update failure can expose the device to security vulnerabilities. Follow these steps: 1. Verify update compatibility with your device model on the vendor's website."},{"term":"5. Check for sufficient free space:","def":"• On iOS: Go to Settings > General > Storage. • On Android: Go to Settings > Storage."}],"21":[{"term":"Backup Operations","def":"Data backup is a system maintenance task that enables you to store copies of critical data for safekeeping. Backup protects against loss of data due to disasters such as file corruption or hardware failure. Data recovery is a task that enables you to restore user access to lost or"},{"term":"Backup Methods","def":"When considering a file server or database server, the execution and frequency of backups must be carefully planned and guided by policies. Each backup job records data as it was at a certain point in time. As each backup job might take up a lot of space and there is"},{"term":"Two main factors govern backup operations:","def":"• Frequency is the period between backup jobs. The frequency configuration reflects how much lost work can be tolerated. For example, if employees can recall and input the previous day’s work on document files, a daily backup will meet the requirement. If the edits are much"},{"term":"Backup Chains","def":"The requirements for backup frequency and retention must be managed against the capacity of the backup media and the time it takes to complete a backup job. These requirements are managed by using different types of jobs in a backup chain. The main types of backups are full"},{"term":"Type Data Selection Backup Job Time Recovery Archive Attribute","def":"and Storage Complexity Requirement Full All selected data High Low (single job) Cleared regardless of when it was previously backed up Incremental New files and files Low High (multiple Cleared modified since last jobs) backup job Differential New files and files Moderate Moderate (two Not cleared"},{"term":"Requirement","def":"Full All selected data High Low (single job) Cleared regardless of when it was previously backed up Incremental New files and files Low High (multiple Cleared modified since last jobs) backup job Differential New files and files Moderate Moderate (two Not cleared"},{"term":"Synthetic Full Backup","def":"A synthetic full backup is similar to a Full backup, but instead of scanning the system again to create the full backup, the system will use the original full backup and then add in the data from the incremental backups to create a new full up-to-date backup."},{"term":"Backup Media Requirements","def":"A backup rotation scheme allows some media to be reused once the retention period of the job stored on it has expired. Rotation is most closely associated with the use of tape media but can be applied to disk devices too. There are many backup rotation schemes, but the most widely"},{"term":"Grandfather - Father - Son (GFS) Scheme","def":"The GFS scheme labels the backup tapes in generations. Son tapes store the most recent data and have the shortest retention period (one week, for example). Grandfather tapes are the oldest and have the longest retention period (one year, for example). Assuming a single"},{"term":"On Site versus Off Site Storage","def":"On site backup storage means that the production system and backup media are in the same location. This means that if a disaster strikes the facility, there is the risk of losing both the production and backup copies of the data."},{"term":"3-2-1 Backup Rule","def":"The 3-2-1 backup rule is a best-practice maxim that you can apply to your backup procedures to verify that you are implementing a solution that can mitigate the widest possible range of disaster scenarios. It states that you should have three copies of your data (including the"},{"term":"Backup Testing and Recovery Best Practices","def":"When you design a backup scheme, test it to make sure it's reliable. To test the backup: • Try restoring some of the backed-up data into a test directory, making sure you don't overwrite any data when doing so. Alternatively, use a virtual machine to test recovery"},{"term":"Recovery Options","def":"When performing a recovery, you can choose to either perform an in-place (overwrite) recovery or recover to an alternate location."},{"term":"Regulated Data Classification","def":"Regulated data is information that must be collected, processed, and stored in compliance with federal and/or state legislation. If a company processes regulated data collected from customers who reside in different countries, it must comply with the relevant legislation for each"},{"term":"Personally Identifiable Information","def":"Personally identifiable information (PII) is data that can be used to identify, contact, or locate an individual or, in the case of identity theft, to impersonate them. A cell phone number is a good example of PII. Others include name, date of birth, email address, street address, biometric"},{"term":"Personal Government-issued Information","def":"Personal government-issued information that is issued to individuals by federal or state governments is also PII. Examples include a social security number (SSN), passport, driving license, and birth/marriage certificates. Data collected and held by the US federal government"},{"term":"Healthcare Data","def":"Protected-health-information refers to medical and insurance records plus associated hospital and laboratory test results. Healthcare data may be associated with a specific person or used as an anonymized or de-identified data set for analysis and research, such as in clinical trials to"},{"term":"Credit Card Transactions","def":"There are also industry-enforced regulations mandating data security. A good example is the Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard (PCI DSS) which governs the processing of credit card transactions and other bank card payments. It sets out protections that must"},{"term":"Data Handling Best Practices","def":"Employees should be trained to identify PII and to handle personal or sensitive data appropriately. This means not making unauthorized copies or allowing the data to be seen or captured by any unauthorized persons. Examples of treating sensitive data carelessly include"},{"term":"Data Retention Requirements","def":"Another issue for regulated data is its retention on both file and database servers and in backup files: • Regulation might set a maximum period for the retention of data. For example, if a company collects a customer’s address and credit card information to fulfill an order and the customer"},{"term":"Prohibited Content and Licensing Issues","def":"As well as ensuring secure handling of confidential and sensitive data, you need to consider methods for identifying and removing prohibited content and unlicensed software from company workstations."},{"term":"Prohibited Content","def":"Employee workstations should only be used for work-related activity and data storage. In this context, prohibited content is any information that is not applicable to work. It can also specifically mean content that is obscene or illegally copied/pirated. The acceptable use"},{"term":"End-User License Agreements","def":"Prohibited content also extends to the unauthorized installation and use of software. When you install software, you must accept the license governing its use, often called the end-user license agreement. The terms of the license will vary according to the type of software, but the"},{"term":"License Compliance Monitoring","def":"Software is often activated using a product key, which will be a long string of characters and numbers. The product key will generate a different product ID, which is often used to obtain The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 21: Using Data Security"},{"term":"Open-source Licenses","def":"Software released under an open-source license generally makes it free to use, modify, and share and makes the program code used to design it available. The idea is that other programmers can investigate the program and make it more stable and useful. An open-source"},{"term":"Digital Rights Management","def":"Digital music and video are often subject to copy protection and digital rights management."},{"term":"Non-Disclosure Agreements","def":"Non-disclosure agreements (NDA) are agreements designed to protect sensitive information. A NDA is a legally binding contract that obligates all parties to protect sensitive information that is shared between them. An NDA agreement can be either unilateral or mutual:"},{"term":"Incident Response","def":"While performing technical support, you may have to report or respond to security incidents. A security incident could be one of a wide range of different scenarios, such as: • A computer or network infected with viruses, worms, or Trojans."},{"term":"Data Integrity and Preservation","def":"Forensics is the science of collecting evidence from computer systems to a standard that will be accepted in a court of law. Like DNA or fingerprints, digital evidence is mostly latent. Latent means that the evidence cannot be seen with the naked eye; rather, it must be interpreted using"},{"term":"Order of Volatility","def":"When recovering evidence, it is important to follow the order of volatility. This is the order that data should be collected based on how long it is likely to remain available. The most volatile data should be collected first and then go down the list to the data that is least likely to"},{"term":"A general order of volatility from most to least volatile is:","def":"1. CPU cache and registers - This data is extremely volatile and changes rapidly."},{"term":"Chain of Custody","def":"It is vital that the evidence collected at the crime scene conforms to a valid timeline. Digital information is susceptible to tampering, so access to the evidence must be tightly controlled."},{"term":"Data Destruction Methods","def":"Data destruction and disposal refer to either destroying or decommissioning data storage media, including hard disks, flash drives, tape media, and CDs/DVDs. The problem has become particularly prominent as organizations repurpose and recycle their old computers, either by"},{"term":"Erasing / Wiping","def":"Disk erasing/wiping software ensures that old data is destroyed by writing to each location on a hard disk drive, either using zeroes or in a random pattern. This leaves the disk in a \"clean\" state ready to be passed to the new owner. This overwriting method is suitable for all but the"},{"term":"Low Level Format","def":"Most disk vendors supply low-level formatting tools to reset a disk to its factory condition. Most of these tools will now incorporate some type of sanitize function. You must verify the specific capability of each disk model, but the following functions are typical:"}],"22":[{"term":"Change Requests","def":"Change management (CM) refers to policies and procedures that reduce the risk of configuration changes causing service downtime. Change management is closely related to configuration management."},{"term":"Risk Analysis","def":"Each change being requested should undergo a risk analysis. Risk analysis is a systematic approach to identify possible risks associated with implementing the change. Could the change cause a temporary outage of a critical system or service? Is there a risk of injury or death to the"},{"term":"Change Board Approvals","def":"When a change request has been drafted and submitted, it must go through an approval process."},{"term":"Implementation and Acceptance","def":"Implementation is when we have the approval to make a change and we have the authority to make the change. This process may require multiple steps to be followed to accomplish the change task."},{"term":"Asset Management","def":"While troubleshooting or implementing changes to a system or service, it will be important to ensure you are working on the correct system or service. Accurate tracking of what physical assets your company or organization has is just as important as fixing the issues with those"},{"term":"Warranty and Licensing","def":"Each asset record should include appropriate procurement documentation, such as the invoice and warranty/support contract (along with appropriate contact information). For software, it should record the licensing details with device/user allocations and limits."},{"term":"Compliance with Regulations","def":"When performing PC maintenance work, you may need to take into account compliance with government regulations. Regulations that typically affect PC maintenance or the installation of new equipment are: • Health and safety laws— Keeping the workplace free from hazards."},{"term":"Electrical Safety","def":"Electricity flows in a circuit. A circuit is made when conductors form a continuous path between the positive and negative terminals of a power source. An electrical circuit has the following properties: • Current is the amount of charge flowing through a conductor, measured in amps (A or I)."},{"term":"Safety Equipment and Tools","def":"While working with electronic equipment, it would be wise to utilize insulated tools and gloves to protect yourself from harm. You may also want to test the component with a voltage tester before attempting to work on it. Ensuring there is no voltage present can protect you from"},{"term":"Fuses","def":"An electrical device must be fitted with a fuse appropriate to its maximum current, such as 3A, 5A, or 13A. A fuse blows if there is a problem with the electrical supply, breaking the circuit to the power source. If the fuse fitted is rated too low, it will blow too easily; if the rating is too"},{"term":"Equipment Grounding","def":"Electrical equipment must be grounded. If there is a fault that causes metal parts in the equipment to become live in the circuit, the ground provides a path of least resistance for the electrical current to flow away harmlessly. Devices such as PCs and printers are connected to"},{"term":"Proper Power Handling and Personal Safety","def":"Whenever you add or replace components within a PC or laptop, the power must be disconnected first. Remove the AC plug and also remove the battery if present. Hold down the power button on the device to ensure the circuits are drained of any residual power from the"},{"term":"Electrical Fire Safety","def":"Faulty electrical equipment can pose a fire risk. If the equipment allows more current to flow through a cable than the cable is rated for, the cable will heat up. This could ignite flammable material close to the cable. If an electrical wire does start a fire, it is important to use the"},{"term":"Equipment Placement","def":"Equipment placement or location is just as important as the equipment itself. Ensuring equipment is located in a secure location near a power source and out of public access ensures that only authorized personnel can access it. Servers and other networking equipment should"},{"term":"Other Safety Hazard Mitigations","def":"In addition to electrical hazards, there are other safety hazards that computer technicians must account for."},{"term":"Trip Hazards","def":"A trip hazard is caused by putting any object in pathways where people walk."},{"term":"Lifting Techniques","def":"Lifting a heavy object in the wrong way can damage your back or cause muscle strains and ligament damage. You may also drop the object and injure yourself or damage the object. When you need to lift or carry items, be aware of the maximum safe lifting weight as well as any"},{"term":"Environmental Impacts","def":"The location in which computer equipment is placed can affect its proper operation and lifespan. All electronic equipment should be kept away from extremes of temperature and damp or dusty conditions."},{"term":"Dust Cleanup","def":"Dust is drawn into the computer via ventilation holes. Over time, the dust can form a thick layer over components, heat sinks, fan blades, peripheral connection ports, and ventilation slots, preventing effective heat dissipation. It can clog up peripherals such as keyboards and mice."},{"term":"Air filter mask","def":"Image © 123RF.com Note: Do not use compressed air blasters to clean up a toner spill or a laser printer within an office-type area. You will blow fine toner dust into the atmosphere and create a health hazard."},{"term":"Temperature, Humidity, and Ventilation Control","def":"A computer that is too hot is likely to be unreliable. A computer must be ventilated so that its fans can draw relatively cool air across the motherboard and expel the warmed air from the rear vents. You must ensure that the room (ambient) temperature is not too high and that there is"},{"term":"Electrostatic Discharge Mitigation","def":"Static electricity is a high voltage, low current charge stored in an insulated body. Electrostatic discharge occurs when a path allows electrons to rush from a statically charged body to a component that has no charge. This can occur through touch or even over a small gap if the"},{"term":"Proper Component Handling","def":"Proper component handling tools and techniques protect electronic components against ESD when you service a PC or mobile device: • If possible, work in an uncarpeted area. Ideally, use an ESD-safe floor or chair mat."},{"term":"Electrostatic Discharge (ESD) wrist strap on ESD mat","def":"The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 22: Implementing Operational Procedures Image by Audrius Merfeldas ©123RF.com."},{"term":"An example of an electrostatic discharge (ESD) workstation","def":"The Official CompTIA A+ Core 1 and Core 2 Student Guide Module 22: Implementing Operational Procedures Image by Audrius Merfeldas ©123RF.com."},{"term":"Building Power Issues and Mitigations","def":"Faults in building power supply cause power problems such as surges, brownouts, and blackouts: • Surges— A surge is a brief increase in voltage, while a spike is an intense surge. A surge or spike can be caused by machinery and other high-power devices being turned on or off and"},{"term":"Surge Suppressors","def":"Passive protection devices can be used to filter out the effects of surges and spikes. The simplest surge suppressor devices come in the form of adapters, trailing sockets, or filter plugs, with the protection circuitry built into the unit. These devices offer low-cost protection to"},{"term":"Battery Backups","def":"Sudden power loss is likely to cause file corruption. If there is loss of power due to a brownout or blackout, system operation can be sustained for a few minutes by using battery backup."},{"term":"Example of a UPS","def":"Image by magraphics© 123RF.com. The key characteristics of a UPS are volt-amperes (VA) rating and runtime: • VA rating is the maximum load the UPS can sustain. To work out the minimum VA, sum the wattage of all the devices that will be attached to the UPS and multiply by 1.67 to account for"},{"term":"Materials Handling and Responsible Disposal","def":"Some of the components and consumables used with computer and printer systems can be hazardous to health and to the environment. You must comply with all relevant regulations when handling and disposing of these substances."},{"term":"Material Safety Data Sheets","def":"Employers are obliged to assess the risk to their workforce from hazardous substances at work and to take steps to eliminate or control that risk. No work with hazardous substances should take place unless an assessment has been made. Employees are within their rights to refuse to"},{"term":"Proper Disposal","def":"Even with procedures in place to properly maintain IT equipment, eventually, it will need to be decommissioned and either disposed of or recycled. IT equipment contains numerous components and materials that can cause environmental damage if they are disposed of"},{"term":"Special care must be taken with respect to the following device types:","def":"• Battery disposal— Swollen or leaking batteries from laptop computers or cell phones and tablets must be handled very carefully and stored within appropriate containers. Use gloves and safety goggles to minimize any risk of burns from corrosive material. Batteries must be"},{"term":"4.8 Identify the basics of scripting","def":"Learning Outcomes As you study this lesson, answer the following questions: • What is a script?"},{"term":"An example of a Linux shell script open in the Vim text editor","def":"Remember that in Linux, the script file must have the execute permission set to run. Execute can be set as a permission for the user, group, or world (everyone). If a PATH variable to the script has not been configured, execute it from the working directory by preceding the filename"}]}};

const pbqScenarios = [{"title":"System File Check","desc":"A Windows system is suspected of having corrupted protected system files.","objective":"Enter the command used to scan protected Windows system files and automatically repair corrupted files.","hint":"Use the System File Checker command from the Windows command-line material.","expected":["sfc /scannow"],"success":"Correct. The System File Checker scan command is accepted.","failure":"Not correct. This scenario requires the SFC scan command used to check protected system files."},{"title":"DHCP Lease Renewal","desc":"A Windows client needs to request a new DHCP lease.","objective":"Enter the Windows command used to renew the DHCP lease.","hint":"Use ipconfig with the lease-renewal switch.","expected":["ipconfig /renew"],"success":"Correct. The DHCP lease renewal command is accepted.","failure":"Not correct. This scenario requires the ipconfig lease-renewal command."},{"title":"DNS Cache Flush","desc":"A Windows client has a stale local DNS resolver cache.","objective":"Enter the command used to clear the local DNS resolver cache.","hint":"Use ipconfig with the DNS-cache flush switch.","expected":["ipconfig /flushdns"],"success":"Correct. The local DNS resolver cache can now be cleared.","failure":"Not correct. This scenario requires the ipconfig DNS-cache flush command."},{"title":"Remote Desktop Port","desc":"A technician needs the default TCP port used by Remote Desktop Protocol.","objective":"Enter the RDP port number.","hint":"Find RDP in the Core 2 ports/protocols material.","expected":["3389"],"success":"Correct. TCP 3389 is the RDP port listed in the Core 2 material.","failure":"Not correct. The required value is the RDP port from the Core 2 material."},{"title":"WPA3 / SAE","desc":"A SOHO wireless network needs the WPA3 authentication method covered in Core 2.","objective":"Enter the wireless security standard or authentication term associated with WPA3.","hint":"Use WPA3 or SAE.","expected":["wpa3","wpa3-sae","sae"],"success":"Correct. WPA3 / SAE is accepted.","failure":"Not correct. Use WPA3 or SAE for this scenario."},{"title":"PowerShell Script","desc":"A technician needs to identify the file extension for a PowerShell script.","objective":"Enter the PowerShell script extension.","hint":"Look at the scripting/file-type material.","expected":[".ps1"],"success":"Correct. .ps1 is the PowerShell script extension.","failure":"Not correct. The PowerShell script extension is required."},{"title":"Backup Type","desc":"A backup plan needs a backup that stores changes since the last full backup.","objective":"Enter the backup type that matches the description.","hint":"Compare full, incremental, and differential backups.","expected":["differential"],"success":"Correct. Differential backup matches the description.","failure":"Not correct. The required backup type stores changes since the last full backup."},{"title":"Change Review","desc":"A proposed IT change needs formal review before implementation.","objective":"Enter the abbreviation for the change board used in change management.","hint":"See the change-management material.","expected":["cab","change advisory board"],"success":"Correct. CAB / Change Advisory Board is accepted.","failure":"Not correct. Enter CAB or Change Advisory Board."}];
